        userTime: "00:54",
        avgTime: "00:34",
        question: "12 men complete a work in 9 days. After they have worked for 6 days, 6 more men join them. How many days will they take to complete the remaining work?",
        options: {
          a: "2 days",
          b: "3 days",
          c: "4 days",
          d: "5 days"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nRemaining days of work for the initial 12 men = 9 - 6 = 3 days.\nRemaining work in terms of man-days = 12 men \xD7 3 days = 36 man-days.\nNew number of men working = 12 + 6 = 18 men.\nDays required to complete the remaining work = 36 \xF7 18 = 2 days.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2 days.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nInitial number of men = 12\nTotal days required = 9\nDays they worked before others joined = 6\nNew men joined = 6\n\nFormula Used:\nTotal Work = Number of Men \xD7 Days\n\nPerson	Days	Total Work	Efficiency\n12 Men	9	108	12\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the efficiency of 1 man be 1 unit/day.\n\u21D2 Total work to be done = 108 units\n\u21D2 Work completed by 12 men in 6 days = 12 \xD7 6 = 72 units\n\u21D2 Remaining work = 108 - 72 = 36 units\n\u21D2 Total men present after 6 days = 12 + 6 = 18 men\n\u21D2 Combined efficiency of 18 men = 18 units/day\n\u21D2 Time taken to complete the remaining work = Remaining Work \xF7 Combined Efficiency\n\u21D2 Time taken = 36 \xF7 18 = 2 days\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nMDH Rule (Chain Rule)\n\nIf M1 men can do W1 work in D1 days working H1 hours/day, and M2 men can do W2 work in D2 days working H2 hours/day, then the relation is given by: (M1 \xD7 D1 \xD7 H1) \xF7 W1 = (M2 \xD7 D2 \xD7 H2) \xF7 W2.\n\nWork and Men Proportionality\n\nThe number of men required is inversely proportional to the number of days needed to complete the same amount of work. More men take fewer days, and fewer men take more days to complete identical work.\n\nWork & Wages\n\nWages are always distributed in the ratio of the total work done by each person. If all individuals work for the same number of days, their wages are distributed directly in the ratio of their daily efficiencies.",
