        status: "Correct (Slow)",
        chosenOption: "A",
        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:21",
        avgTime: "00:13",
        question: "In what ratio should rice costing Rs. 67.5 per kg be mixed with rice costing Rs. 60 per kg to get an average price of Rs. 64 per kg?",
        options: {
          a: "8 \u2236 7",
          b: "5 \u2236 6",
          c: "6 \u2236 5",
          d: "7 \u2236 8"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nBy using the direct alligation cross-multiplication rule:\n\nQuantity Ratio = (Mean Price - Cheaper Price) : (Dearer Price - Mean Price)\n\u21D2 Ratio = (64 - 60) : (67.5 - 64)\n\u21D2 Ratio = 4 : 3.5\n\u21D2 Ratio = 8 : 7\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8 : 7.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPrice of first rice type = \u20B967.5 per kg\nPrice of second rice type = \u20B960 per kg\nAverage mean price = \u20B964 per kg\n\nFormula Used:\nIf two types of items of price p1 and p2 are mixed to get an average mean price pm, the ratio of their quantities q1 : q2 is (pm - p2) : (p1 - pm).\n\nCalculations:\nLet the required ratio be x : y.\n\u21D2 Quantity ratio = (Mean Price - Price of Cheaper) : (Price of Dearer - Mean Price)\n\u21D2 x : y = (64 - 60) : (67.5 - 64)\n\u21D2 x : y = 4 : 3.5\n\nMultiply both sides by 2 to convert into integer ratio:\n\n\u21D2 x : y = 8 : 7\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8 : 7.\n\nAdditional Information\nAlligation Rule\n\nIt is a quick method to find the ratio in which two or more ingredients at given prices must be mixed to produce a mixture of a desired median price.\n\nWeighted Average Formula\n\nAverage Price = (q1 \xD7 p1 + q2 \xD7 p2) \xF7 (q1 + q2)\n\nReplacement Method\n\nIf from a vessel containing volume V of pure liquid, volume x is removed and replaced n times, then Final Volume = V \xD7 (1 - x \xF7 V)n.",
