        status: "Correct (Slow)",
        chosenOption: "C",
        correctOption: "C",
        userTime: "01:03",
        avgTime: "00:44",
        question: "In a division sum, the divisor is 13 times the quotient and 6 times the remainder. If the remainder is 39, then the dividend is:",
        options: {
          a: "4,800",
          b: "4,576",
          c: "4,251",
          d: "4,240"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nGiven Remainder = 39\nDivisor = 6 \xD7 39 = 234\nQuotient = 234 \xF7 13 = 18\nDividend = (Divisor \xD7 Quotient) + Remainder\nDividend = (234 \xD7 18) + 39 = 4212 + 39 = 4251\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4251.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nRemainder\n(R) = 39\nDivisor (D) = 13 \xD7 Quotient\n(Q)\nDivisor (D) = 6 \xD7 Remainder\n(R)\n\nFormula Used:\nDividend = (Divisor \xD7 Quotient) + Remainder\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Divisor (D) = 6 \xD7 39\n\u21D2 D = 234\n\u21D2 234 = 13 \xD7 Q\n\u21D2 Q = 234 \xF7 13\n\u21D2 Q = 18\n\u21D2 Dividend = (234 \xD7 18) + 39\n\u21D2 Dividend = 4212 + 39\n\u21D2 Dividend = 4251\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4251.\n\nAdditional Information\nDivision Algorithm\n\nThe fundamental relation in division is: Dividend = (Divisor \xD7 Quotient) + Remainder, where the remainder is always strictly less than the divisor.\n\nDivisibility Rule for 13\n\nTo quickly check if a number is divisible by 13, multiply the last digit by 4 and add it to the remaining truncated number. If the new result is divisible by 13, the original number is also divisible.\n\nRemainder Theorem\n\nIn polynomial algebra, when a polynomial P(x) is divided by a linear polynomial (x - a), the remainder obtained is equal to the value of P(a).",
