        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:23",
        avgTime: "00:14",
        question: "A group of 25 tourists consume 5 containers of food in 8 days in a hotel. How many containers will be required for 10 tourists to eat for 16 days?",
        options: {
          a: "4",
          b: "3",
          c: "6",
          d: "5"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nUsing the Chain Rule (MDH) formula: M1 \xD7 D1 / W1 = M2 \xD7 D2 / W2\nWhere M = tourists, D = days, W = containers of food.\n\u21D2 (25 \xD7 8) / 5 = (10 \xD7 16) / W2\n\u21D2 40 = 160 / W2 \u21D2 W2 = 4\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 containers.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nGroup 1: M1 = 25 tourists, D1 = 8 days, W1 = 5 containers\nGroup 2: M2 = 10 tourists, D2 = 16 days, W2 = ?\n\nFormula Used:\nM1 \xD7 D1 / W1 = M2 \xD7 D2 / W2\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 (25 \xD7 8) / 5 = (10 \xD7 16) / W2\n\u21D2 200 / 5 = 160 / W2\n\u21D2 40 = 160 / W2\n\u21D2 W2 = 160 / 40\n\u21D2 W2 = 4\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 containers.\n\nAdditional Information\nChain Rule (MDH Formula)\n\nThe foundational formula for equivalence is M1 \xD7 D1 \xD7 H1 / W1 = M2 \xD7 D2 \xD7 H2 / W2, where M is the number of people, D is the number of days, H is the number of hours, and W is the total work done or resource consumed.\n\nDirect Proportion\n\nTwo quantities are directly proportional if they increase or decrease together at the same rate. For example, keeping the days constant, more tourists will predictably consume a proportionally higher amount of food.\n\nInverse Proportion\n\nTwo quantities are inversely proportional if an increase in one factor automatically causes a decrease in the other. For instance, given a fixed amount of food, increasing the number of tourists reduces the number of days that food will last.",
        topic: "Time & Work",
