        userTime: "00:47",
        avgTime: "00:36",
        question: "Mukesh sells almonds at the cost price but uses a false weight and thus gains a 23% profit. How many grams of almonds is he giving for 3.075 kg?",
        options: {
          a: "2500",
          b: "2700",
          c: "2400",
          d: "2600"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nSince the shopkeeper sells at cost price, the profit of 23% is entirely due to false weight.\n\nRatio of Claimed Weight (SP) to Actual Weight (CP) = (100 + 23) : 100 = 123 : 100\nHere, Claimed Weight = 3.075 kg = 3075 g\n\n123 units \u2192 3075 g\n\n1 unit \u2192 25 g\n\nActual Weight (100 units) = 25 \xD7 100 = 2500 g\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2500 g.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nClaimed Weight = 3.075 kg = 3075 g\nProfit = 23%\n\nFormula Used:\nProfit % = [(Claimed Weight - Actual Weight) \xF7 Actual Weight] \xD7 100\n\nCalculations:\nLet the actual weight given by Mukesh be x grams.\n\u21D2 23 = [(3075 - x) \xF7 x] \xD7 100\n\u21D2 23x = 307500 - 100x\n\u21D2 123x = 307500\n\u21D2 x = 307500 \xF7 123\n\u21D2 x = 2500\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2500 g.\n\nAdditional Information\nDishonest Dealer Concept\n\nWhen a dealer sells goods at Cost Price (CP) but uses a false weight, his Profit % is determined directly by the error in weight. Formula: Profit % = [Error \xF7 (Claimed Weight - Error)] \xD7 100.\n\nNet Profit with Markup\n\nIf the dealer marks up his goods by a certain percentage and simultaneously cheats by using a false weight, the net profit is calculated by combining both effects using the ratio method or successive percentage formula.\n\nSelling Price and Markup\nSelling Price (SP) = Marked Price (MP) \xD7 [(100 - Discount%) \xF7 100]. This relationship helps identify the final price when a dealer offers a discount while committing weight fraud.",
