        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIf the worker travelled the entire 11 hours on foot, the distance covered = 11 \xD7 4.5 = 49.5 km.\n\nExtra distance remaining to be covered = 81 - 49.5 = 31.5 km.\nDifference in speeds between the cycle and foot = 15 - 4.5 = 10.5 km/h.\nTime spent travelling on the cycle = 31.5 \xF7 10.5 = 3 hours.\nDistance covered on the cycle = 3 \xD7 15 = 45 km.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 45 km.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTotal distance = 81 km\nTotal time = 11 hours\nSpeed on foot = 4.5 km/h\nSpeed on cycle = 15 km/h\n\nFormula Used:\nDistance = Speed \xD7 Time\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the time travelled on foot be t hours.\n\u21D2 Time travelled on the cycle = (11 - t) hours.\n\u21D2 Distance covered on foot + Distance covered on cycle = Total distance\n\u21D2 (4.5 \xD7 t) + 15 \xD7 (11 - t) = 81\n\u21D2 4.5t + 165 - 15t = 81\n\u21D2 10.5t = 165 - 81\n\u21D2 10.5t = 84\n\u21D2 t = 84 \xF7 10.5 = 8 hours\n\u21D2 Time travelled on cycle = 11 - 8 = 3 hours.\n\u21D2 Distance covered on cycle = Speed \xD7 Time = 15 \xD7 3 = 45 km.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 45 km.\n\nAdditional Information\nAverage Speed\n\nAverage Speed = Total Distance \xF7 Total Time. It represents the constant speed required to cover the whole distance in the given timeframe without any variations.\n\nAlligation Method in Speed\n\nWhen applying the cross-alligation method directly on two different speeds to obtain an average speed, the resulting ratio strictly represents the ratio of time taken, not the distance.\n\nShift / Minimum Value Trick\n\nWhen a total value is comprised of two parts, assuming the entire operation happens at the minimum rate allows finding the specific remainder. Dividing this remainder by the unit rate difference reveals the proportion of the higher rate component.",
