        userTime: "01:41",
        avgTime: "00:25",
        question: "If the radius of a sphere is increased by 3 cm, then its surface area increases by 156\u03C0 cm^2. The radius of the sphere before the increase was:",
        options: {
          a: "7 cm",
          b: "8 cm",
          c: "6 cm",
          d: "5 cm"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIncrease in surface area = 4\u03C0[(r + 3)\xB2 - r2] = 156\u03C0\n\u21D2 (r + 3)\xB2 - r2 = 39\n\u21D2 r2 + 6r + 9 - r2 = 39\n\u21D2 6r = 30 \u21D2 r = 5 cm\n\u2234 The correct answer is 5 cm.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nIncrease in radius = 3 cm\nIncrease in surface area = 156\u03C0 cm2\n\nFormula Used:\nSurface area of a sphere = 4\u03C0r2\n\nCalculations:\nLet the original radius of the sphere be r cm.\n\u21D2 Original surface area = 4\u03C0r2\n\u21D2 New radius = (r + 3) cm\n\u21D2 New surface area = 4\u03C0(r + 3)\xB2\n\nAccording to the given condition:\n\n\u21D2 4\u03C0(r + 3)\xB2 - 4\u03C0r2 = 156\u03C0\n\nDividing the entire equation by 4\u03C0:\n\n\u21D2 (r + 3)\xB2 - r2 = 39\n\u21D2 (r2 + 6r + 9) - r2 = 39\n\u21D2 6r + 9 = 39\n\u21D2 6r = 30\n\u21D2 r = 5 cm\n\u2234 The correct answer is 5 cm.\n\nAdditional Information\nVolume of a Sphere\n\nThe volume of a sphere is given by V = (4\xF73)\u03C0r3, where r is the radius.\n\nHemisphere Surface Area\n\nThe curved surface area of a solid hemisphere is 2\u03C0r2, and its total surface area is 3\u03C0r2.\n\nVolume of a Hemisphere\n\nThe volume of a hemisphere is exactly half that of a full sphere, given by V = (2\xF73)\u03C0r3.",
