        userTime: "01:09",
        avgTime: "00:32",
        question: "If the denominator of a fraction is multiplied by 2 and the numerator is increased by 2, the fraction becomes 1/2. If instead, the numerator is multiplied by 2 and the denominator is increased by 2, it becomes 6/7. What is the sum of the numerator and the denominator of the original fraction (in lowest form)?",
        options: {
          a: "11",
          b: "8",
          c: "7",
          d: "6"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nGiven:\nCondition 1: Numerator increased by 2 and denominator multiplied by 2 = 1/2\n\nCondition 2: Numerator multiplied by 2 and denominator increased by 2 = 6/7\n\nFormula used:\nOriginal Fraction = x/y\n\nSum = x + y\n\nCalculation:\n\u21D2 (x + 2) / 2y = 1/2\n\u21D2 2 \xD7 (x + 2) = 2y\n\u21D2 y = x + 2\n\u21D2 2x / (y + 2) = 6/7\n\u21D2 14x = 6 \xD7 (y + 2)\n\u21D2 7x = 3y + 6\n\u21D2 7x = 3 \xD7 (x + 2) + 6\n\u21D2 7x = 3x + 6 + 6\n\u21D2 4x = 12\n\u21D2 x = 3\n\u21D2 y = 3 + 2 = 5\n\u21D2 Original Fraction = 3/5\n\n\u21D2 Sum = 3 + 5 = 8\n\u2234 The correct answer is option (2).",
        topic: "Simplification",
        subtopic: "Linear Equations with Fractions",
