        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTotal capacity = LCM of 2 and 4 = 4 units.\nEfficiency: Inlet = +2 units/hr, Outlet = -1 unit/hr.\nVolume filled in the 1st hour (both open) = 2 - 1 = +1 unit.\nRemaining capacity to be filled = 4 - 1 = 3 units.\nTotal time = 1 hr (initial) + (3 \xF7 2) hr (remaining filled by inlet) = 2.5 hours.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2 hours 30 minutes.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nAn inlet pipe fills the cistern in 2 hours.\n\nAn outlet pipe empties the cistern in 4 hours.\n\nBoth pipes are opened together for the first 1 hour.\n\nCalculations:\nLet Total Capacity be the LCM of given times (2 and 4).\n\nPipe	Hours	Total Capacity	Efficiency\nInlet Pipe	2	4	4 \xF7 2 = 2\nOutlet Pipe	4	4 \xF7 4 = -1\n\u21D2 Combined efficiency of both pipes when open = 2 - 1 = 1 unit/hr.\n\u21D2 Part of the cistern filled in the 1st hour = 1 \xD7 1 = 1 unit.\n\u21D2 Remaining capacity to be filled = 4 - 1 = 3 units.\n\u21D2 After 1 hour, the outlet pipe is closed, meaning only the inlet pipe operates with an efficiency of 2 units/hr.\n\u21D2 Time taken by the inlet pipe to fill the remaining cistern = 3 \xF7 2 = 1.5 hours.\n\u21D2 1.5 hours is equal to 1 hour 30 minutes.\n\u21D2 Total time taken = 1 hour (initial time) + 1 hour 30 minutes (later time) = 2 hours 30 minutes.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2 hours 30 minutes.\n\nAdditional Information\nPipes and Cisterns Basics\n\nAn inlet pipe fills a tank, so its work is taken as positive. An outlet pipe (or leak) empties it, so its work is taken as negative.\n\nNet Part Filled in 1 Hour\n\nIf an inlet pipe fills a tank in x hours and an outlet pipe empties it in y hours (where y > x), the net part filled in 1 hour is (1/x) - (1/y).\n\nTime and Work Equivalence\n\nPipes and cisterns problems follow the exact same rules as standard Time & Work problems. Total capacity acts as total work, and the rate of filling/emptying acts as efficiency.",
