        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nGiven: $x + \\frac{1}{x} = y + \\frac{1}{y}$\n\u21D2 $(x - y) + \\left(\\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{y}\\right) = 0$\n\u21D2 $(x - y) + \\frac{y - x}{xy} = 0$\n\u21D2 $(x - y) - \\frac{x - y}{xy} = 0$\n\u21D2 $(x - y)\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{xy}\\right) = 0$\n\nThis gives two solutions:\nCase 1: $x - y = 0 \\implies x = y$\nCase 2: $1 - \\frac{1}{xy} = 0 \\implies xy = 1 \\implies x = \\frac{1}{y}$\n\nTherefore, $x = y$ or $x = \\frac{1}{y}$ (i.e. $y, \\frac{1}{y}$).\n\u2234 The correct answer is $y, \\frac{1}{y}$.\n\nAlternate Method\nRearrange as a quadratic in $x$:\n\u21D2 $x^2 + 1 = x\\left(y + \\frac{1}{y}\\right)$\n\u21D2 $x^2 - \\left(y + \\frac{1}{y}\\right)x + 1 = 0$\n\nFactoring the quadratic:\n\u21D2 $(x - y)\\left(x - \\frac{1}{y}\\right) = 0$\n\u21D2 $x = y$ or $x = \\frac{1}{y}$\n\u2234 The values of $x$ are $y, \\frac{1}{y}$.\n\nAdditional Information\nSymmetry in Equations\nEquations of the form $f(x) = f(y)$ where $f(t) = t + 1/t$ naturally have symmetry: $x = y$ is always a trivial root, while the reciprocal involution gives the second root $x = 1/y$.",
