        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nDifference (2 years) = P \xD7 (R / 100)\xB2\n\u21D2 160 = 25000 \xD7 (R / 100)\xB2\n\u21D2 (R / 100)\xB2 = 160 \xF7 25000 = 16 / 2500 = (4 / 50)\xB2\n\u21D2 R = (4 \xD7 100) \xF7 50 = 8\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPrincipal (P) = \u20B925,000\nTime (T) = 2 years\nDifference between CI and SI (D) = \u20B9160\n\nFormula Used:\nFor 2 years, D = P \xD7 (R / 100)\xB2\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 160 = 25000 \xD7 (R / 100)\xB2\n\u21D2 160 = 25000 \xD7 (R2 / 10000)\n\u21D2 160 = 2.5 \xD7 R2\n\u21D2 R2 = 160 \xF7 2.5\n\u21D2 R2 = 1600 \xF7 25\n\u21D2 R2 = 64\n\u21D2 R = \u221A64 = 8%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8%.\n\nAdditional Information\nDifference between CI and SI for 3 years\n\nFor 3 years, the difference between CI and SI is calculated as: D = P \xD7 (R / 100)\xB2 \xD7 ((300 + R) / 100).\n\nEffective Compound Interest Rate\n\nThe effective compound interest rate for 2 years is calculated using the successive percentage formula: Rate = 2R + (R2 / 100)%.\n\nRule of 72\n\nTo quickly estimate the number of years required to double an investment at a given annual compound interest rate, use the formula: Time \u2248 72 \xF7 R.",
