        userTime: "00:41",
        avgTime: "00:29",
        question: "There are two sections A and B of a class, consisting of 48 and 60 students respectively. If the average weight of section A is 51kg and that of section B is 42 kg, find the average weight of the whole class.",
        options: {
          a: "46 kg",
          b: "52 kg",
          c: "54 kg",
          d: "42 kg"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nRatio of students in sections A and B = 48 : 60 = 4 : 5.\nLet the assumed average be 42 kg (the lower average).\nDeviation of section A from assumed average = 51 - 42 = +9 kg.\nTotal deviation distributed over total parts = (4 \xD7 +9) \xF7 (4 + 5) = 36 \xF7 9 = +4 kg.\nCombined average = 42 + 4 = 46 kg.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 46 kg.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nNumber of students in section A (n1) = 48, Average weight (A1) = 51 kg\nNumber of students in section B (n2) = 60, Average weight (A2) = 42 kg\n\nFormula Used:\nCombined Average = (n1 \xD7 A1 + n2 \xD7 A2) \xF7 (n1 + n2)\u20751 kg\nSection A\n42 kg\nSection B\nx\nMean Avg\nx - 42\n51 - x\nStudent Ratio = 48 : 60 = 4 : 5\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Combined Average = (48 \xD7 51 + 60 \xD7 42) \xF7 (48 + 60)\n\u21D2 Combined Average = (2448 + 2520) \xF7 108\n\u21D2 Combined Average = 4968 \xF7 108 = 46 kg\n\nUsing Alligation (from diagram):\n\n\u21D2 Ratio of students = (x - 42) \xF7 (51 - x) = 48 \xF7 60\n\u21D2 (x - 42) \xF7 (51 - x) = 4 \xF7 5\n\u21D2 5 \xD7 (x - 42) = 4 \xD7 (51 - x)\n\u21D2 5x - 210 = 204 - 4x\n\u21D2 9x = 414 \u21D2 x = 46 kg\n\u2234 The correct answer is 46 kg.\n\nAdditional Information\nCombined Average Formula\n\nWhen two groups of sizes n1 and n2 have averages A1 and A2 respectively, their combined average is given by: A = (n1A1 + n2A2) \xF7 (n1 + n2).\n\nAlligation Rule\n\nIt is a quick method used to find the ratio in which two groups are mixed. The ratio of the quantities is given by the cross-difference of their values from the mean average.\n\nNet Deviation Method\n\nA trick to minimize calculation by assuming an arbitrary average. The actual average is the assumed average plus the total net deviation divided by the total number of observations.",
        topic: "Average",
