        userTime: "00:51",
        avgTime: "00:25",
        question: "John sold a watch to Sue at a gain of 20%, and Sue sold it to Rima at a profit of 10%. If Rima paid Rs. 2,178 for it, how much did John pay (in Rs.) for the watch?",
        options: {
          a: "1,650",
          b: "1,478",
          c: "1,780",
          d: "1,200"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nUsing the multiplying factor method for successive profits:\n\n20% profit = +1/5 \u21D2 Multiplier = 6/5\n10% profit = +1/10 \u21D2 Multiplier = 11/10\nInitial Price \xD7 (6/5) \xD7 (11/10) = Final Price\nCP \xD7 66/50 = 2178 \u21D2 CP = 2178 \xD7 50/66 = 1650\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B91650.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nProfit% earned by John = 20%\nProfit% earned by Sue = 10%\nAmount paid by Rima = \u20B92178\n\nFormula Used:\nSelling Price (SP) = Cost Price (CP) \xD7 (100 + Profit%) \xF7 100\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the cost price for John be x.\n\u21D2 Selling price of John = Cost price of Sue = x \xD7 (100 + 20)/100 = 1.2x\n\u21D2 Selling price of Sue = Cost price of Rima = 1.2x \xD7 (100 + 10)/100 = 1.32x\n\u21D2 As Rima paid \u20B92178, we can equate the values:\n\u21D2 1.32x = 2178\n\u21D2 x = 2178 \xF7 1.32 = 217800 \xF7 132\n\u21D2 x = 1650\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B91650.\n\nAdditional Information\nSuccessive Profit/Loss Formula\n\nIf an article is sold successively at profits of p1% and p2%, the final price is calculated as CP \xD7 (1 + p1/100) \xD7 (1 + p2/100).\n\nNet Effective Profit%\n\nWhen two successive profits p1% and p2% are applied, the effective net profit is given by (p1 + p2 + p1p2/100)%. Here, 20 + 10 + (200/100) = 32% overall profit.\n\nMultiplier Method\n\nMultiplying factors streamline successive changes. A 20% gain gives a multiplier of 1.2, and a 10% gain gives 1.1. Combined multiplier = 1.2 \xD7 1.1 = 1.32.",
