        question: "A completes one-third of the work in 5 days. He is then assisted by B. and they together finish the remaining work in 6 days. B alone can complete the entire piece of work in _________.",
        options: {
          a: "22.5 days",
          b: "18.5 days",
          c: "18 days",
          d: "20.5 days"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTime to complete full work: A = 5 \xD7 3 = 15 days, (A + B) = 6 \xD7 (3/2) = 9 days.\nTotal work = LCM(15, 9) = 45 units.\nEfficiency: A = 45 \xF7 15 = 3 units/day, (A + B) = 45 \xF7 9 = 5 units/day.\nEfficiency of B = 5 - 3 = 2 units/day.\nTime taken by B alone = 45 \xF7 2 = 22.5 days.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 22.5 days.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\n\u21D2 A completes 1/3 of the work in 5 days.\n\u21D2 Remaining work = 1 - 1/3 = 2/3.\n\u21D2 A and B together complete the remaining 2/3 of the work in 6 days.\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Time taken by A alone for the whole work = 5 \xD7 3 = 15 days.\n\u21D2 Time taken by (A + B) together for the whole work = 6 \xD7 (3/2) = 9 days.\n\u21D2 Let the total work be the LCM of 15 and 9.\n\nPerson	Days	Total Work	Efficiency\nA	15	45	45 \xF7 15 = 3\nA + B	9	45 \xF7 9 = 5\n\u21D2 Efficiency of B = Efficiency of (A + B) - Efficiency of A\n\u21D2 Efficiency of B = 5 - 3 = 2 units/day.\n\u21D2 Time taken by B alone = Total Work \xF7 Efficiency of B\n\u21D2 Time taken by B alone = 45 \xF7 2 = 22.5 days.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 22.5 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nPipes and Cisterns\n\nIt operates on the same logic as Time and Work. Inlet pipes have positive efficiency (filling), and outlet pipes or leaks have negative efficiency (emptying).\n\nWork and Wages\n\nWages are always distributed in the exact ratio of the actual work completed by each person. If everyone works for the same number of days, the wages are divided in the ratio of their efficiencies.\n\nAlternate Day Work\n\nFind the total work done in a complete cycle of days (for example, 2 days if two people are alternating). Multiply the cycle to get as close to the total work as possible, and calculate the remaining work for the next turns manually.",
