        userTime: "00:37",
        avgTime: "00:27",
        question: "If a person takes as much time in running 20 meter as a car takes in covering 50 meter; the distance covered by the person during the time car covers 1 km is:",
        options: {
          a: "140 m",
          b: "100 m",
          c: "500 m",
          d: "400 m"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nWhen the time of travel is constant, the ratio of distances covered is equal to the ratio of their speeds.\n\nRatio of distance covered (Person : Car) = 20 : 50 = 2 : 5\nDistance covered by the car = 1 km = 1000 m\nDistance covered by the person = 1000 \xD7 (2 \xF7 5)\nDistance covered by person = 400 m\n\u2234 The correct answer is 400 m.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nDistance covered by person = 20 m\nDistance covered by car = 50 m in the same time duration.\nTotal distance to be covered by car = 1 km = 1000 m\n\nFormula Used:\nSpeed = Distance \xF7 Time\nDistance = Speed \xD7 Time\n\nCar Distance\n0 m\n1000 m\nPerson Distance\n0 m\n? (400 m)\nRatio of distances in equal time = 50 : 20 = 5 : 2\nPerson Distance = 1000 \xD7 (2 \xF7 5) = 400 m\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the common time taken for the initial distances be t.\n\u21D2 Speed of person = 20 \xF7 t\n\u21D2 Speed of car = 50 \xF7 t\n\u21D2 Time taken by the car to cover 1000 m = Distance \xF7 Speed\n\u21D2 Time taken by car = 1000 \xF7 (50 \xF7 t) = 20t\n\u21D2 Distance covered by the person in this 20t duration = Speed of person \xD7 Time\n\u21D2 Distance = (20 \xF7 t) \xD7 20t\n\u21D2 Distance = 400 m\n\u2234 The correct answer is 400 m.\n\nAdditional Information\nDirect Proportionality of Distance\n\nWhen the time allowed for travel is kept constant, the distance covered is directly proportional to the speed (D1 \xF7 D2 = S1 \xF7 S2).\n\nInverse Proportionality of Time\n\nWhen the distance to be covered is constant, the time taken is inversely proportional to the speed (T1 \xF7 T2 = S2 \xF7 S1).\n\nUnit Conversion Trick\n\nTo convert kmph to m/s, multiply the speed by 5 \xF7 18. To convert m/s to kmph, multiply by 18 \xF7 5.",
