        userTime: "00:46",
        avgTime: "00:33",
        question: "Two wires of lengths 10 m 54 cm and 11 m 56 cm are both cut into pieces of length x cm each, where x is an integer. Find the maximum value of x.",
        options: {
          a: "42",
          b: "56",
          c: "34",
          d: "28"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nConvert the lengths to cm: 1054 cm and 1156 cm.\n\nTo find the maximum equal piece length x, calculate the HCF of 1054 and 1156.\n\nDifference between the numbers = 1156 - 1054 = 102.\n\nThe HCF must be a factor of the difference (102). Factors of 102 are 102, 51, 34, 17, etc.\n\nBoth numbers are even and not divisible by 102. Checking 34: 1054 \xF7 34 = 31 and 1156 \xF7 34 = 34.\n\n\u2234 The correct answer is 34 cm.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nLength of first wire = 10 m 54 cm = 1000 cm + 54 cm = 1054 cm\nLength of second wire = 11 m 56 cm = 1100 cm + 56 cm = 1156 cm\n\nBoth wires are cut into pieces of length x cm. For x to be maximum, x must be the Highest Common Factor (HCF) of 1054 and 1156.\n\nCalculations:\nWe will use the prime factorization method to find the HCF.\n\n\u21D2 1054 = 2 \xD7 17 \xD7 31\n\u21D2 1156 = 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 17 \xD7 17\n\nThe common prime factors between the two numbers are 2 and 17.\n\n\u21D2 HCF = 2 \xD7 17 = 34\n\u21D2 The maximum possible integer value of x is 34.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 34 cm.\n\nAdditional Information\nHCF Application\n\nHCF is used when we need to split things into smaller, perfectly equal sections without leaving any remainder, or when trying to find the maximum possible length, weight, or size.\n\nDifference Method for HCF\n\nThe HCF of two numbers is always either the absolute difference between the numbers or a factor of that difference. This is a powerful shortcut for exam problems.\n\nPrime Factorization Method\n\nExpress each number as a product of prime numbers. The HCF is the product of the lowest powers of all common prime factors shared between the numbers.",
