        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nRevolutions per minute = 12\nRevolutions per second = 12 \xF7 60 = 1/5\nAngle completed in 1 revolution = 2\u03C0 radians\nAngle described in 1 second = 1/5 \xD7 2\u03C0 = 2\u03C0/5 radians\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2\u03C0/5 radians.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nRevolutions made by the wheel in 1 minute = 12 / 1 minute = 60 seconds\n\nFormula Used:\nAngle rotated (in radians) = Number of revolutions \xD7 2\u03C0\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Number of revolutions in 60 seconds = 12\n\u21D2 Number of revolutions in 1 second = 12 \xF7 60\n\u21D2 Number of revolutions in 1 second = 1/5\n\u21D2 We know that in 1 complete revolution, the angle described at the center is 2\u03C0 radians.\n\u21D2 Angle described in 1 second = (1/5) \xD7 2\u03C0\n\u21D2 Angle described in 1 second = 2\u03C0/5 radians\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2\u03C0/5 radians.\n\nAdditional Information\nAngular Velocity (\u03C9)\n\nIt is defined as the rate of change of angular displacement. It is given by \u03C9 = \u03B8 \xF7 t, and is measured in radians per second.\n\nRadian to Degree Conversion\n\n1 radian = 180\xB0 \xF7 \u03C0. An angle of 2\u03C0/5 radians directly corresponds to (2 \xD7 180\xB0) \xF7 5 = 72\xB0.\n\nRevolutions to Radians Relation\n\n1 complete revolution around a circle subtends a full central angle of 360\xB0, which is exactly equal to 2\u03C0 radians.",
