        question: "A sum increases by 75% in 10 years at certain rate of simple interest per annum. By what percentage will the same sum increase in 6 years at the same rate of simple interest per annum?",
        options: {
          a: "42%",
          b: "45%",
          c: "38%",
          d: "48%"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIn Simple Interest, the percentage increase is directly proportional to the time period.\n\nPercentage increase in 10 years = 75%\nPercentage increase in 1 year = 75% \xF7 10 = 7.5%\nPercentage increase in 6 years = 7.5% \xD7 6 = 45%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 45%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nA sum increases by 75% in 10 years at simple interest.\n\nTime (T1) = 10 years, Time (T\xB2) = 6 years.\n\nFormula Used:\nSimple Interest (SI) = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) \xF7 100\n\nCalculation:\nLet the principal (P) be 100.\n\u21D2 Since the sum increases by 75%, Simple Interest for 10 years = 75.\n\u21D2 75 = (100 \xD7 R \xD7 10) \xF7 100\n\u21D2 75 = 10R\n\u21D2 R = 7.5% per annum\nNow, calculate the SI for 6 years at the same rate of interest:\n\u21D2 New SI = (100 \xD7 7.5 \xD7 6) \xF7 100\n\u21D2 New SI = 7.5 \xD7 6\n\u21D2 New SI = 45\n\u21D2 Since the principal is 100, an SI of 45 means the sum increases by 45%.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 45%.\n\nAdditional Information\nDirect Proportion in Simple Interest\n\nFor a fixed principal and constant rate, the interest earned is directly proportional to the time period (SI \u221D T). You can directly use the ratio: SI1 \xF7 T1 = SI\xB2 \xF7 T\xB2.\n\nPercentage Increase\n\nWhen a problem states that a sum increases by a certain percentage, that percentage increase is exactly equal to the Simple Interest accumulated. Amount = Principal + SI.\n\nRate of Interest\n\nRate per annum (R) can be determined efficiently by isolating it from the SI formula: R = (SI \xD7 100) \xF7 (P \xD7 T).",
