        status: "Correct (Slow)",
        chosenOption: "A",
        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:47",
        avgTime: "00:30",
        question: "The graphs of the equations 3x + y - 5 = 0 and 2x - y - 5 = 0 intersect at the point P(\u03B1, \u03B2). What will be the value of (\u03B1 + \u03B2)?",
        options: {
          a: "1",
          b: "0",
          c: "2",
          d: "-1"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nAdd both given equations directly to eliminate y: (3x + y - 5) + (2x - y - 5) = 0 \u21D2 5x - 10 = 0 \u21D2 x = 2\nSubstitute x = 2 into the first equation: 3(2) + y - 5 = 0 \u21D2 6 + y - 5 = 0 \u21D2 y = -1\nThe point of intersection is (\u03B1, \u03B2) = (2, -1), so \u03B1 + \u03B2 = 2 + (-1) = 1.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 1.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nEquation 1: 3x + y - 5 = 0\nEquation 2: 2x - y - 5 = 0\n\nIntersection point is P(\u03B1, \u03B2).\n\nO / X\n\nY\n\n3x + y = 5\n2x - y = 5\n\nP(2, -1)\xB2 -1\n\nCalculations:\nFrom Equation 1, express y in terms of x:\n\n\u21D2 y = 5 - 3x\n\nSubstitute the value of y into Equation 2:\n\n\u21D2 2x - (5 - 3x) - 5 = 0\n\u21D2 2x - 5 + 3x - 5 = 0\n\u21D2 5x - 10 = 0\n\u21D2 5x = 10\n\u21D2 x = 2\nNow, substitute x = 2 back into the expression for y:\n\u21D2 y = 5 - 3(2)\n\u21D2 y = 5 - 6 = -1\n\nThe point of intersection P(\u03B1, \u03B2) is (2, -1).\n\nTherefore, \u03B1 = 2 and \u03B2 = -1.\nNow, calculate the required value (\u03B1 + \u03B2):\n\u21D2 \u03B1 + \u03B2 = 2 + (-1) = 1\n\u2234 The correct answer is 1.\n\nAdditional Information\nLinear Equations in Two Variables\n\nThe general form is ax + by + c = 0. Its graph always represents a straight line on the coordinate plane.\n\nIntersection of Two Lines\n\nTwo distinct linear equations intersect at exactly one unique point if their slopes are different, forming a unique mathematical solution.\n\nCoordinates and Solutions\n\nThe intersection point (\u03B1, \u03B2) is the only point that simultaneously satisfies the algebraic conditions of both linear equations.",
