        testName: "Quantitative Aptitude Sectional Test - 7",
        platform: "Testbook",
        status: "Correct (Slow)",
        chosenOption: "A",
        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:48",
        avgTime: "00:27",
        question: "The average of five consecutive odd numbers is 15. What is the smallest of these five numbers?",
        options: {
          a: "11",
          b: "13",
          c: "15",
          d: "9"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nFor consecutive odd numbers, the average is exactly the middle term.\n\nSince there are 5 numbers, the 3rd (middle) number is 15.\n\nConsecutive odd numbers always decrease by 2 as we move left.\n\nSmallest number (1st number) = 15 - 2 \xD7 2 = 11.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 11.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nAverage of 5 consecutive odd numbers = 15\n\nFormula Used:\nAverage = Sum of all terms \xF7 Total number of terms - 2 -2\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the 5 consecutive odd numbers be x, x + 2, x + 4, x + 6, and x + 8.\n\u21D2 Sum = x + (x + 2) + (x + 4) + (x + 6) + (x + 8) = 5x + 20\n\u21D2 Average = (5x + 20) \xF7 5 = 15\n\u21D2 x + 4 = 15\n\u21D2 x = 11\n\u21D2 The smallest number is x = 11.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 11.\n\nAdditional Information\nAverage of an Arithmetic Progression\n\nFor any sequence with a constant difference (like consecutive even or odd numbers), the average is exactly the middle term if the count of terms is odd, and the average of the two middle terms if the count is even. It can also be found using: Average = (First Term + Last Term) \xF7 2.\n\nDifference Between Consecutive Terms\n\nThe difference between any two consecutive even numbers or consecutive odd numbers is always 2.\n\nSum of First n Odd Numbers\n\nThe sum of the first n consecutive odd numbers starting from 1 is always exactly n\xB2.",
        topic: "Average",
