        userTime: "00:30",
        avgTime: "00:22",
        question: "A man loses 25% of his money. After spending 60% of the remaining money, he has Rs. 360 left. What is the amount of money (in Rs.) he originally had?",
        options: {
          a: "1200",
          b: "1962",
          c: "654",
          d: "2400"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nLet the original amount of money be P.\nUsing the successive percentage multiplying factor method: P \xD7 75% \xD7 40% = 360\nP \xD7 (3/4) \xD7 (2/5) = 360\nP = 360 \xD7 (10/3) = 1200\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B91200.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPercentage of money lost = 25%\nPercentage of remaining money spent = 60%\nAmount left = \u20B9360\n\nFormula Used:\nRemaining Amount = Total \xD7 (1 - L1/100) \xD7 (1 - L\xB2/100)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the original amount be 100 units.\n\u21D2 After losing 25%, remaining amount = 100 - 25 = 75 units.\n\u21D2 He then spends 60% of the remaining amount.\n\u21D2 Amount spent = 60% of 75 units = (60 \xF7 100) \xD7 75 = 45 units.\n\u21D2 Remaining amount left with him = 75 - 45 = 30 units.\n\u21D2 According to the question, 30 units = \u20B9360.\n\u21D2 1 unit = 360 \xF7 30 = \u20B912.\n\u21D2 Original amount = 100 units = 100 \xD7 12 = \u20B91200.\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B91200.\n\nAdditional Information\nSuccessive Percentage Change\n\nWhen a value is subjected to multiple percentage changes sequentially, the final value can be calculated directly using: Final = Initial \xD7 (1 - a/100) \xD7 (1 - b/100).\n\nBase of Percentage\n\nIt is critical to identify the correct base for any percentage. Words like 'of the remaining' indicate the new percentage must be calculated on the newly reduced value, not the original starting total.",
        topic: "Percentage",
