        userTime: "01:14",
        avgTime: "00:52",
        question: "An article is sold at a certain price. If it is sold at 33\\(\\frac{1}{3}\\)% of this price, there is a loss of 33\\(\\frac{1}{3}\\)%, What is the percentage profit or percentage loss when it is sold at 40% of the original selling price?",
        options: {
          a: "Profit 18%",
          b: "Loss 12%",
          c: "Loss 20%",
          d: "Profit 15%"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nLet Original Selling Price (SP) = 300 units.\nNew SP = 331\u20443% of 300 = 100 units.\nLoss of 331\u20443% means SP is 2/3 of CP \u21D2 2/3 \xD7 CP = 100 \u21D2 CP = 150 units.\nSelling at 40% of Original SP = 40% of 300 = 120 units.\nLoss % = (150 - 120) \xF7 150 \xD7 100 = 20%.\n\u2234 The correct answer is Loss 20%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nSelling price at 1st condition = 331\u20443% of original selling price\nLoss at 1st condition = 331\u20443%\nSelling price at 2nd condition = 40% of original selling price\n\nFormula Used:\nLoss = Cost Price (CP) - Selling Price (SP)\nLoss % = (Loss \xF7 CP) \xD7 100\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let Original Selling Price = SP and Cost Price = CP\n\u21D2 Selling price in 1st condition = 331\u20443% of SP = SP \xF7 3\n\u21D2 Loss % in 1st condition = 331\u20443% = 1/3\n\u21D2 SP \xF7 3 = CP - (1/3 \xD7 CP)\n\u21D2 SP \xF7 3 = 2/3 \xD7 CP\n\u21D2 SP = 2 \xD7 CP\n\u21D2 Selling Price in 2nd condition = 40% of SP = 0.4 \xD7 (2 \xD7 CP) = 0.8 \xD7 CP\n\u21D2 Since New SP is less than CP, there is a loss.\n\u21D2 Loss = CP - 0.8 \xD7 CP = 0.2 \xD7 CP\n\u21D2 Percentage Loss = (0.2 \xD7 CP \xF7 CP) \xD7 100 = 20%\n\u2234 The correct answer is Loss 20%.\n\nAdditional Information\nCost Price & Selling Price\nProfit occurs when Selling Price > Cost Price (Profit = SP - CP). Loss occurs when Cost Price > Selling Price (Loss = CP - SP).\nProfit and Loss Percentage\n\nBoth profit and loss percentages are always calculated on the Cost Price unless stated otherwise. Formula: Profit/Loss % = (Profit or Loss \xF7 CP) \xD7 100.\n\nFractional Equivalents\n\nConverting percentages to fractions speeds up calculations. For example, 331\u20443% = 1/3, 20% = 1/5, and 40% = 2/5.",
