        userTime: "00:50",
        avgTime: "00:35",
        question: "If the ratio of the mode and median is 13 \u2236 9, then find the ratio of the mean and mode using empirical relation.",
        options: {
          a: "9 \u2236 8",
          b: "8 \u2236 13",
          c: "8 \u2236 9",
          d: "7 \u2236 13"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nLet Mode = 13 and Median = 9 based on the given ratio.\nEmpirical formula: Mode = 3 \xD7 Median - 2 \xD7 Mean\n\u21D2 13 = 3 \xD7 9 - 2 \xD7 Mean\n\u21D2 2 \xD7 Mean = 27 - 13 = 14\n\u21D2 Mean = 7\n\u2234 The correct answer is 7 : 13.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nRatio of Mode to Median = 13 : 9\n\nFormula Used:\nEmpirical Relation: Mode = 3 \xD7 Median - 2 \xD7 Mean\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let Mode = 13x and Median = 9x\n\u21D2 Substituting the values into the empirical formula:\n\u21D2 13x = 3 \xD7 (9x) - 2 \xD7 Mean\n\u21D2 13x = 27x - 2 \xD7 Mean\n\u21D2 2 \xD7 Mean = 27x - 13x\n\u21D2 2 \xD7 Mean = 14x\n\u21D2 Mean = 7x\n\u21D2 Required ratio = Mean : Mode\n\u21D2 Ratio = 7x : 13x = 7 : 13\n\u2234 The correct answer is 7 : 13.\n\nAdditional Information\nEmpirical Relationship\n\nFor a moderately skewed distribution, the relationship between the three measures of central tendency is given by: Mode = 3 \xD7 Median - 2 \xD7 Mean.\n\nTypes of Distribution Skewness\n\nSymmetrical Distribution: Mean = Median = Mode.\nPositively Skewed: Mean > Median > Mode.\nNegatively Skewed: Mean < Median < Mode.\n\nMeasures of Central Tendency\n\nMean is the average of all data points. Median is the middlemost value when data is arranged in ascending order. Mode is the value that occurs most frequently in the dataset.",
