        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nWhen distance is constant, Speed \u221D 1 / Time\n\nRatio of Time = T1 : T\xB2 = 7 : 19/4 = 28 : 19\nRatio of Speed = S1 : S\xB2 = 19 : 28\nGiven 19 units = 190 km/h \u21D2 1 unit = 10 km/h\nRequired Speed = 28 units = 280 km/h\n\u2234 The correct answer is 280 km/h.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nInitial speed = 190 km/h\nInitial time = 7 hours\nNew time = 4(3/4) hours = 19/4 hours\n\nFormula Used:\nDistance = Speed \xD7 Time\nNew Speed = Total Distance \xF7 New Time\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Total Distance = 190 \xD7 7 = 1330 km\n\u21D2 New Speed = Total Distance \xF7 New Time\n\u21D2 New Speed = 1330 \xF7 (19/4)\n\u21D2 New Speed = 1330 \xD7 (4 / 19)\n\u21D2 New Speed = 70 \xD7 4 = 280 km/h\n\u2234 The correct answer is 280 km/h.\n\nAdditional Information\nSpeed, Distance & Time Relationship\nDistance = Speed \xD7 Time. For a constant distance, speed is inversely proportional to time (S \u221D 1 / T).\n\nUnit Conversions\n\nTo convert km/h to m/s, multiply by 5/18. To convert m/s to km/h, multiply by 18/5.\n\nAverage Speed\n\nAverage Speed = Total Distance \xF7 Total Time. If two equal distances are covered at speeds a and b, Average Speed = (2ab) \xF7 (a + b).",
