        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nWe can use the constant product ratio method.\n\nPercentage increase in price = 25% = 1/4\n\nIf price increases by a ratio of 1/x, consumption must decrease by 1/(x + 1) to keep expenditure constant.\n\nRequired decrease in consumption = 1 \xF7 (4 + 1) = 1/5\nPercentage decrease = (1/5) \xD7 100% = 20%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 20%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPrice increase = 25%\n\nExpenditure remains the same.\n\nFormula Used:\nExpenditure = Price \xD7 Consumption\nPercentage decrease = [ (Original Value - New Value) \xF7 Original Value ] \xD7 100%\n\nCalculation:\n\u21D2 Let the initial price of fruits be \u20B9100 per unit.\n\u21D2 Let the initial consumption be 100 units.\n\u21D2 Initial Expenditure = 100 \xD7 100 = \u20B910000\n\u21D2 The new price after a 25% increase = 100 + 25 = \u20B9125 per unit.\n\u21D2 To keep the expenditure at \u20B910000, let the new consumption be C units.\n\u21D2 125 \xD7 C = 10000\n\u21D2 C = 10000 \xF7 125 = 80 units\n\u21D2 Decrease in consumption = 100 - 80 = 20 units\n\u21D2 Percentage decrease in consumption = (20 \xF7 100) \xD7 100% = 20%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 20%.\n\nAdditional Information\nConstant Product Rule\n\nIf the product of two variables A and B is constant, an increase of 1/x in A requires a corresponding decrease of 1/(x + 1) in B to maintain the same total.\n\nPercentage Increase to Decrease Conversion\n\nIf a value is increased by R%, to restore it back to the original value, it must be decreased by [R \xF7 (100 + R)] \xD7 100%.\n\nSuccessive Percentage Change\n\nIf a value changes by a% and then consecutively by b%, the net percentage change is given by the formula (a + b + ab \xF7 100)%.",
