        question: "The supplement of an angle is 15\xB0 more than three times its complement. The measure of the angle is:",
        options: {
          a: "57.5\xB0",
          b: "65\xB0",
          c: "52.5\xB0",
          d: "72.5\xB0"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nThe supplement of any angle is always 90\xB0 more than its complement (C).\n\nSo, C + 90\xB0 = 3C + 15\xB0\n\u21D2 2C = 75\xB0 \u21D2 C = 37.5\xB0\nThe required angle is 90\xB0 - C = 90\xB0 - 37.5\xB0 = 52.5\xB0\n\u2234 The correct answer is 52.5\xB0.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nThe supplement of an angle is 15\xB0 more than three times its complement.\n\nFormula Used:\nComplement of an angle x = 90\xB0 - x\n\nCalculation:\n\u21D2 180\xB0 - x = 3 \xD7 (90\xB0 - x) + 15\xB0\n\u21D2 180\xB0 - x = 270\xB0 - 3x + 15\xB0\n\u21D2 180\xB0 - x = 285\xB0 - 3x\n\u21D2 3x - x = 285\xB0 - 180\xB0\n\u21D2 2x = 105\xB0\n\u21D2 x = 52.5\xB0\n\u2234 The correct answer is 52.5\xB0.\n\nAdditional Information\nComplementary Angles\n\nTwo angles are complementary if their sum is exactly 90\xB0. If one angle is \u03B8, its complement is 90\xB0 - \u03B8.\n\nSupplementary Angles\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their sum is exactly 180\xB0. If one angle is \u03B8, its supplement is 180\xB0 - \u03B8.\n\nDifference Between Supplement and Complement\n\nFor any given angle, the difference between its supplement and its complement is always constant and equals exactly 90\xB0.",
