        question: "Average of 'n' observations is 38, average of 'n' other observations is 42 and average of remaining 'n' observations is 55. Average of all the observations is:",
        options: {
          a: "45",
          b: "35",
          c: "55",
          d: "40"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nSince the number of observations in all three groups is equal ('n'), the overall average is simply the arithmetic mean of their individual averages.\n\nOverall Average = (Sum of all averages) \xF7 (Number of groups)\nOverall Average = (38 + 42 + 55) \xF7 3\nOverall Average = 135 \xF7 3 = 45\n\u2234 The correct answer is 45.\n\nAlternate Method\nn obs.\n\nAvg: 38 + n obs.\nAvg: 42 + n obs.\nAvg: 55\nTotal: 3n obs.\nAvg: ?\n\nGiven:\nThree groups of observations, each containing 'n' observations.\n\nAverages of the three groups are 38, 42, and 55 respectively.\n\nFormula Used:\nSum of observations = Average \xD7 Number of observations\nOverall Average = Total Sum of Observations \xF7 Total Number of Observations\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Sum of first 'n' observations = 38 \xD7 n = 38n\n\u21D2 Sum of second 'n' observations = 42 \xD7 n = 42n\n\u21D2 Sum of remaining 'n' observations = 55 \xD7 n = 55n\n\u21D2 Total sum of all observations = 38n + 42n + 55n = 135n\n\u21D2 Total number of observations = n + n + n = 3n\n\u21D2 Overall average = 135n \xF7 3n\n\u21D2 Overall average = 45\n\u2234 The correct answer is 45.\n\nAdditional Information\nCombined Average\n\nWhen combining multiple groups with different numbers of items (n1, n\xB2, ...), the combined average is given by the weighted average formula: A = (n1A1 + n2A\xB2 + ...) \xF7 (n1 + n\xB2 + ...).\n\nEqual Weights Shortcut\n\nIf the number of items in all combined groups is identical (n1 = n\xB2 = n\xB3 ...), the combined average simplifies to the basic arithmetic mean of the individual averages: (A1 + A\xB2 + A\xB3 + ...) \xF7 Total Groups.\n\nNet Deviation Method\n\nAssume an arbitrary mean (e.g., 40). Find the net deviation of each group's average from it. The final average is calculated as: Assumed Mean + (Net Deviation \xF7 Total Number of Items). This technique helps in fast mental calculations.",
