        userTime: "01:04",
        avgTime: "00:27",
        question: "An inlet pipe can fill an empty tank in 120 hours while an outlet pipe drains a completely-filled tank in 54 hours. If 8 inlet pipes and 3 outlet pipes are opened simultaneously, when the tank is empty, then in how many hours will the tank get completely filled?",
        options: {
          a: "81",
          b: "96",
          c: "72",
          d: "90"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTotal Capacity (LCM of 120 and 54) = 1080 units.\nEfficiency of 1 inlet pipe = 1080 \xF7 120 = +9 units/hr.\nEfficiency of 1 outlet pipe = 1080 \xF7 (-54) = -20 units/hr.\nNet efficiency of (8 inlets + 3 outlets) = (8 \xD7 9) + (3 \xD7 -20) = 72 - 60 = +12 units/hr.\nTime required to fill tank = 1080 \xF7 12 = 90 hours.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 90 hours.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\n\u21D2 Time taken by 1 inlet pipe to fill the tank = 120 hours\n\u21D2 Time taken by 1 outlet pipe to empty the tank = 54 hours\n\u21D2 Number of inlet pipes opened = 8\n\u21D2 Number of outlet pipes opened = 3\n\nFormula Used:\n\u21D2 Efficiency = Total Work \xF7 Time\n\u21D2 Total Time = Total Work \xF7 Net Combined Efficiency\n\nPipe Hours Total Work Efficiency\n\n1 Inlet Pipe 120 1080 + 9\n\n1 Outlet Pipe 54 - 20\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Efficiency of 8 inlet pipes = 8 \xD7 (+9) = +72 units/hour\n\u21D2 Efficiency of 3 outlet pipes = 3 \xD7 (-20) = -60 units/hour\n\u21D2 Net combined efficiency when both types are opened together = +72 - 60 = +12 units/hour\n\u21D2 The positive sign indicates that the tank is being filled.\n\u21D2 Total time required to completely fill the tank = 1080 \xF7 12\n\u21D2 Total time = 90 hours\n\u2234 The correct answer is 90 hours.\n\nAdditional Information\nPipes and Cisterns Basics\n\nIf an inlet pipe fills a tank in x hours and an outlet pipe empties it in y hours, the net part of the tank filled in 1 hour is given by (1/x - 1/y).\n\nEfficiency Approach (LCM Method)\n\nTreat inlet pipes as positive work providers (filling) and outlet pipes as negative work providers (draining). The total capacity of the tank is considered as the Least Common Multiple (LCM) of their individual completion times.\n\nMultiple Similar Pipes\n\nWhen multiple pipes of the same capacity operate simultaneously, simply multiply the individual pipe's efficiency by the total number of identical pipes before evaluating the net overall flow.",
        topic: "Pipes & Cisterns",
