        userTime: "01:04",
        avgTime: "00:48",
        question: "Mahesh sold \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) of his stock at a gain of 20% and the remaining stock at a gain of 15%. Find the total gain percentage (approximate).",
        options: {
          a: "19%",
          b: "20%",
          c: "15%",
          d: "17%"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nUse the weighted average formula to directly calculate the net gain.\n\nTotal Gain % = (Part1 \xD7 Gain1%) + (Part\xB2 \xD7 Gain2%)\nTotal Gain % = (3/4 \xD7 20%) + (1/4 \xD7 15%)\nTotal Gain % = 15% + 3.75% = 18.75% \u2248 19%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 19%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nFraction of stock sold at 20% gain = 3/4\nRemaining fraction of stock = 1 - 3/4 = 1/4\nGain on the remaining stock = 15%\n\nFormula Used:\nTotal Net Gain % = (Fraction1 \xD7 Gain1) + (Fraction\xB2 \xD7 Gain\xB2)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the total cost price (CP) of the stock be 400 units to make calculations easier.\n\u21D2 CP of 3/4th of the stock = 3/4 \xD7 400 = 300 units\n\u21D2 CP of the remaining 1/4th of the stock = 1/4 \xD7 400 = 100 units\n\u21D2 Profit on the first part = 20% of 300 = (20 \xF7 100) \xD7 300 = 60 units\n\u21D2 Profit on the second part = 15% of 100 = (15 \xF7 100) \xD7 100 = 15 units\n\u21D2 Total Profit = 60 + 15 = 75 units\n\u21D2 Total Gain % = (Total Profit \xF7 Total CP) \xD7 100\n\u21D2 Total Gain % = (75 \xF7 400) \xD7 100\n\u21D2 Total Gain % = 18.75%\n\u21D2 Rounding off to the nearest integer, we get 19%.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 19%.\n\nAdditional Information\nOverall Profit / Loss on Partial Sales\n\nWhen items are divided into fractions and sold at different profit or loss percentages, the net percentage is the sum of products of each fraction and its respective profit/loss. Losses must be assigned a negative sign.\n\nWeighted Average Method\n\nTotal Average = (n1x1 + n2x\xB2) \xF7 (n1 + n\xB2). This is the fastest method to use when quantities (or fractions) are mixed with different average values or percentages.\n\nAlligation Method\n\nIf two parts having profit percentages P1 and P\xB2 are mixed to yield an overall profit percentage P, the ratio of the quantities in which they are mixed is (P - P\xB2) : (P1 - P).",
