        question: "A shopkeeper gives 20% discount on MRP. Joginder buys a suitcase from the shop, at an additional discount of 20% on the reduced price. If the MRP of the suitcase is Rs. 1,200, then find the purchasing price paid by Joginder.",
        options: {
          a: "Rs. 864",
          b: "Rs. 800",
          c: "Rs. 768",
          d: "Rs. 600"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nEquivalent discount for two successive discounts of 20% = 20 + 20 - (20 \xD7 20) / 100 = 36%.\nPurchasing Price = (100 - 36)% of MRP\n\u21D2 Purchasing Price = 64% of 1200\n\u21D2 64/100 \xD7 1200 = 768\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 768.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nMarked Retail Price (MRP) = Rs. 1200\nFirst discount (d1) = 20%\nSecond discount (d\xB2) = 20%\n\nFormula Used:\nSelling Price (SP) = MRP \xD7 (100 - d1)/100 \xD7 (100 - d\xB2)/100\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 First, apply a 20% discount on Rs. 1200\n\u21D2 Reduced Price = 1200 \xD7 (100 - 20) / 100 = 1200 \xD7 80 / 100 = Rs. 960\n\u21D2 Second, apply an additional 20% discount on the reduced price of Rs. 960\n\u21D2 Purchasing Price = 960 \xD7 (100 - 20) / 100 = 960 \xD7 80 / 100 = Rs. 768\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 768.\n\nAdditional Information\nEquivalent Discount\n\nWhen two successive discounts d1% and d2% are given, the single equivalent discount % = d1 + d\xB2 - (d1 \xD7 d\xB2) / 100.\n\nMarked Price (MRP)\n\nIt is the printed price of an article. Any discount offered to the customer is always calculated on the Marked Price unless stated otherwise.\n\nProfit / Loss Percentage\nProfit % = (SP - CP) / CP \xD7 100. Loss % = (CP - SP) / CP \xD7 100. Both are strictly calculated on the Cost Price (CP).",
