        userTime: "00:41",
        avgTime: "00:32",
        question: "The diagonal of a square is \\(\\sqrt{200}\\) cm. If the ratio of length and breadth of a rectangle is 5 : 2, whose area is equal to the area of a square, then what is the length of the rectangle?",
        options: {
          a: "\\(\\rm \\sqrt{250} \\ cm\\)",
          b: "\\(\\rm \\sqrt{200} \\ cm\\)",
          c: "\\(\\rm 2\\sqrt{10} \\ cm\\)",
          d: "\\(\\rm \\sqrt{20} \\ cm\\)"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nArea of the square = d\xB2 \xF7 2 = (\u221A200)2 \xF7 2 = 100 cm\xB2.\nLet rectangle dimensions be 5x and 2x, so Area = 5x \xD7 2x = 10x\xB2.\nEquating areas: 10x\xB2 = 100 \u21D2 x\xB2 = 10 \u21D2 x = \u221A10.\nLength = 5x = 5\u221A10 = \u221A250 cm.\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u221A250 cm.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\n\u21D2 Diagonal of the square, d = \u221A200 cm\n\u21D2 Ratio of Length to Breadth of the rectangle = 5 : 2\n\u21D2 Area of the rectangle = Area of the square\n\nFormula Used:\n\u21D2 Area of a square = d\xB2 \xF7 2\n\u21D2 Area of a rectangle = Length \xD7 Breadth\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Area of the square = (\u221A200)2 \xF7 2\n\u21D2 Area of the square = 200 \xF7 2 = 100 cm\xB2\n\u21D2 Let the length and breadth of the rectangle be 5x and 2x respectively.\n\u21D2 Area of the rectangle = 5x \xD7 2x = 10x\xB2\n\u21D2 Since their areas are strictly equal:\n\u21D2 10x\xB2 = 100\n\u21D2 x\xB2 = 10\n\u21D2 x = \u221A10 cm\n\u21D2 Length of the rectangle = 5x = 5\u221A10 cm\n\u21D2 Expressing 5\u221A10 under a single square root factor: \u221A(52 \xD7 10) = \u221A(25 \xD7 10) = \u221A250 cm\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u221A250 cm.\n\nAdditional Information\nArea of a Square\n\nIt can be directly calculated using the side length as Area = a\xB2, or by using the diagonal length as Area = d\xB2 \xF7 2.\n\nRectangle Perimeter\n\nThe total length of the continuous boundary of a given rectangle is algebraically calculated as Perimeter = 2 \xD7 (Length + Breadth).\n\nDiagonal of a Rectangle\n\nBoth diagonals are strictly equal in length. The spatial length of each diagonal is calculated systematically using Pythagoras theorem as d = \u221A(L\xB2 + B\xB2).",
