        question: "A is 5 times as good as workman as B and therefore is able to complete a job in 60 days less than B. In how many days will they finish it working together?",
        options: {
          a: "12\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) days",
          b: "12 days",
          c: "14\\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) days",
          d: "14 days"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nEfficiency ratio of A : B = 5 : 1 \u2192 Time ratio of A : B = 1 : 5.\nTime difference = 5 - 1 = 4 units \u2192 4 units = 60 days, so 1 unit = 15 days.\nTime taken by A = 15 days, Time taken by B = 75 days.\nTotal work = LCM(15, 75) = 75 units.\nTime taken together = Total Work \xF7 Total Efficiency = 75 \xF7 6 = 12.5 days.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 121\u20442 days.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nA is 5 times as efficient as B.\n\nA takes 60 days less than B to complete the job.\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Efficiency is inversely proportional to the time taken.\n\u21D2 Ratio of efficiencies A : B = 5 : 1\n\u21D2 Ratio of time taken A : B = 1 : 5\n\u21D2 Difference in ratio parts = 5 - 1 = 4 parts\n\u21D2 4 parts = 60 days \u21D2 1 part = 15 days\n\u21D2 Time taken by A = 1 \xD7 15 = 15 days\n\u21D2 Time taken by B = 5 \xD7 15 = 75 days\n\nPerson Days Total Work Efficiency\n\nA 15 75 5\n\nB 75 1\n\n\u21D2 Combined efficiency of A and B = 5 + 1 = 6 units/day\n\u21D2 Time taken working together = Total Work \xF7 Combined Efficiency\n\u21D2 Time taken = 75 \xF7 6 = 25/2 = 12.5 days = 121\u20442 days\n\u2234 The correct answer is 121\u20442 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nEfficiency & Time Relationship\n\nEfficiency and time are strictly inversely proportional to each other. If the efficiency ratio of two workers is x : y, the ratio of the time taken by them to complete the same work will be y : x.\n\nTotal Work Formula\nTotal Work = Efficiency \xD7 Time Taken. To find the combined time taken by multiple workers, simply divide the total work by the sum of their individual efficiencies.\n\nAlternate Day Work Concept\n\nWhen workers work on alternate days, calculate the total work done in one complete cycle (e.g., 2 days for two workers) and scale it near to the total work before manually adding the remaining units.",
