        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIn \u0394SXR, the exterior angle is equal to the sum of the two opposite interior angles.\n\n\u21D2 \u2220QXR = \u2220XSR + \u2220XRS\n\u21D2 120\xB0 = \u2220XSR + 10\xB0 \u21D2 \u2220XSR = 110\xB0\n\nAngles subtended by the same arc (QR) in the same segment of a circle are equal.\n\n\u21D2 \u2220QPR = \u2220QSR = \u2220XSR = 110\xB0\n\u2234 The correct answer is 110\xB0.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\n\u21D2 \u2220QXR = 120\xB0\n\u21D2 \u2220XRS = 10\xB0\n\nFormula Used:\n\u21D2 Linear pair sum = 180\xB0\n\u21D2 Sum of angles in a triangle = 180\xB0\n\u21D2 Angles subtended by the same arc at the circumference are equal.\n\nQ / P\n\nS / R\n\nX\n\n120\xB0\n\n10\xB0\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Since QS is a straight line segment, \u2220SXR and \u2220QXR form a linear pair.\n\u21D2 \u2220SXR + \u2220QXR = 180\xB0\n\u21D2 \u2220SXR = 180\xB0 - 120\xB0 = 60\xB0\n\u21D2 In \u0394SXR, the sum of all interior angles is 180\xB0.\n\u21D2 \u2220XSR + \u2220SXR + \u2220XRS = 180\xB0\n\u21D2 \u2220XSR + 60\xB0 + 10\xB0 = 180\xB0\n\u21D2 \u2220XSR = 180\xB0 - 70\xB0 = 110\xB0\n\u21D2 We know that angles subtended by the same arc at the circumference are equal.\n\u21D2 Therefore, \u2220QPR and \u2220QSR (which is the same as \u2220XSR) subtending the same arc QR must be equal.\n\u21D2 \u2220QPR = \u2220QSR = 110\xB0\n\u2234 The correct answer is 110\xB0.\n\nAdditional Information\nAngles in the Same Segment\n\nAngles subtended by the same arc at any point on the remaining part of the circle are always equal.\n\nExterior Angle Theorem\n\nThe measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is equal to the sum of the measures of its two remote interior angles.\n\nCyclic Quadrilateral\n\nA quadrilateral formed by four points on a circle is called a cyclic quadrilateral. The sum of its opposite interior angles is always 180\xB0.",
        topic: "Geometry",
        subtopic: "Circles - Angles in the same segment",
        conceptTested: "Exterior Angle Theorem of a triangle and angles subtended by the same arc in a circle are equal.",
