        userTime: "00:07",
        avgTime: "00:44",
        question: "A square pyramid with side 10 cm has a volume of 400 cm^3. Find the total surface area of the pyramid.",
        options: {
          a: "240 cm2",
          b: "300 cm2",
          c: "360 cm2",
          d: "420 cm2"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nVolume = 1/3 \xD7 a2 \xD7 h \u21D2 400 = 1/3 \xD7 100 \xD7 h \u21D2 h = 12 cm.\nSlant height l = \u221A(h2 + (a/2)\xB2) = \u221A(122 + 52) = 13 cm.\nTotal Surface Area (TSA) = Base Area + Lateral Surface Area\n\u21D2 TSA = a2 + 2al\n\u21D2 TSA = 102 + 2 \xD7 10 \xD7 13 = 100 + 260 = 360 cm2.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 360 cm2.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nBase side (a) = 10 cm\nVolume (V) = 400 cm3\n\nFormula Used:\nVolume = 1/3 \xD7 a2 \xD7 h\nSlant height (l) = \u221A(h2 + (a/2)\xB2)\nTotal Surface Area = a2 + 2al\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 400 = 1/3 \xD7 102 \xD7 h\n\u21D2 400 = 1/3 \xD7 100 \xD7 h\n\u21D2 h = (400 \xD7 3) \xF7 100\n\u21D2 h = 12 cm\nNow, finding the slant height (l):\n\u21D2 l = \u221A(122 + (10/2)\xB2)\n\u21D2 l = \u221A(144 + 25)\n\u21D2 l = \u221A169 = 13 cm\nNow, finding the Total Surface Area (TSA):\n\u21D2 TSA = (10)\xB2 + 2 \xD7 10 \xD7 13\n\u21D2 TSA = 100 + 260\n\u21D2 TSA = 360 cm2\n\u2234 The correct answer is 360 cm2.\n\nAdditional Information\nVolume of a Pyramid\n\nThe volume of any pyramid is defined as exactly one-third of the volume of the corresponding prism: V = 1/3 \xD7 Base Area \xD7 Height.\n\nLateral Surface Area (LSA)\n\nFor a regular pyramid, the lateral surface area is formed by identical isosceles triangles: LSA = 1/2 \xD7 Perimeter of Base \xD7 Slant Height.\n\nEuler's Formula for Polyhedrons\n\nFor any solid polyhedron (like a pyramid), the number of Faces (F), Vertices (V), and Edges (E) are related by the formula: F + V = E + 2.",
