        status: "Unattempted",
        chosenOption: "Unattempted",
        correctOption: "D",
        userTime: "00:25",
        avgTime: "00:39",
        question: "Two trains start at the same time from Bangalore and Chennai and proceed towards each other at the speeds of 80 km/h and 95 km/h. When they meet, it is found that one train has travelled 180 km more than the other. The distance between Bangalore and Chennai is:",
        options: {
          a: "345 km",
          b: "400 km",
          c: "1200 km",
          d: "2100 km"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nWhen time is constant, Distance \u221D Speed.\n\nDifference in Speed = 95 - 80 = 15 km/h.\nTotal Speed = 95 + 80 = 175 km/h.\nTotal Distance = (Total Speed \xF7 Difference in Speed) \xD7 Difference in Distance\nTotal Distance = (175 \xF7 15) \xD7 180 = 175 \xD7 12 = 2100 km.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2100 km.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nSpeed of first train = 80 km/h\nSpeed of second train = 95 km/h\nDifference in distance travelled at meeting time = 180 km\n\nFormula Used:\nDistance = Speed \xD7 Time\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the time taken for both trains to meet be t hours.\n\u21D2 Distance covered by first train = 80 \xD7 t\n\u21D2 Distance covered by second train = 95 \xD7 t\n\u21D2 The difference in distance covered = 95t - 80t = 15t\n\u21D2 15t = 180\n\u21D2 t = 180 \xF7 15 = 12 hours\n\u21D2 Total distance between Bangalore and Chennai = 80t + 95t = 175t\n\u21D2 Total distance = 175 \xD7 12\n\u21D2 Total distance = 2100 km\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2100 km.\n\nAdditional Information\nRelative Speed (Opposite Directions)\n\nWhen two bodies move towards each other, their relative speed is the sum of their individual speeds (S1 + S2).\n\nRelative Speed (Same Direction)\n\nWhen two bodies move in the same direction, their relative speed is the difference of their individual speeds (|S1 - S2|).\n\nConstant Time Property\n\nIf two objects travel for the exact same amount of time, the ratio of distances they cover is equal to the ratio of their respective speeds (D1 \xF7 D2 = S1 \xF7 S2).",
        topic: "Time, Speed & Distance",
