        status: "Unattempted",
        chosenOption: "Unattempted",
        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:03",
        avgTime: "00:07",
        question: "Two individuals, A and B, have rented a field. A places 12 horses for 5 months and 20 sheep for 4 months. B places 15 cows for 6 months and 30 sheep for 3 months. If 3 horses consume the same food as 5 cows and 6 cows consume the same food as 10 sheep, find what portion of the rent A should pay?",
        options: {
          a: "3773",
          b: "2345",
          c: "4356",
          d: "5534"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nConvert all animals to Cows (C) to find the total equivalent consumption ratio.\n\nGiven 3H = 5C \u21D2 1H = 5/3 C, and 10S = 6C \u21D2 1S = 3/5 C.\nA's usage = [12 \xD7 (5/3) \xD7 5] + [20 \xD7 (3/5) \xD7 4] = 100 + 48 = 148 cow-months.\nB's usage = (15 \xD7 6) + [30 \xD7 (3/5) \xD7 3] = 90 + 54 = 144 cow-months.\nRatio of rent A : B = 148 : 144 = 37 : 36.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 37/73.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nA places 12 horses for 5 months and 20 sheep for 4 months.\n\nB places 15 cows for 6 months and 30 sheep for 3 months.\n\n3 horses consume same as 5 cows, and 6 cows consume same as 10 sheep.\n\nFormula Used:\nRent Share Ratio = (Number of animals \xD7 Time \xD7 Consumption rate)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let us convert the number of all animals into equivalent number of Cows (C) for simplicity.\n\u21D2 3 Horses = 5 Cows \u21D2 1 Horse = 5/3 Cows\n\u21D2 10 Sheep = 6 Cows \u21D2 1 Sheep = 3/5 Cows\n\u21D2 Equivalent animals placed by A in terms of cows:\n\u21D2 12 Horses = 12 \xD7 (5/3) = 20 Cows\n\u21D2 20 Sheep = 20 \xD7 (3/5) = 12 Cows\n\u21D2 Total equivalent consumption by A = (20 Cows \xD7 5 months) + (12 Cows \xD7 4 months)\n\u21D2 A's total units = 100 + 48 = 148 cow-months\n\u21D2 Equivalent animals placed by B in terms of cows:\n\u21D2 15 Cows = 15 Cows\n\u21D2 30 Sheep = 30 \xD7 (3/5) = 18 Cows\n\u21D2 Total equivalent consumption by B = (15 Cows \xD7 6 months) + (18 Cows \xD7 3 months)\n\u21D2 B's total units = 90 + 54 = 144 cow-months\n\u21D2 Ratio of rent (A : B) = 148 : 144\n\u21D2 Simplifying by dividing both terms by 4, Ratio = 37 : 36\n\u21D2 Portion of rent A should pay = A's share \xF7 (A's share + B's share)\n\u21D2 A's portion = 37 \xF7 (37 + 36) = 37/73\n\u2234 The correct answer is 37/73.\n\nAdditional Information\nPartnership Rent Rule\n\nWhen multiple people rent a common property (like a pasture), the rent paid by each person is directly proportional to the number of resources used multiplied by the duration of usage (Rent \u2248 Animals \xD7 Time).\n\nCompound Ratio Equivalency\n\nIf distinct categories are given (like Horses, Cows, and Sheep), establish a single common base unit by utilizing equivalent ratios. Converting all distinct variables into one unified variable simplifies compound calculations.",
        topic: "Partnership",
        subtopic: "Rent Proportion with Equivalent Consumption",
