        status: "Unattempted",
        chosenOption: "Unattempted",
        correctOption: "B",
        userTime: "00:03",
        avgTime: "00:15",
        question: "What will be the difference between mean and median of the given data?\n21, 11, 27, 8, 5, 12, 7, 23, 3, 14, 9, 19",
        options: {
          a: "1.25",
          b: "1.75",
          c: "1.45",
          d: "1.5"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nMean of the given data = Sum of data \xF7 Total numbers = 159 \xF7 12 = 13.25\nArranging data in ascending order: 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 14, 19, 21, 23, 27\nMedian = Average of 6th and 7th terms = (11 + 12) \xF7 2 = 11.5\nDifference = 13.25 - 11.5 = 1.75\n\u2234 The correct answer is 1.75.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nData set: 21, 11, 27, 8, 5, 12, 7, 23, 3, 14, 9, 19\nTotal number of observations (n) = 12\n\nFormula Used:\nMean = Sum of observations \xF7 Total number of observations\nMedian (for even n) = Average of the (n/2)th and (n/2 + 1)th observations in sorted data\n\nMean vs Median Comparison\n0\n10\n20\n30\nMedian 11.5\nMean 13.25 / 1.75\nDifference\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Sum of observations = 21 + 11 + 27 + 8 + 5 + 12 + 7 + 23 + 3 + 14 + 9 + 19 = 159\n\u21D2 Mean = 159 \xF7 12 = 13.25\n\u21D2 Arranging the given data in ascending order: 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 14, 19, 21, 23, 27\n\u21D2 Since n = 12 (an even number), the median lies between the 6th and 7th terms.\n\u21D2 6th term = 11, and 7th term = 12\n\u21D2 Median = (11 + 12) \xF7 2 = 23 \xF7 2 = 11.5\n\u21D2 Difference = Mean - Median\n\u21D2 Difference = 13.25 - 11.5 = 1.75\n\u2234 The correct answer is 1.75.\n\nAdditional Information\nMean\n\nThe arithmetic average of a set of numbers, calculated by dividing the sum of all observations by the total number of observations.\n\nMedian\n\nThe exact middle value of a sorted dataset. If the dataset has an even number of observations, the median is the average of the two middlemost values.\n\nMode\n\nThe value that appears most frequently in a dataset. A dataset can be unimodal, bimodal, multimodal, or have no mode at all.\n\nEmpirical Relationship\n\nFor a moderately skewed continuous distribution, the fundamental relationship between mean, median, and mode is given by: Mode \u2248 3 \xD7 Median - 2 \xD7 Mean.",
