        userTime: "00:04",
        avgTime: "00:12",
        question: "The areas of the three adjacent faces of a closed cuboidal box are 120 cm^2, 60 cm^2 and 72 cm^2 respectively. Then, the total surface area of the box is:",
        options: {
          a: "252 cm2",
          b: "378 cm2",
          c: "504 cm2",
          d: "630 cm2"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nThe total surface area of a cuboid is exactly twice the sum of the areas of its three adjacent faces.\n\n\u21D2 Total Surface Area = 2 \xD7 (Sum of adjacent faces)\n\u21D2 Total Surface Area = 2 \xD7 (120 + 60 + 72)\n\u21D2 Total Surface Area = 2 \xD7 252 = 504 cm2\n\u2234 The correct answer is 504 cm2.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nThe areas of three adjacent faces of the closed cuboidal box are 120 cm2, 60 cm2, and 72 cm2.\n\nFormula Used:\nTotal Surface Area of Cuboid = 2(lb + bh + hl)\nWhere l, b, and h are the length, breadth, and height of the cuboid.\n\nCalculations:\nLet the length, breadth, and height of the box be l, b, and h respectively.\n\u21D2 Area of the first face (lb) = 120 cm2\n\u21D2 Area of the second face (bh) = 60 cm2\n\u21D2 Area of the third face (hl) = 72 cm2\n\nSubstitute these given areas directly into the total surface area formula:\n\n\u21D2 Total Surface Area = 2 \xD7 (lb + bh + hl)\n\u21D2 Total Surface Area = 2 \xD7 (120 + 60 + 72)\n\u21D2 Total Surface Area = 2 \xD7 252\n\u21D2 Total Surface Area = 504 cm2\n\u2234 The correct answer is 504 cm2.\n\nAdditional Information\nVolume of a Cuboid\n\nIf A1, A2, and A3 are the areas of the three adjacent faces, the volume V is given directly by the square root of their product: V = \u221A(A1 \xD7 A2 \xD7 A3).\n\nLateral Surface Area\n\nThe lateral surface area (area of the four vertical walls) of a cuboid is given by 2h(l + b), which excludes the top and bottom faces.\n\nLength of Diagonal\n\nThe length of the longest diagonal possible inside a given cuboid is calculated as \u221A(l2 + b2 + h2).",
        topic: "Mensuration 3D",
