        userTime: "00:11",
        avgTime: "00:45",
        question: "To pack a set of books, Gautam got cartons of a certain height that were 48 inches long and 27 inches wide. If the volume of such a carton was 22.5 cubic feet, what was the height of each carton? [Use 1 foot = 12 inches.]",
        options: {
          a: "36 inches",
          b: "32.5 inches",
          c: "30 inches",
          d: "32 inches"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nConvert the given volume directly into cubic inches by multiplying by 123.\n\nVolume in inches = 22.5 \xD7 1728 = 38880 inch3\nSince Volume = Length \xD7 Width \xD7 Height\nHeight = 38880 \xF7 (48 \xD7 27)\nHeight = 38880 \xF7 1296 = 30 inches\n\u2234 The correct answer is 30 inches.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nLength (L) = 48 inches\nWidth (B) = 27 inches\nVolume (V) = 22.5 ft3\n1 ft = 12 inches\n\nFormula Used:\nVolume of a cuboid = L \xD7 B \xD7 H\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Volume in inch3 = 22.5 \xD7 12 \xD7 12 \xD7 12\n\u21D2 Volume = 22.5 \xD7 1728 = 38880 inch3\n\u21D2 L \xD7 B \xD7 H = 38880\n\u21D2 48 \xD7 27 \xD7 H = 38880\n\u21D2 1296 \xD7 H = 38880\n\u21D2 H = 38880 \xF7 1296\n\u21D2 H = 30 inches\n\u2234 The correct answer is 30 inches.\n\nAdditional Information\nVolume of a Cuboid\n\nV = L \xD7 B \xD7 H, where L, B, and H denote length, breadth, and height respectively.\n\nSurface Area of a Cuboid\n\nTotal Surface Area (TSA) = 2(LB + BH + HL). Lateral Surface Area (LSA) = 2H(L + B).\n\nDiagonal of a Cuboid\n\nThe length of the longest space diagonal inside a cuboid is \u221A(L2 + B2 + H2).\n\nUnit Conversions\n\nSince 1 foot = 12 inches, calculating volume requires taking the cube of 12. Thus, 1 cubic foot = 123 = 1728 cubic inches.",
