        userTime: "00:05",
        avgTime: "00:11",
        question: "In a group of 260 people, one-fourth are women, two-fifth are men and the rest are children. The average age of women is five-sixth of the average age of men, average age of children is one-third of the average age of men. If the average age of men is 48 years, what is the average age of the group in years?",
        options: {
          a: "42.7",
          b: "34.8",
          c: "38.4",
          d: "32.7"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nWe can find the average directly using fractions without calculating the total number of people.\n\nFraction of Women = 1/4, Men = 2/5 \u21D2 Children = 1 - (1/4 + 2/5) = 1 - 13/20 = 7/20.\nAverage age of Men = 48 years.\nAverage age of Women = 5/6 \xD7 48 = 40 years | Average age of Children = 1/3 \xD7 48 = 16 years.\nGroup Average = (1/4 \xD7 40) + (2/5 \xD7 48) + (7/20 \xD7 16) = 10 + 19.2 + 5.6 = 34.8.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 34.8 years.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTotal people = 260\nNumber of Women = 1/4 of 260 = 65\nNumber of Men = 2/5 of 260 = 104\nNumber of Children = 260 - (65 + 104) = 91\nAverage age of Men = 48 years\nTotal Group (260 people)\nWomen (1/4)\nAvg: 40\nMen (2/5)\nAvg: 48\nChildren (7/20)\nAvg: 16\nWeighted Average Calculation\nGroup Avg = 34.8 years\n\nFormula Used:\nAverage = (Sum of all observations) \xF7 (Total number of observations)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Average age of Women = 5/6 \xD7 48 = 40 years\n\u21D2 Average age of Children = 1/3 \xD7 48 = 16 years\n\u21D2 Total age of Women = 65 \xD7 40 = 2600\n\u21D2 Total age of Men = 104 \xD7 48 = 4992\n\u21D2 Total age of Children = 91 \xD7 16 = 1456\n\u21D2 Total age of the group = 2600 + 4992 + 1456 = 9048\n\u21D2 Average age of the group = 9048 \xF7 260\n\u21D2 Average = 34.8 years\n\u2234 The correct answer is 34.8 years.\n\nAdditional Information\nWeighted Average Method\n\nWhen different sub-groups have unique averages, the overall combined average is calculated as (N1A1 + N2A2 + ...) \xF7 (N1 + N2 + ...).\n\nIndependence from Absolute Totals\n\nIf the relative ratios or percentage fractions of multiple groups are known along with their respective averages, the overall average remains constant regardless of the actual base population size.\n\nProperties of Averages\n\nIf all individual items in a data set are multiplied or divided by a specific constant, the overall average of the data set will also be multiplied or divided by that same constant.",
