        userTime: "00:03",
        avgTime: "00:17",
        question: "While listing N consecutive even numbers for determining their average, the last number, by mistake, got replaced by the next odd number and thereby the average turned out to be more than the desired value by 0.0125. What is the value of N?",
        options: {
          a: "50",
          b: "100",
          c: "40",
          d: "80"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\nShortcut Trick\nThe next odd number after any even number L is L + 1.\n\nTotal Increase in Sum = (L + 1) - L = 1.\nIncrease in Average = (Total Increase in Sum) \xF7 (Number of Terms N).\n0.0125 = 1 \xF7 N\nN = 1 \xF7 0.0125 = 80.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 80.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nNumber of consecutive even numbers = N\nError: Last even number replaced by next odd number\nIncrease in average = 0.0125\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the N consecutive even numbers be E1, E2, ..., EN.\n\u21D2 Let their original sum be S.\n\u21D2 Original Average = S \xF7 N.\n\u21D2 The last number EN is replaced by (EN + 1).\n\u21D2 New Sum = S - EN + (EN + 1) = S + 1.\n\u21D2 New Average = (S + 1) \xF7 N = (S \xF7 N) + (1 \xF7 N).\n\u21D2 Change in average = (New Average) - (Original Average) = 1 \xF7 N.\n\u21D2 Given that this change is 0.0125.\n\u21D2 1 \xF7 N = 0.0125\n\u21D2 N = 1 \xF7 0.0125\n\u21D2 N = 10000 \xF7 125 = 80.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 80.\n\nAdditional Information\nEffect of Observation Change on Average\n\nIf a single observation in a set of N terms is increased by 'k', the new average increases by exactly k \xF7 N compared to the old average.\n\nConsecutive Even Numbers\n\nConsecutive even numbers form an Arithmetic Progression (AP) with a common difference of 2. The average of any N consecutive numbers in an AP is (First term + Last term) \xF7 2.\n\nDecimal to Fraction Conversion\n\nShortcut for N: Since 0.125 = 1/8, then 0.0125 = 1/80. Therefore, 1 \xF7 N = 1 \xF7 80 implies N = 80.",
