        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:05",
        avgTime: "00:20",
        question: "In the set of first 100 multiples of 5, P is the average of the multiples on the odd places and Q is the average of the multiples on the even places. By what number, the value of Q is more than P?",
        options: {
          a: "5",
          b: "10",
          c: "2.5",
          d: "12.5"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\nShortcut Trick\nEach even-placed multiple is exactly 5 more than its preceding odd-placed multiple.\n\nMultiples: (5, 10), (15, 20), (25, 30), ... (495, 500).\nDifference between every pair (Even - Odd) = 5.\nTherefore, Average of Even (Q) - Average of Odd (P) = Constant difference = 5.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 5.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven: First 100 multiples of 5: {5, 10, 15, 20, ..., 500}\nFormula Used: Average of an Arithmetic Progression (AP) = (First term + Last term) \xF7 2\n\u21D2 Set P (Odd places): 1st, 3rd, ..., 99th multiples\n\u21D2 P = {5, 15, 25, ..., 495}\n\u21D2 Average P = (5 + 495) \xF7 2 = 500 \xF7 2 = 250\n\u21D2 Set Q (Even places): 2nd, 4th, ..., 100th multiples\n\u21D2 Q = {10, 20, 30, ..., 500}\n\u21D2 Average Q = (10 + 500) \xF7 2 = 510 \xF7 2 = 255\n\u21D2 Difference = Q - P = 255 - 250 = 5\n\u2234 The correct answer is 5.\n\nAdditional Information\nAverage of Arithmetic Progression\n\nFor any sequence in AP, the average is always the middle term or the mean of the first and last terms: (a + l) \xF7 2.\n\nConsecutive Terms Difference\n\nIf every term of set B is 'k' more than the corresponding term of set A, then Average(B) - Average(A) = k.\n\nSum of First n Multiples\n\nSum of first n multiples of 'x' = x \xD7 [n(n+1) \xF7 2]. The average is x \xD7 (n+1) \xF7 2.",
