        correctOption: "B",
        userTime: "01:03",
        avgTime: "01:01",
        question: "The simple interest on a certain sum of money at the rate of 5% per annum for 9 years is \u20B91,260. At what rate of interest can the same amount of interest be received on the same sum after 6 years?",
        options: {
          a: "8.0%",
          b: "7.5%",
          c: "8.5%",
          d: "6.5%"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\nShortcut Trick\nSince the Principal (P) and Simple Interest (I) are the same in both cases, the product of Rate (R) and Time (T) must be constant.\n\n\u21D2 R1 \xD7 T1 = R2 \xD7 T2\n\u21D2 5 \xD7 9 = R2 \xD7 6\n\u21D2 45 = 6 \xD7 R2\n\u21D2 R2 = 45 \xF7 6 = 7.5%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 7.5%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nCase 1: Interest (I) = \u20B91,260, Rate (R1) = 5%, Time (T1) = 9 years\nCase 2: Interest (I) = \u20B91,260, Time (T2) = 6 years, Principal (P) is same as Case 1.\n\nFormula Used:\nSimple Interest (I) = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) \xF7 100\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 1,260 = (P \xD7 5 \xD7 9) \xF7 100\n\u21D2 1,260 = (P \xD7 45) \xF7 100\n\u21D2 P = (1,260 \xD7 100) \xF7 45\n\u21D2 P = 28 \xD7 100 = \u20B92,800\nNow, using this Principal for the second case:\n\u21D2 1,260 = (2,800 \xD7 R2 \xD7 6) \xF7 100\n\u21D2 1,260 = 28 \xD7 R2 \xD7 6\n\u21D2 1,260 = 168 \xD7 R2\n\u21D2 R2 = 1,260 \xF7 168\n\u21D2 R2 = 7.5%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 7.5%.\n\nAdditional Information\nSimple Interest Property\n\nIf the simple interest and principal remain constant, the rate of interest is inversely proportional to the time period (R \u221D 1/T).\n\nTotal Amount in SI\nThe total amount (A) is the sum of Principal and Interest: A = P + (P \xD7 R \xD7 T)/100 = P(1 + RT/100).\n\nSI vs CI Difference\n\nSimple interest is calculated only on the principal, whereas compound interest is calculated on the principal plus the accumulated interest of previous periods.",
        topic: "Simple Interest",
