        userTime: "00:25",
        avgTime: "00:17",
        question: "The average output of n machines is k units per day. If one of the machines is excluded, whose output is 780 units per day, the average output will decrease by 30 units. Instead, if one dead machine is included, the average output will decrease by 90 units. What is the total output of n machines?",
        options: {
          a: "4020",
          b: "3900",
          c: "3640",
          d: "3780"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nCase 1: Removal of 780 units machine reduces average by 30 \u21D2 780 - k = 30(n - 1)\nCase 2: Addition of 0 units machine reduces average by 90 \u21D2 k - 0 = 90(n + 1)\nSubstitute k = 90n + 90 in first equation: 780 - (90n + 90) = 30n - 30\n690 - 90n = 30n - 30 \u21D2 120n = 720 \u21D2 n = 6\nAverage k = 90(6 + 1) = 630 units. Total output = 6 \xD7 630 = 3780 units.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3780 units.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nNumber of machines = n | Average output = k units/day\nValue of machine excluded = 780 units | Value of machine included = 0 units\n\nFormula Used:\nTotal Output = Number of Machines \xD7 Average Output\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Total initial output = n \xD7 k\n\u21D2 When 780 is excluded: (nk - 780) \xF7 (n - 1) = k - 30\n\u21D2 nk - 780 = (n - 1)(k - 30) = nk - 30n - k + 30\n\u21D2 30n + k = 810 ... (Eq. 1)\n\u21D2 When a dead machine (0 units) is included: (nk + 0) \xF7 (n + 1) = k - 90\n\u21D2 nk = (n + 1)(k - 90) = nk - 90n + k - 90\n\u21D2 k - 90n = 90 \u21D2 k = 90n + 90 ... (Eq. 2)\n\u21D2 Substituting (Eq. 2) in (Eq. 1): 30n + (90n + 90) = 810\n\u21D2 120n = 720 \u21D2 n = 6\n\u21D2 k = 90(6) + 90 = 630\n\u21D2 Total Output = nk = 6 \xD7 630 = 3780 units\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3780 units.\n\nAdditional Information\nNet Deviation Principle\n\nIn any average problem, the sum of deviations from the average is always zero. If an item is added or removed, the total change in the sum equals the deviation of that item from the original average.\n\nImpact of Adding Zero\n\nWhen a zero - value item is added to a set, the total sum remains constant, but the average decreases because the denominator (count) increases: New Avg = Old Sum \xF7 (n + 1).\n\nAverage Property\n\nIf every element in a set is increased or decreased by a constant value 'x', the average also increases or decreases by 'x' respectively.",
