        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:01",
        avgTime: "00:12",
        question: "Suhas mistakenly took as dividend a number which was 10% less than the original dividend. He also mistakenly took as divisor a number which was 20% less than the original divisor. If the correct quotient of the original question of division was 24 and the remainder was 0, then what quotient did Suhas obtain, assuming there was no error in his calculations?",
        options: {
          a: "27",
          b: "21.6",
          c: "26.4",
          d: "30"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nNew Quotient = (New Dividend % \xF7 New Divisor %) \xD7 Original Quotient\nNew Quotient = [(100% - 10%) \xF7 (100% - 20%)] \xD7 24\nNew Quotient = (90% \xF7 80%) \xD7 24\nNew Quotient = (9 \xF7 8) \xD7 24 = 27\n\u2234 The correct answer is 27.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nOriginal Quotient = 24\nOriginal Remainder = 0\nNew Dividend is 10% less than the Original Dividend.\nNew Divisor is 20% less than the Original Divisor.\n\nFormula Used:\nDividend = (Divisor \xD7 Quotient) + Remainder\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the Original Dividend be D and the Original Divisor be d.\n\u21D2 D = (d \xD7 24) + 0\n\u21D2 D \xF7 d = 24\n\u21D2 New Dividend = D - (10% of D) = 0.9D\n\u21D2 New Divisor = d - (20% of d) = 0.8d\n\u21D2 New Quotient = New Dividend \xF7 New Divisor\n\u21D2 New Quotient = 0.9D \xF7 0.8d\n\u21D2 New Quotient = (0.9 \xF7 0.8) \xD7 (D \xF7 d)\n\u21D2 Substitute D \xF7 d = 24:\n\u21D2 New Quotient = (9 \xF7 8) \xD7 24\n\u21D2 New Quotient = 9 \xD7 3 = 27\n\u2234 The correct answer is 27.\n\nAdditional Information\nDivision Algorithm\n\nThe foundational rule of division states that Dividend = (Divisor \xD7 Quotient) + Remainder. When the remainder is exactly zero, the dividend is a perfect multiple of the divisor.\n\nPercentage Change in Fractions\n\nIf the numerator of a fraction changes by x% and the denominator changes by y%, the resulting fraction becomes [(100 \xB1 x) \xF7 (100 \xB1 y)] \xD7 Original Fraction.\n\nBase Change\n\nWhen any given quantity is decreased by R%, the remaining quantity is computed directly as (100 - R)% of its original value. For example, a 10% decrease perfectly leaves 90% of the original value.",
