        userTime: "00:03",
        avgTime: "01:02",
        question: "A certain sum amounts to Rs. 4,900 in 3 years 4 months at the rate of 7.5% p.a. at simple interest. What will be the amount (in Rs.) of the same sum in 3 3/4 years at the rate of 8% p.a. simple interest?",
        options: {
          a: "5,147",
          b: "5,048",
          c: "4,956",
          d: "5,096"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nEffective interest in 1st case = 7.5% \xD7 (10/3) = 25% \u2192 Amount = 125% of Principal.\nEffective interest in 2nd case = 8% \xD7 (15/4) = 30% \u2192 Amount = 130% of Principal.\nSince 125% of Principal = \u20B94900\n130% of Principal = (4900 \xF7 125) \xD7 130 = 39.2 \xD7 130 = \u20B95096.\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B95096.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nAmount in 1st case (A1) = \u20B94900\nRate (R1) = 7.5% p.a., Time (T1) = 3 years 4 months = 3 + 1/3 = 10/3 years\nRate (R\xB2) = 8% p.a., Time (T\xB2) = 3 + 3/4 = 15/4 years\n\nFormula Used:\nSimple Interest (SI) = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) \xF7 100\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 In the 1st case, SI1% = 7.5 \xD7 (10/3) = 25%\n\u21D2 Amount (A1) = 100% + 25% = 125% of P\n\u21D2 125% of P = \u20B94900\n\u21D2 P = (4900 \xD7 100) \xF7 125 = \u20B93920\n\u21D2 In the 2nd case, SI2% = 8 \xD7 (15/4) = 30%\n\u21D2 Amount (A\xB2) = 100% + 30% = 130% of P\n\u21D2 A\xB2 = 130% of 3920 = (130 \xF7 100) \xD7 3920\n\u21D2 A\xB2 = 1.3 \xD7 3920 = \u20B95096\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B95096.\n\nAdditional Information\nEffective Interest Rate in SI\n\nIn Simple Interest, the effective interest percentage for a given period is simply the product of the rate and time (R \xD7 T)%. This allows for direct calculation without finding the principal every time.\n\nAmount Formula for Simple Interest\n\nAmount (A) = P + SI = P(1 + RT/100). The principal remains constant throughout the entire period, unlike compound interest where interest is added to the principal.\n\nSimple vs Compound Interest\n\nFor the first year, both simple and compound interests are identical (if compounded annually). From the second year onwards, CI will be greater than SI because interest is earned on accumulated interest.",
