        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTriangles sharing the same vertex and having their bases on the same straight line have areas proportional to their base lengths.\n\nFrom the diagram dimensions, the ratio of the base segment SR to the total base PR is 5 : 6.\n\nArea of \u0394 SQR = (SR \xF7 PR) \xD7 Area of \u0394 PQR\nArea of \u0394 SQR = (5 \xF7 6) \xD7 36 = 30\n\u2234 The correct answer is 30.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nArea of \u0394 PQR = 36\n\nPoint S lies on the side PR of \u0394 PQR.\n\nBased on the given figure, the ratio of the base length SR to the total base PR is 5 : 6.\n\nConcept Used: When two triangles share the same perpendicular height, the ratio of their areas is equal to the ratio of their respective bases.\n\nh / P\n\nQ\n\nRs. 1x\n\n5x\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Both \u0394 PQR and \u0394 SQR share the same topmost vertex Q, and their bases PR and SR lie strictly on the exact same straight line.\n\u21D2 Therefore, they have the exact same perpendicular height (h) drawn from Q to the line base PR.\n\u21D2 Area of \u0394 PQR = 1/2 \xD7 Base PR \xD7 Height h = 36\n\u21D2 Area of \u0394 SQR = 1/2 \xD7 Base SR \xD7 Height h\n\u21D2 Ratio of areas = Area of \u0394 SQR \xF7 Area of \u0394 PQR = SR \xF7 PR\n\u21D2 Area of \u0394 SQR \xF7 36 = 5 \xF7 6\n\u21D2 Area of \u0394 SQR = 36 \xD7 (5 \xF7 6)\n\u21D2 Area of \u0394 SQR = 6 \xD7 5 = 30\n\u2234 The correct answer is 30.\n\nAdditional Information\nArea of Triangles with Same Vertex\n\nWhen two or more triangles share a common vertex and their bases lie on the same straight line, the ratio of their areas is exactly proportional to the ratio of their base lengths because their perpendicular height remains identical.\n\nSimilar Triangles Property\n\nIf two triangles are entirely similar, the ratio of their enclosed areas is equal to the square of the ratio of their corresponding sides: Area1 \xF7 Area\xB2 = (Side1 \xF7 Side\xB2)2.\n\nMedian of a Triangle\n\nA median divides a triangle into two smaller inner triangles of perfectly equal area because it naturally bisects the main base into a 1:1 ratio while sharing the exact same vertex height.",
