        userTime: "00:04",
        avgTime: "00:31",
        question: "A field roller, in the shape of a cylinder, has a diameter of 1 m and length of 1 1/4 m (i.e. 5/4 m). If the speed at which the roller rolls is 14 revolutions per minute, then the maximum area (in m\xB2) that rolls in 1 hour is:",
        options: {
          a: "3560",
          b: "3300",
          c: "3600",
          d: "3960"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nArea rolled in 1 revolution = Curved Surface Area = \u03C0dh\nArea per revolution = 22/7 \xD7 1 \xD7 5/4 = 55/14 m\xB2\nTotal revolutions in 1 hour (60 min) = 14 \xD7 60 = 840\nTotal area = Total revolutions \xD7 Area per revolution\nTotal area = 840 \xD7 (55/14) = 60 \xD7 55 = 3300 m\xB2\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3300 m\xB2.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nDiameter of the roller (d) = 1 m\nLength of the roller (h) = 1 1/4 m = 5/4 m\nSpeed = 14 revolutions per minute\nTime = 1 hour = 60 minutes\n\nFormula Used:\nCurved Surface Area (CSA) of a cylinder = \u03C0 \xD7 d \xD7 h\nTotal Area Rolled = Total Revolutions \xD7 CSA\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Total revolutions in 1 hour = 14 revolutions/minute \xD7 60 minutes = 840 revolutions\n\u21D2 Area covered in 1 revolution (CSA) = (22/7) \xD7 1 \xD7 (5/4)\n\u21D2 Area covered in 1 revolution = 110/28 = 55/14 m\xB2\n\u21D2 Total area rolled in 1 hour = 840 \xD7 (55/14)\n\u21D2 Total area = (840 \xF7 14) \xD7 55\n\u21D2 Total area = 60 \xD7 55\n\u21D2 Total area = 3300 m\xB2\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3300 m\xB2.\n\nAdditional Information\nCylinder Properties\n\nVolume = \u03C0r2h | Total Surface Area = 2\u03C0r(r + h) | Curved Surface Area = 2\u03C0rh or \u03C0dh\n\nRevolutions and Rolled Area\n\nWhen a cylindrical roller rolls on the ground, the flat area covered in one full revolution is exactly equal to its Curved Surface Area (CSA).\n\nRevolutions and Linear Distance\n\nIf a wheel or roller moves forward, the linear distance covered in one revolution is equal to the circumference of its circular base (2\u03C0r or \u03C0d).",
        topic: "Mensuration 3D",
        subtopic: "Cylinder - Curved Surface Area and Revolutions",
