        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "01:30",
        avgTime: "00:19",
        question: "The simple interest on a certain sum for $4 \\frac{1}{2}$ years at 10% p.a. is 60% of the compound interest on a sum of Rs. 12,000 for 1 year at 10% p.a, interest compounded half yearly. The sum on which the simple interest is calculated is:",
        options: {
          a: "Rs. 1,640",
          b: "Rs. 2,450",
          c: "Rs. 1,680",
          d: "Rs. 2,000"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nEffective rate for CI at 10% p.a. compounded half - yearly for 1 year = 5% + 5% + (5 \xD7 5)/100 = 10.25%\n\nTotal Compound Interest = 10.25% of 12000 = Rs. 1230\nGiven Simple Interest = 60% of CI = 0.60 \xD7 1230 = Rs. 738\nUsing SI = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) / 100 \u21D2 P \xD7 10 \xD7 4.5 / 100 = 738\n\nP \xD7 0.45 = 738 \u21D2 P = 1640\n\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 1640.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nCI Principal = Rs. 12000\nCI Rate = 10% p.a. (5% per half - year), Time = 1 year (2 half - years)\nSI Rate = 10% p.a., Time = 4.5 years (9/2 years)\n\nCompound Interest (Half - Yearly)\n\nP = Rs. 12000 | R = 10% p.a. (5% per half - yr) | T = 1 yr\nEffective Rate = 10.25% \u2192 CI = Rs. 1230\n\nSimple Interest\n\nP = ? | R = 10% p.a. | T = 4.5 yrs\nSI = 60% of CI = Rs. 738\n\nFormula Used:\nCI = P \xD7 [(1 + R/100)T - 1]\nSI = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) / 100\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Amount after 1 year (CI) = 12000 \xD7 (1 + 5/100)\xB2\n\u21D2 Amount = 12000 \xD7 (1.05)\xB2\n\u21D2 Amount = 12000 \xD7 1.1025 = Rs. 13230\n\u21D2 CI = 13230 - 12000 = Rs. 1230\n\u21D2 Given that SI is 60% of CI:\n\u21D2 SI = (60/100) \xD7 1230 = Rs. 738\n\u21D2 Now, substitute SI value in formula:\n\u21D2 738 = (Principal \xD7 10 \xD7 4.5) / 100\n\u21D2 738 = Principal \xD7 45 / 100\n\u21D2 738 = Principal \xD7 0.45\n\u21D2 Principal = 738 / 0.45 = 1640\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 1640.\n\nAdditional Information\nHalf - Yearly Compound Interest\n\nWhen interest is compounded half - yearly, the rate is halved (R/2) and the time periods are doubled (2T). Amount = P(1 + R/200)\xB2T.\n\nEffective Rate Formula\n\nFor two successive periods with the same rate R%, the effective rate can be found directly using: R + R + (R\xB2/100)%.\n\nSimple vs Compound Interest\n\nSimple interest is calculated only on the principal amount uniformly across all years, while compound interest is calculated on both principal and the accumulated interest of previous periods.",
