        userTime: "00:45",
        avgTime: "00:20",
        question: "If $\\tan A + \\sin A = p$ and $\\tan A - \\sin A = q$, then which of the following is true?",
        options: {
          a: "$p^2 + q^2 = 4\\sqrt{pq}$",
          b: "$p + q = pq$",
          c: "$p - q = pq$",
          d: "$p^2 - q^2 = 4\\sqrt{pq}$",
          A: "$p^2 + q^2 = 4\\sqrt{pq}$",
          B: "$p + q = pq$",
          C: "$p - q = pq$",
          D: "$p^2 - q^2 = 4\\sqrt{pq}$"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nPut A = 45\xB0. Then tan(45\xB0) = 1 and sin(45\xB0) = 1/\u221A2.\np = 1 + 1/\u221A2 and q = 1 - 1/\u221A2\np\xB2 - q\xB2 = (p + q) \xD7 (p - q) = (2) \xD7 (2/\u221A2) = 2\u221A2\npq = (1 + 1/\u221A2) \xD7 (1 - 1/\u221A2) = 1 - 1/2 = 1 2/4\u221A(pq) = 4 \xD7 \u221A(1/2) = 4/\u221A2 = 2\u221A2\n\u2234 The correct answer is p\xB2 - q\xB2 = 4\u221A(pq).\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\np = tanA + sinA\nq = tanA - sinA\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 p + q = 2tanA\n\u21D2 p - q = 2sinA\n\u21D2 p\xB2 - q\xB2 = (p + q) \xD7 (p - q) = (2tanA) \xD7 (2sinA) = 4tanA \xD7 sinA\nNow, let us calculate the product pq:\n\u21D2 pq = (tanA + sinA) \xD7 (tanA - sinA)\n\u21D2 pq = tan\xB2A - sin\xB2A\n\u21D2 pq = (sin\xB2A \xF7 cos\xB2A) - sin\xB2A\n\u21D2 pq = sin\xB2A \xD7 (1/cos\xB2A - 1)\n\u21D2 pq = sin\xB2A \xD7 (sec\xB2A - 1)\n\u21D2 pq = sin\xB2A \xD7 tan\xB2A\n\u21D2 \u221A(pq) = sinA \xD7 tanA\n\nMultiplying by 4 on both sides:\n\n\u21D2 4\u221A(pq) = 4tanA \xD7 sinA\n\nComparing the values of (p\xB2 - q\xB2) and 4\u221A(pq):\n\n\u21D2 p\xB2 - q\xB2 = 4\u221A(pq)\n\u2234 The correct answer is p\xB2 - q\xB2 = 4\u221A(pq).\n\nAdditional Information\nTrigonometric Identities\n\nThe three fundamental identities are: sin\xB2\u03B8 + cos\xB2\u03B8 = 1, sec\xB2\u03B8 - tan\xB2\u03B8 = 1, and cosec\xB2\u03B8 - cot\xB2\u03B8 = 1.\nDifference of Squares\nThe algebraic identity a\xB2 - b\xB2 = (a - b) \xD7 (a + b) helps simplify quadratic differences rapidly.\n\nTangent Formula\n\nThe relationship tan\u03B8 = sin\u03B8 \xF7 cos\u03B8 is useful for converting all terms into sine and cosine to simplify complex equations.",
        topic: "Trigonometry",
