        testName: "Quantitative Aptitude Sectional Test - 10",
        platform: "Testbook",
        status: "Unattempted",
        chosenOption: "Unattempted",
        correctOption: "D",
        userTime: "01:27",
        avgTime: "01:14",
        question: "A and B invested their money in a business in the ratio of 9 \u2236 5. If 10% of the total profit goes for charity and A\u2019s share is Rs. 29,840, what is the total profit (in Rs., to the nearest integer)?",
        options: {
          a: "Rs. 51,745",
          b: "Rs. 55,715",
          c: "Rs. 57,545",
          d: "Rs. 51,575"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTotal distributed profit = Total profit \xD7 90%.\nA's share = Total profit \xD7 (90 / 100) \xD7 [9 / (9 + 5)].\n29840 = Total profit \xD7 (9 / 10) \xD7 (9 / 14).\nTotal profit = 29840 \xD7 (140 / 81) = 4177600 / 81 = 51575.30...\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 51, 575.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nInvestment ratio of A and B = 9 : 5\nPercentage of profit going to charity = 10%\nA's profit share = Rs. 29, 840\n\nFormula Used:\nProfit ratio = Investment ratio (when the time period is identical)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the total profit be P.\n\u21D2 Profit distributed between A and B = P - 10% of P = 90% of P = 0.9P.\n\u21D2 Investment ratio of A and B = 9 : 5\n\u21D2 Total ratio parts = 9 + 5 = 14 parts.\n\u21D2 A's profit share = (9 / 14) of the remaining profit\n\u21D2 29840 = (9 / 14) \xD7 0.9P\n\u21D2 29840 = (8.1 / 14) \xD7 P\n\u21D2 P = (29840 \xD7 14) / 8.1\n\u21D2 P = 417760 / 8.1 = 4177600 / 81\n\u21D2 P = 51575.308...\n\u21D2 Rounding to the nearest integer, Total Profit \u2248 Rs. 51, 575\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 51, 575.\n\nAdditional Information\nSleeping Partner\n\nA partner who only invests money but does not take part in the management of the business is known as a sleeping partner. Active partners receive a fixed salary or a certain percentage of the profit for managing the business before the remaining profit is distributed among all partners.\n\nCompound Partnership\n\nWhen investments are made for different time periods, it is called a compound partnership. The profit share ratio is the product of capital invested and the time period of investment (P1 : P\xB2 = C1 \xD7 T1 : C\xB2 \xD7 T\xB2).\n\nJoining Mid - way\n\nIf a partner joins x months after the start of a financial year, their investment time period for that year is (12 - x) months, which must be multiplied by their capital to determine their final profit share ratio compared to the primary investors.",
