        testName: "Quantitative Aptitude Sectional Test - 8",
        platform: "Testbook",
        status: "Unattempted",
        chosenOption: "Unattempted",
        correctOption: "B",
        userTime: "00:20",
        avgTime: "01:01",
        question: "Manish bought two watches for Rs. 1056. He sold one at a loss of 25 percent and other at a profit of 25 percent. If each watch was sold for the same price, then what is the cost price of the watch which was sold at loss?",
        options: {
          a: "Rs. 720",
          b: "Rs. 660",
          c: "Rs. 560",
          d: "Rs. 600"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nWhen Selling Prices are equal, the ratio of Cost Prices is inversely proportional to their respective Selling Price percentages.\n\nCP1 (at 25% loss) : CP\xB2 (at 25% profit) = (100 + 25) : (100 - 25)\nCP1 : CP\xB2 = 125 : 75 = 5 : 3\nTotal ratio parts = 5 + 3 = 8 parts \u2192 Rs. 1056\nCP of watch sold at loss (CP1) = (1056 \xF7 8) \xD7 5 = 132 \xD7 5 = 660\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 660.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTotal Cost Price = Rs. 1056\nLoss percentage on the first watch = 25%\nProfit percentage on the second watch = 25%\nSelling Price of both watches is the same.\n\nFormula Used:\nSelling Price (SP) = CP \xD7 (100 \xB1 Profit/Loss)%\n\nCalculation:\n\u21D2 Let the cost price of the first watch be CP1 and the second watch be CP\xB2.\n\u21D2 Selling price of the first watch (SP1) = CP1 \xD7 (100 - 25)% = 0.75 \xD7 CP1\n\u21D2 Selling price of the second watch (SP\xB2) = CP\xB2 \xD7 (100 + 25)% = 1.25 \xD7 CP\xB2\n\u21D2 Since SP1 = SP\xB2\n\u21D2 0.75 \xD7 CP1 = 1.25 \xD7 CP\xB2\n\u21D2 CP1 \xF7 CP\xB2 = 1.25 \xF7 0.75 = 125 \xF7 75 = 5 \xF7 3\n\u21D2 Let CP1 = 5x and CP\xB2 = 3x\n\u21D2 Total CP = 5x + 3x = 8x\n\u21D2 8x = 1056\n\u21D2 x = 1056 \xF7 8 = 132\n\u21D2 Cost price of the watch sold at a loss = 5x = 5 \xD7 132 = 660\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 660.\n\nAdditional Information\nOverall Loss Concept (Equal SP)\n\nIf two items are sold at the same price, one at a profit of x% and the other at a loss of x%, there is always an overall loss. Loss % = (x \xF7 10)2 %.\n\nRatio of Cost Prices (Equal SP)\n\nWhen Selling Prices are equal, the Cost Prices are inversely proportional to their respective Selling Price percentage values: CP1 : CP\xB2 = (100 \xB1 x\xB2) : (100 \xB1 x1).\n\nNo Profit No Loss Concept (Equal CP)\n\nIf two articles are bought for the same Cost Price and sold at x% profit and x% loss respectively, there is neither profit nor loss in the overall transaction.",
        topic: "Profit, Loss & Discount",
        subtopic: "Profit & Loss - Equal Selling Price",
