        question: "The total salary of A and B is Rs. 1215. A spent $85 \\frac{5}{7}$\\% of his income and B spent $83 \\frac{1}{3}$\\% of his income. The ratio of saving is 3 : 4. Find their individual salary.",
        options: {
          a: "Rs. 567, Rs. 648",
          b: "Rs. 597, Rs. 618",
          c: "Rs. 578, Rs. 637",
          d: "Rs. 689, Rs. 526"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nA's savings % = 100% - 85(5/7)% = 100% - 600/7% = 100/7% = 1/7 of A's salary.\nB's savings % = 100% - 83(1/3)% = 100% - 250/3% = 50/3% = 1/6 of B's salary.\nRatio of savings \u21D2 A/7 : B/6 = 3 : 4 \u21D2 6A / 7B = 3 / 4 \u21D2 A : B = 7 : 8\nTotal salary = 7 + 8 = 15 units = 1215 \u21D2 1 unit = 81\nA's salary = 7 \xD7 81 = 567 and B's salary = 8 \xD7 81 = 648.\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 567, Rs. 648.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTotal salary of A and B = Rs. 1215\nA's expenditure = 85(5/7)% and B's expenditure = 83(1/3)%\nRatio of their savings = 3 : 4\n\nFormula Used:\nSavings = Income \xD7 (100% - Expenditure%)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 A's savings = 100% - 85(5/7)% = (100 - 600/7)% = 100/7% = 1/7\n\u21D2 B's savings = 100% - 83(1/3)% = (100 - 250/3)% = 50/3% = 1/6\n\u21D2 Ratio of savings = (1/7 \xD7 A) / (1/6 \xD7 B) = 3 / 4\n\u21D2 (6 \xD7 A) / (7 \xD7 B) = 3 / 4\n\u21D2 A / B = (3 \xD7 7) / (4 \xD7 6) = 21 / 24 = 7 / 8\n\u21D2 Total salary ratio = 7 + 8 = 15 parts\n\u21D2 15 parts = Rs. 1215\n\u21D2 1 part = 1215 \xF7 15 = Rs. 81\n\u21D2 A's salary = 7 \xD7 81 = Rs. 567\n\u21D2 B's salary = 8 \xD7 81 = Rs. 648\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 567, Rs. 648.\n\nAdditional Information\nPercentage to Fraction Conversion\n\nConverting complex percentages into fractions saves calculation time. For instance, 14(2/7)% = 1/7 and 16(2/3)% = 1/6. By extending this, 85(5/7)% directly converts to 6/7, and 83(1/3)% translates to 5/6.\n\nIncome, Expenditure & Savings Relation\n\nAlways apply the basic equation: Income = Expenditure + Savings. If values are presented in percentages, you can subtract from 100% to directly fetch the missing entity.\n\nRatio Distribution\n\nWhen any total quantity is distributed among entities in the ratio m : n, their respective shares are given by (m / (m + n)) \xD7 Total and (n / (m + n)) \xD7 Total.",
