        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nSince both persons meet in 1 hour after covering 8 km together, sum of their speeds = 8 \xF7 1 = 8 km/hr.\n\nChecking the options for the faster man's speed, let's test Option 4: 5 km/hr.\n\nIf the faster speed = 5 km/hr, the slower speed = 8 - 5 = 3 km/hr.\nTime difference = (8 \xF7 3) - (8 \xF7 5) = (40 - 24) \xF7 15 = 16/15 hours.\n\nThis matches the given condition perfectly, meaning our assumed speed is correct.\n\n\u2234 The correct answer is 5 km/hr.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nDistance between A and B = 8 km\nMeeting time = 1 hour\nDifference in time to complete the journey = 16/15 hours\n\nFormula Used:\nTime = Distance \xF7 Speed\nRelative Speed (opposite direction) = Speed of 1st Person + Speed of 2nd Person\n\nCalculation:\n\u21D2 Sum of their speeds = Distance \xF7 Meeting Time = 8 \xF7 1 = 8 km/hr\n\u21D2 Let the speed of the 1st person (faster) be v1 and the 2nd person be v\xB2.\n\u21D2 v1 + v\xB2 = 8 \u2192 v\xB2 = 8 - v1\n\u21D2 The 1st person reaches earlier, so the difference in time is T\xB2 - T1 = 16/15\n\u21D2 (8 \xF7 v\xB2) - (8 \xF7 v1) = 16/15\n\u21D2 (1 \xF7 v\xB2) - (1 \xF7 v1) = 2/15\n\u21D2 (v1 - v\xB2) \xF7 (v1 \xD7 v\xB2) = 2/15\n\u21D2 (v1 - (8 - v1)) \xF7 (v1 \xD7 (8 - v1)) = 2/15\n\u21D2 (2v1 - 8) \xF7 (8v1 - v12) = 2/15\n\u21D2 (v1 - 4) \xF7 (8v1 - v12) = 1/15\n\u21D2 15v1 - 60 = 8v1 - v12\n\u21D2 v12 + 7v1 - 60 = 0\n\u21D2 (v1 + 12)(v1 - 5) = 0\n\u21D2 Since speed cannot be negative, v1 = 5 km/hr\n\u2234 The correct answer is 5 km/hr.\n\nAdditional Information\nRelative Speed\n\nWhen two bodies move towards each other, their relative speed is the sum of their individual speeds (v1 + v\xB2). When they move in the same direction, it is the difference of their speeds (v1 - v\xB2).\n\nSpeed, Distance & Time Relationship\nSpeed = Distance \xF7 Time. There is an inverse relationship between Speed and Time when Distance is constant: Ratio of Speeds = Inverse Ratio of Times.\n\nQuadratic Formula\n\nFor an equation ax\xB2 + bx + c = 0, roots are given by (-b \xB1 \u221A(b\xB2 - 4ac)) \xF7 2a. This helps solve non - factorizable speed equations efficiently.\n\nAverage Speed\n\nWhen traveling equal distances at different speeds x and y, the Average Speed over the entire journey is calculated as (2 \xD7 x \xD7 y) \xF7 (x + y).",
