        status: "Unattempted",
        chosenOption: "Unattempted",
        correctOption: "B",
        userTime: "00:56",
        avgTime: "00:27",
        question: "A and B together can do a piece of work in 12 days, B and C together in 15 days and C and A together in 20 days. In how many days will all three of them together complete the work?",
        options: {
          a: "5",
          b: "10",
          c: "$15 \\frac{1}{2}$",
          d: "$18 \\frac{1}{3}$"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTotal work = LCM of 12, 15 and 20 = 60 units.\nCombined efficiency of 2 \xD7 (A + B + C) = (60 \xF7 12) + (60 \xF7 15) + (60 \xF7 20) = 5 + 4 + 3 = 12 units/day.\nEfficiency of (A + B + C) together = 12 \xF7 2 = 6 units/day.\nTime taken by all three together = Total work \xF7 Efficiency = 60 \xF7 6 = 10 days.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10 days.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTime taken by A and B together = 12 days\nTime taken by B and C together = 15 days\nTime taken by C and A together = 20 days\nConcept Used: Total Work = LCM of individual time taken\nEfficiency = Total Work \xF7 Time Taken\n\nCalculations:\nPerson Days Total Work Efficiency\n\nA + B 12 60 5\n\nB + C 15 4\n\nC + A 20 3\n\n\u21D2 Sum of efficiencies: (A + B) + (B + C) + (C + A) = 5 + 4 + 3\n\u21D2 2 \xD7 (A + B + C) = 12\n\u21D2 Efficiency of (A + B + C) = 12 \xF7 2 = 6 units/day\n\u21D2 Total time taken by (A + B + C) = Total Work \xF7 Total Efficiency\n\u21D2 Time taken = 60 \xF7 6 = 10 days\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nIndividual Completion Time\n\nTo find individual time taken, simply subtract the combined efficiency of the other two from the total efficiency. Example: A's efficiency = (A + B + C) - (B + C) = 6 - 4 = 2 units/day. Thus, A alone will finish the work in 60 \xF7 2 = 30 days.\n\nWork and Wages\n\nWhen multiple people work together to finish a piece of work, their total earnings are divided strictly in the ratio of their respective efficiencies or the total amount of work done by each.\n\nPipes and Cisterns\n\nThe LCM efficiency concept directly applies to pipes filling or emptying a tank. Inlet pipes carry a positive efficiency value, while outlet (leak/emptying) pipes carry a negative efficiency value.",
