        userTime: "00:00",
        avgTime: "00:19",
        question: "The distance between two station A and B is 1000 km. A train starts from A and moves towards B at an average speed of 60 km/hr. Another train starts from B, 2 hours later than the train from A, and moves towards A at an average speed of 50 km/hr. When will the two trains cross each other after A starts journey?",
        options: {
          a: "12 hours",
          b: "10 hours",
          c: "8.5 hours",
          d: "11.5 hours"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nDistance covered by train A in the first 2 hours = 60 \xD7 2 = 120 km.\nRemaining distance between the two trains = 1000 - 120 = 880 km.\nRelative speed when moving towards each other = 60 + 50 = 110 km/hr.\nTime taken to cross each other after train B starts = 880 \xF7 110 = 8 hours.\nTotal time after train A starts its journey = 2 + 8 = 10 hours.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10 hours.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nDistance between station A and B = 1000 km.\nSpeed of train A = 60 km/hr.\nSpeed of train B = 50 km/hr.\n\nTrain B starts 2 hours later than train A.\n\nFormula Used:\nDistance = Speed \xD7 Time\n\nCalculation:\nLet the two trains cross each other t hours after train A starts its journey.\n\u21D2 Distance covered by train A in t hours = 60 \xD7 t\n\u21D2 Since train B starts 2 hours later, time traveled by train B = (t - 2) hours.\n\u21D2 Distance covered by train B in (t - 2) hours = 50 \xD7 (t - 2)\n\u21D2 When they meet, the sum of the distances covered by both trains is equal to the total distance.\n\u21D2 60t + 50(t - 2) = 1000\n\u21D2 60t + 50t - 100 = 1000\n\u21D2 110t = 1100\n\u21D2 t = 1100 \xF7 110\n\u21D2 t = 10 hours\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10 hours.\n\nAdditional Information\nRelative Speed\n\nWhen two objects move towards each other, their relative speed is the sum of their individual speeds (S1 + S\xB2).\n\nMeeting Point Equation\n\nWhen two bodies start from opposite ends and meet, the sum of distances covered by them exactly equals the total initial distance between them.\n\nDelayed Starts\n\nIf object B starts 't' hours after object A, their travel times must be adjusted. If A travels for 'x' hours, B travels for (x - t) hours.",
