        avgTime: "00:47",
        question: "A sum of Rs. 2700 amounts to Rs. 8100 in 4 years at a certain rate of compound interest (compounding annually). How much this sum will amounts to after 16 years?",
        options: {
          a: "Rs. 128760",
          b: "Rs. 152760",
          c: "Rs. 218700",
          d: "Rs. 287100"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIn Compound Interest, if a sum becomes a certain multiple in 't' years, it grows by the same multiple over every 't' years.\n\nMultiplying factor in 4 years = 8100 \xF7 2700 = 3.\n\nThe sum becomes 3 times every 4 years.\n\nTime given is 16 years, which consists of four 4 - year periods (16 \xF7 4 = 4).\nTotal amount after 16 years = Principal \xD7 34 = 2700 \xD7 81 = 218700.\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 218700.\n\nAlternate Method\n\xD7 3 \u2192 \xD7 3 \u2192 \xD7 3 \u2192 \xD7 3 \u2192\n\n0 yrs 4 yrs 8 yrs 12 yrs 16 yrs 2700\n\n8100\n\n24300\n\n72900\n\n218700\n\nCompound Interest Progression\n\nGiven:\nPrincipal (P) = Rs. 2700\nAmount after 4 years (A4) = Rs. 8100\nTime (t) = 4 years\n\nFormula Used:\nAmount in Compound Interest: A = P(1 + R/100)n\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 8100 = 2700(1 + R/100)4\n\u21D2 (1 + R/100)4 = 8100 \xF7 2700\n\u21D2 (1 + R/100)4 = 3\n\nWe need to find the amount after 16 years (A16).\n\n\u21D2 A16 = P(1 + R/100)16\n\u21D2 A16 = 2700 \xD7 [(1 + R/100)4]4\n\u21D2 A16 = 2700 \xD7 (3)4\n\u21D2 A16 = 2700 \xD7 81\n\u21D2 A16 = 218700\n\u2234 The correct answer is Rs. 218700.\n\nAdditional Information\nConstant Multiple Rule in CI\n\nIn compound interest, if a sum becomes 'x' times in 't' years, it will become xn times in 'n \xD7 t' years. This happens because interest grows exponentially.\n\nSimple vs Compound Interest\n\nSimple Interest grows linearly as interest is calculated only on the base principal. Compound Interest grows exponentially as interest is calculated on both the principal and previously accumulated interest.\n\nCI Formula\n\nThe interest accumulated over time can be calculated independently using CI = P[(1 + R/100)n - 1].",
