        question: "As observed from the top of a lighthouse 42 m high above sea-level, the angle of depression of a ship sailing directly towards it changes from 30\xB0 to 45\xB0. The distance travelled by the ship during the period of observation is:",
        options: {
          a: "$42(\\sqrt{3} - 1)\\text{ m}$",
          b: "$42(\\sqrt{3} + 1)\\text{ m}$",
          c: "$42\\text{ m}$",
          d: "$42(1 - \\sqrt{3})\\text{ m}$",
          A: "$42(\\sqrt{3} - 1)\\text{ m}$",
          B: "$42(\\sqrt{3} + 1)\\text{ m}$",
          C: "$42\\text{ m}$",
          D: "$42(1 - \\sqrt{3})\\text{ m}$"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIf the angle of elevation (or depression) of a tower of height h changes from \u03B81 to \u03B82 as an object moves directly towards it, the distance travelled is: Distance = h \xD7 (cot \u03B81 - cot \u03B82)\n\nHere, h = 42 m, \u03B81 = 30\xB0, \u03B82 = 45\xB0\nDistance = 42 \xD7 (cot 30\xB0 - cot 45\xB0)\nDistance = 42 \xD7 (\u221A3 - 1)\n\u2234 The correct answer is 42(\u221A3 - 1) m.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nHeight of the lighthouse (AB) = 42 m\nAngle of depression at initial position C = 30\xB0 \u21D2 Angle of elevation \u2220ACB = 30\xB0\nAngle of depression at final position D = 45\xB0 \u21D2 Angle of elevation \u2220ADB = 45\xB0\n\nFormula Used:\nIn a right - angled triangle, tan \u03B8 = Perpendicular \xF7 Base\n\nCalculations:\nIn right - angled triangle \u0394ABD,\n\n\u21D2 tan 45\xB0 = AB \xF7 BD\n\u21D2 1 = 42 \xF7 BD\n\u21D2 BD = 42 m\n\nIn right - angled triangle \u0394ABC,\n\n\u21D2 tan 30\xB0 = AB \xF7 BC\n\u21D2 1 \xF7 \u221A3 = 42 \xF7 BC\n\u21D2 BC = 42\u221A3 m\n\nThe distance travelled by the ship is CD, which is the difference between BC and BD.\n\n\u21D2 Distance CD = BC - BD\n\u21D2 Distance CD = 42\u221A3 - 42\n\u21D2 Distance CD = 42(\u221A3 - 1) m\n\u2234 The correct answer is 42(\u221A3 - 1) m.\n\nAdditional Information\nHeights and Distances (Trigonometry)\n\nThe angle of elevation is the angle formed between the horizontal reference line and the line of sight when looking up at an object. The angle of depression is the angle formed when looking down from a height.\n\nTwo Objects on the Same Side\n\nIf two objects are observed on the ground from a tower of height h at angles \u03B81 and \u03B82, the distance separating them is easily calculated as h(cot \u03B81 - cot \u03B82).\n\nTwo Objects on Opposite Sides\n\nWhen two objects are situated on opposite sides of a central tower of height h, having respective angles of elevation \u03B81 and \u03B82, the total distance between them is h(cot \u03B81 + cot \u03B82).",
