        chosenOption: "Unattempted",
        correctOption: "B",
        userTime: "00:54",
        avgTime: "00:32",
        question: "A train X takes 2 hours less than a train Y to cover a distance of 192 km between two cities. Their average speeds differ by 16 km/hr. How long does the faster train take to cover the journey?",
        options: {
          a: "3 hours",
          b: "4 hours",
          c: "5 hours",
          d: "6 hours"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nFor such questions, the fastest method is checking the options to satisfy the given condition.\n\nLet T be the time taken by the faster train, so the slower train takes (T + 2) hours.\n\nDifference in speeds = 192/T - 192/(T + 2) = 16.\nCheck T = 4 hours from Option 2: Speeds are 192/4 = 48 km/hr and 192/6 = 32 km/hr.\nDifference = 48 - 32 = 16 km/hr, which perfectly satisfies the condition.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 hours.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nDistance between the two cities = 192 km\nTime difference between trains X and Y = 2 hours\nSpeed difference between trains X and Y = 16 km/hr\n\nFormula Used:\nSpeed = Distance \xF7 Time\n\nCalculations:\nLet the time taken by the faster train (Train X) be T hours.\nThen, the time taken by the slower train (Train Y) = (T + 2) hours.\n\u21D2 Speed of the faster train = 192 / T km/hr\n\u21D2 Speed of the slower train = 192 / (T + 2) km/hr\n\nAccording to the given condition, the difference in speeds is 16 km/hr:\n\n\u21D2 192/T - 192/(T + 2) = 16\n\nDividing the entire equation by 16:\n\n\u21D2 12/T - 12/(T + 2) = 1\n\u21D2 [12(T + 2) - 12T] \xF7 [T(T + 2)] = 1\n\u21D2 24 \xF7 (T\xB2 + 2T) = 1\n\u21D2 T\xB2 + 2T = 24\n\u21D2 T\xB2 + 2T - 24 = 0\n\u21D2 T\xB2 + 6T - 4T - 24 = 0\n\u21D2 T(T + 6) - 4(T + 6) = 0\n\u21D2 (T - 4)(T + 6) = 0\nSince time cannot be negative, T = 4 hours.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 hours.\n\nAdditional Information\nRelative Speed\n\nWhen two bodies are moving in the same direction, Relative Speed = |S1 - S2|. When moving in opposite directions, Relative Speed = S1 + S\xB2.\n\nAverage Speed\n\nAverage Speed is the total distance traveled divided by the total time taken. For two equal distances covered at speeds S1 and S\xB2, Average Speed = (2 \xD7 S1 \xD7 S\xB2) \xF7 (S1 + S\xB2).\n\nUnit Conversion\n\nTo convert km/hr to m/s, multiply the speed by 5/18. To convert m/s to km/hr, multiply by 18/5.",
