        correctOption: "B",
        userTime: "00:04",
        avgTime: "00:21",
        question: "When the article sold at the rate of Rs.3753 earned a profit of 11% more than that of the loss incurred when the same article sold at the rate of Rs.1432. Find the Cost price of the article.",
        options: {
          a: "Rs.2325",
          b: "Rs.2532",
          c: "Rs.2523",
          d: "Rs.2352"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nRatio of Loss to Profit = 100 : 111\nTotal difference in SP = 3753 - 1432 = 2321\nSum of ratio parts = 100 + 111 = 211 units\nValue of 1 unit = 2321 \xF7 211 = 11\nLoss = 100 units \xD7 11 = \u20B91100\nCost Price = Lower SP + Loss = 1432 + 1100 = \u20B92532\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B92532.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nSelling Price 1 (SP1) = \u20B93753 (Profit is earned)\nSelling Price 2 (SP\xB2) = \u20B91432 (Loss is incurred)\nProfit at SP1 is 11% more than the loss at SP\xB2.\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the Loss incurred at SP\xB2 be 100x.\n\u21D2 Profit = 100x + 11% of 100x = 111x\n\u21D2 Difference in Selling Prices = SP1 - SP\xB2\n\u21D2 Difference = 3753 - 1432 = 2321\n\u21D2 Also, Difference in Selling Prices = Profit + Loss\n\u21D2 111x + 100x = 2321\n\u21D2 211x = 2321\n\u21D2 x = 2321 \xF7 211 = 11\n\u21D2 Loss = 100x = 100 \xD7 11 = \u20B91100\n\u21D2 Cost Price (CP) = SP\xB2 + Loss\n\u21D2 CP = 1432 + 1100 = \u20B92532\n\u2234 The correct answer is \u20B92532.\n\nAdditional Information\nBasic Profit and Loss Structure\n\nProfit occurs when SP > CP (Profit = SP - CP) and Loss occurs when CP > SP (Loss = CP - SP).\n\nGap/Difference Method\n\nWhenever an article is sold twice producing a profit in one case and a loss in another, the numerical difference between the two selling prices always equals the sum of the profit and the loss amounts.\n\nEqual Profit and Loss Case\n\nIf the profit amount earned is exactly equal to the loss amount incurred across two different selling prices, the Cost Price is exactly the average of the two Selling Prices: CP = (SP1 + SP\xB2) \xF7 2.",
        topic: "Profit, Loss & Discount",
        subtopic: "Profit & Loss - Two Selling Prices & Loss-Profit Relation",
