        userTime: "00:02",
        avgTime: "00:16",
        question: "The average weight of all the students of a class is 43 kg. Four more students whose weights are 43 kg, 36.5 kg, 39.5 kg and 42 kg join the class. Now their average weight becomes 500 grams less than before. Initially the number of students is:",
        options: {
          a: "18",
          b: "22",
          c: "24",
          d: "28"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nUsing Net Deviation: sum of deviations of new students from the initial 43 kg average is: (43 - 43) + (36.5 - 43) + (39.5 - 43) + (42 - 43) = 0 - 6.5 - 3.5 - 1 = -11 kg.\n\nThis total deviation of - 11 kg reduces the overall class average by 0.5 kg per student.\n\nTotal number of students = Absolute Total Deviation \xF7 Decrease in Average = 11 \xF7 0.5 = 22.\nInitial number of students = 22 (total) - 4 (new) = 18.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 18 students.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\n\u21D2 Initial average weight = 43 kg\n\u21D2 Total weight of 4 new students = 43 + 36.5 + 39.5 + 42 = 161 kg\n\u21D2 Decrease in average = 500 g = 0.5 kg\n\nFormula Used:\nQuantity Ratio = (Combined Avg - Avg 2) : (Avg 1 - Combined Avg)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Average weight of 4 new students = 161 \xF7 4 = 40.25 kg\n\u21D2 Combined average of the class = 43 - 0.5 = 42.5 kg\n\u21D2 Applying Alligation (as shown in the diagram):\n\u21D2 Ratio of Initial Students to New Students = (42.5 - 40.25) : (43 - 42.5)\n\u21D2 Ratio = 2.25 : 0.5 = 22.5 : 5 = 9 : 2\n\u21D2 We know the new group has 4 students.\n\u21D2 2 units = 4 students \u21D2 1 unit = 2 students\n\u21D2 Initial number of students = 9 units = 9 \xD7 2 = 18\n\u2234 The correct answer is 18 students.\n\nAdditional Information\nNet Deviation Method\n\nIf new items join a group, the change in the average is equal to the sum of deviations of the new items from the old average, divided by the total number of items in the final combined group.\n\nAlligation Rule\n\nWhen two groups with averages A1 and A\xB2 are mixed to form a combined average Aw, the ratio of their quantities is n1 : n\xB2 = (A\xB2 - Aw) : (Aw - A1).\n\nWeighted Average Formula\n\nThe overall average of multiple groups can also be verified using the standard formula Aw = (n1A1 + n2A\xB2) \xF7 (n1 + n\xB2).",
