        userTime: "00:01",
        avgTime: "00:16",
        question: "A student walks from his house at 5 km/hr and reaches his school 10 minute late. If his speed had been 6 km/hr he would have reached 15 minutes early. Then what is the distance of his school from his house?",
        options: {
          a: "12.5 km",
          b: "25 km",
          c: "37.5 km",
          d: "50 km"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIf distance is constant, Distance = (S1 \xD7 S\xB2) \xF7 (S\xB2 - S1) \xD7 \u0394t\nTime difference \u0394t = 10 min late + 15 min early = 25 min = 25 \xF7 60 hours\nDistance = (5 \xD7 6) \xF7 (6 - 5) \xD7 (25 \xF7 60)\nDistance = 30 \xD7 (5 \xF7 12) = 12.5 km\n\u2234 The correct answer is 12.5 km.\n\nAlternate Method\nDistance from Home to School\n\nHome\n\nSchool\n\nDistance = D km\nSpeed: 5 km/hr | Time: Late by 10 min\nSpeed: 6 km/hr | Time: Early by 15 min\n\nGiven:\nSpeed 1 (S1) = 5 km/hRs. peed 2 (S\xB2) = 6 km/hr\nTime difference (\u0394t) = 10 min late + 15 min early = 25 min = 25 \xF7 60 hr\n\nFormula Used:\nTime = Distance \xF7 Speed\n\nCalculation:\nLet distance be D km.\n\u21D2 Time taken at 5 km/hr = D \xF7 5 hours\n\u21D2 Time taken at 6 km/hr = D \xF7 6 hours\n\u21D2 D \xF7 5 - D \xF7 6 = 25 \xF7 60\n\u21D2 (6D - 5D) \xF7 30 = 5 \xF7 12\n\u21D2 D \xF7 30 = 5 \xF7 12\n\u21D2 D = (5 \xD7 30) \xF7 12\n\u21D2 D = 150 \xF7 12 = 12.5 km\n\u2234 The correct answer is 12.5 km.\n\nAdditional Information\nAverage Speed\n\nWhen a certain distance is covered at speed 'a' and the same distance is covered at speed 'b', the average speed for the whole journey is given by (2 \xD7 a \xD7 b) \xF7 (a + b).\n\nRelative Speed\n\nIf two objects move in the same direction at speeds S1 and S\xB2, their relative speed is |S1 - S2|. If they move in opposite directions, it is (S1 + S\xB2).\n\nTime & Distance Proportionality\n\nIf the distance is constant, speed and time are inversely proportional (S1 \xF7 S\xB2 = T\xB2 \xF7 T1). If speed is constant, distance and time are directly proportional.",
