        question: "The diameter of a metallic sphere is 6 cm. The sphere is melted and drawn into a wire of uniform circular cross-section. If the length of the wire is 36 m, then what is its radius equal to?",
        options: {
          a: "0.1 cm",
          b: "0.01 cm",
          c: "0.001 cm",
          d: "1.0 cm"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nEquate the volumes directly: Volume of Sphere = Volume of cylindrical Wire.\n\u21D2 4/3 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 R\xB3 = \u03C0 \xD7 r\xB2 \xD7 h\n\u21D2 4/3 \xD7 33 = r\xB2 \xD7 3600 (Converting 36 m to 3600 cm)\n\u21D2 36 = r\xB2 \xD7 3600\n\u21D2 r\xB2 = 1/100 \u21D2 r = 0.1 cm\n\u2234 The correct answer is 0.1 cm.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nDiameter of the metallic sphere = 6 cm\nRadius of the sphere (R) = 6 \xF7 2 = 3 cm\nLength of the uniform wire (h) = 36 m\nConverting length to cm \u21D2 h = 36 \xD7 100 = 3600 cm\nLet the radius of the circular cross - section of the wire be r cm.\n\nFormula Used:\nVolume of a Sphere = 4/3 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 R\xB3\nVolume of a Cylinder (Wire) = \u03C0 \xD7 r\xB2 \xD7 h\n\nCalculations:\nWhen an object is melted and recast into another shape, the total volume remains conserved.\n\n\u21D2 Volume of the cylindrical wire = Volume of the metallic sphere\n\u21D2 \u03C0 \xD7 r\xB2 \xD7 h = 4/3 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 R\xB3\n\u21D2 \u03C0 \xD7 r\xB2 \xD7 3600 = 4/3 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 33\n\nCanceling \u03C0 from both sides:\n\n\u21D2 r\xB2 \xD7 3600 = 4/3 \xD7 27\n\u21D2 r\xB2 \xD7 3600 = 4 \xD7 9\n\u21D2 r\xB2 \xD7 3600 = 36\n\u21D2 r\xB2 = 36 \xF7 3600\n\u21D2 r\xB2 = 1 / 100\n\nTaking the square root on both sides:\n\n\u21D2 r = \u221A(1/100)\n\u21D2 r = 1/10 = 0.1 cm\n\u2234 The correct answer is 0.1 cm.\n\nAdditional Information\nPrinciple of Recasting\n\nWhen a 3D solid is melted and transformed into a completely different shape, its volume remains constant while the surface area may change.\n\nVolume of a Sphere\n\nThe total 3D space occupied by a sphere of radius r is calculated using the standard formula V = 4/3 \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 r\xB3.\n\nVolume of a Cylinder\n\nA circular wire is treated geometrically as a solid cylinder. Its volume is V = \u03C0 \xD7 r\xB2 \xD7 h, where r is the radius of the circular base and h is the length or height of the wire.",
