        correctOption: "A",
        userTime: "00:09",
        avgTime: "00:29",
        question: "With a 10% discount on the marked price, a seller earns a 5% profit. If the marked price is increased by 5% and two successive discounts of 5% each are offered, how much percentage profit does the seller earn?",
        options: {
          a: "10.5%",
          b: "10%",
          c: "11%",
          d: "11.5%"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nRatio of Initial MP to CP = (100 + Profit%) \xF7 (100 - Discount%) = 105 \xF7 90 = 7/6\nNew MP = Initial MP \xD7 1.05 = (7/6) \xD7 1.05 = 1.225 times of CP\nMultiplier for two 5% successive discounts = 0.95 \xD7 0.95 = 0.9025\nRatio of New SP to CP = 1.225 \xD7 0.9025 = 1.10556\nNew Profit % = (1.10556 - 1) \xD7 100 = 10.556% \u2248 10.5%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10.5%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\n\u21D2 Initial Discount (D) = 10%\n\u21D2 Initial Profit (P) = 5%\n\u21D2 Increase in Marked Price = 5%\n\u21D2 Successive discounts on new MP = 5% and 5%\n\nFormula Used:\n\u21D2 MP = CP \xD7 (100 + P) \xF7 (100 - D)\n\u21D2 Equivalent Discount = d1 + d\xB2 - (d1 \xD7 d\xB2) \xF7 100\nCP = 100\nMP1 = 116.67 + 5%\nMP\xB2 = 122.5 - 9.75%\nSP\xB2 = 110.56\nProfit = + 10.56%\n\nMarkup\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let Cost Price (CP) = 100\n\u21D2 Initial Selling Price (SP) = 100 + 5% of 100 = 105\n\u21D2 Initial Marked Price (MP) = 105 \xF7 (1 - 0.10) = 105 \xF7 0.90 = 116.67\n\u21D2 New Marked Price = 116.67 \xD7 (1 + 0.05) = 116.67 \xD7 1.05 = 122.5\n\u21D2 Equivalent discount for two 5% discounts = 5 + 5 - (5 \xD7 5) \xF7 100 = 10 - 0.25 = 9.75%\n\u21D2 New Selling Price = 122.5 \xD7 (1 - 0.0975) = 122.5 \xD7 0.9025 = 110.556\n\u21D2 Overall Profit = New SP - CP = 110.556 - 100 = 10.556\n\u21D2 Profit % = 10.556% \u2248 10.5%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10.5%.\n\nAdditional Information\nMarkup and Discount Relation\n\nMP/CP = (100 + Profit%) \xF7 (100 - Discount%). This direct ratio equation is highly useful for directly jumping between Marked Price and Cost Price without calculating the intermediate Selling Price.\n\nSuccessive Discounts\n\nTwo successive discounts of x% and y% are structurally equivalent to a single effective discount of (x + y - xy \xF7 100)%.\n\nBase of Calculations\n\nUnless specified otherwise, Profit and Loss are always evaluated on the Cost Price (CP), whereas Discounts are strictly evaluated on the Marked Price (MP).",
        topic: "Profit, Loss & Discount",
        subtopic: "Markup, Successive Discounts & Profit Percentage",
