        userTime: "00:07",
        avgTime: "00:48",
        question: "A 10 feet long ladder leaning against a wall, reaches the wall at a point 8 feet high. By how much distance should the ladder be moved towards the wall so that its top reaches a point at 9.6 feet high?",
        options: {
          a: "4.4 ft",
          b: "3.2 ft",
          c: "2.8 ft",
          d: "3.92 ft"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nInitial base = \u221A(10\xB2 - 8\xB2) = \u221A(100 - 64) = 6 ft\nFinal base = \u221A(102 - 9.62) = \u221A(100 - 92.16) = 2.8 ft\nDistance moved towards the wall = 6 - 2.8 = 3.2 ft\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3.2 ft.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nLength of the ladder (Hypotenuse) = 10 ft\nInitial height of the wall (Perpendicular1) = 8 ft\nFinal height of the wall (Perpendicular\xB2) = 9.6 ft\n\nFormula Used:\nPythagoras Theorem: Hypotenuse\xB2 = Perpendicular\xB2 + Base\xB2\n\nCalculations:\nLet the initial distance of the ladder from the wall be x ft.\n\u21D2 102 = 82 + x\xB2\n\u21D2 100 = 64 + x\xB2\n\u21D2 x\xB2 = 36 \u21D2 x = 6 ft\nLet the final distance of the ladder from the wall be y ft.\n\u21D2 102 = 9.62 + y\xB2\n\u21D2 100 = 92.16 + y\xB2\n\u21D2 y\xB2 = 7.84 \u21D2 y = 2.8 ft\nDistance the ladder moved towards the wall:\n\u21D2 Distance = x - y\n\u21D2 Distance = 6 - 2.8 = 3.2 ft\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3.2 ft.\n\nAdditional Information\nPythagoras Theorem\n\nIn a right - angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides: a\xB2 + b\xB2 = c\xB2.\n\nPythagorean Triplets\n\nSets of three integers that satisfy the Pythagoras theorem. Common examples include (3, 4, 5), (5, 12, 13), and (8, 15, 17). The first part of this question directly scales the (3, 4, 5) triplet into (6, 8, 10).\n\nDistance vs Displacement\n\nWhile the ladder length remains constant, solving for the shifting horizontal and vertical distances requires treating the movement as two completely separate right - angled triangles.",
