        userTime: "00:32",
        avgTime: "00:16",
        question: "A man and a woman can complete a task in 8 and 12 days respectively. In how many days will 2 men and 3 women be able to complete the task?",
        options: {
          a: "5",
          b: "3",
          c: "4",
          d: "2"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTotal Work = LCM(8, 12) = 24 units.\nEfficiency of 1 man = 24 \xF7 8 = 3 units/day.\nEfficiency of 1 woman = 24 \xF7 12 = 2 units/day.\nCombined efficiency of 2 men and 3 women = (2 \xD7 3) + (3 \xD7 2) = 12 units/day.\nTime required = Total Work \xF7 Combined efficiency = 24 \xF7 12 = 2 days.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2 days.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTime taken by 1 man = 8 days\nTime taken by 1 woman = 12 days\n\nFormula Used:\nTotal Work = LCM of given days\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Efficiency of 2 men = 2 \xD7 3 = 6 units/day\n\u21D2 Efficiency of 3 women = 3 \xD7 2 = 6 units/day\n\u21D2 Total efficiency of 2 men and 3 women = 6 + 6 = 12 units/day\n\u21D2 Time required = Total Work \xF7 Total Efficiency\n\u21D2 Time required = 24 \xF7 12 = 2 days\n\u2234 The correct answer is 2 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nWork and Wages\n\nWages are distributed in proportion to the total work done by each person. If people work for the same duration, wages are distributed in the ratio of their efficiencies.\n\nMDH Rule\n\nIf M1 men do W1 work in D1 days for H1 hours per day, and M\xB2 men do W\xB2 work in D\xB2 days for H\xB2 hours per day, then: (M1 \xD7 D1 \xD7 H1) \xF7 W1 = (M\xB2 \xD7 D\xB2 \xD7 H\xB2) \xF7 W\xB2.\n\nPipes and Cisterns\n\nCalculations are the same as Time and Work. A pipe filling a tank has positive efficiency, while a leak or a pipe emptying the tank has negative efficiency.",
