        question: "Sum of four consecutive multiples of 3 is 78. The largest of these number is:",
        options: {
          a: "18",
          b: "27",
          c: "24",
          d: "21"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nAverage of 4 consecutive multiples of 3 = 78 \xF7 4 = 19.5\n\nThe average lies exactly between the 2nd and 3rd numbers in the sequence.\n\nSince the gap between consecutive multiples is 3, the 3rd number = 19.5 + (3 \xF7 2) = 21\n\nThe 4th (largest) number will be the next multiple = 21 + 3 = 24\n\u2234 The correct answer is 24.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nSum of four consecutive multiples of 3 = 78\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the four consecutive multiples of 3 be x, x + 3, x + 6, and x + 9.\n\u21D2 According to the question, Sum = 78\n\u21D2 x + (x + 3) + (x + 6) + (x + 9) = 78\n\u21D2 4x + 18 = 78\n\u21D2 4x = 78 - 18\n\u21D2 4x = 60\n\u21D2 x = 60 \xF7 4 = 15\n\u21D2 We need to find the largest of these numbers, which is x + 9.\n\u21D2 Largest number = 15 + 9 = 24\n\u2234 The correct answer is 24.\n\nAdditional Information\nConsecutive Multiples\n\nConsecutive multiples of any number n always maintain a constant common difference of n between them. They form an Arithmetic Progression (AP).\n\nAverage of an Arithmetic Progression\n\nThe average (mean) of any Arithmetic Progression is simply the average of its first and last terms. For an even number of terms, it lies exactly halfway between the two middle terms.\n\nSum of an AP\n\nThe sum of terms in an AP can be calculated as: Sum = Number of terms \xD7 Average. Therefore, finding the average first is often the fastest method to solve such series.",
