        userTime: "00:51",
        avgTime: "00:35",
        question: "A student got 20% marks and failed by 72 marks. If he scores 40% marks then he gets 8 marks more than the passing marks. Find the passing marks.",
        options: {
          a: "150",
          b: "152",
          c: "142",
          d: "160"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nDifference in percentage = 40% - 20% = 20%\nDifference in marks = 72 (shortfall) + 8 (excess) = 80 marks\nSince 20% of maximum marks = 80 marks\nPassing marks = 20% marks + 72 marks = 80 + 72 = 152\n\u2234 The correct answer is 152 marks.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nMarks scored in case 1 = 20% (Failed by 72 marks)\nMarks scored in case 2 = 40% (8 marks more than passing marks)\n\nCalculation:\nLet the total maximum marks be x.\n\u21D2 Passing marks = 20% of x + 72\n\u21D2 Passing marks = 40% of x - 8\n\nEquating both the expressions:\n\n\u21D2 20% of x + 72 = 40% of x - 8\n\u21D2 40% of x - 20% of x = 72 + 8\n\u21D2 20% of x = 80\n\u21D2 x = (80 \xD7 100) \xF7 20\n\u21D2 x = 400\n\u21D2 Passing marks = 20% of 400 + 72\n\u21D2 Passing marks = 80 + 72 = 152\n\u2234 The correct answer is 152 marks.\n\nAdditional Information\nPassing Marks Concept\nWhen a student fails by a certain margin, Passing Marks = Obtained Marks + Shortfall Marks.\n\nExcess Marks Concept\n\nWhen a student scores above passing marks, Passing Marks = Obtained Marks - Excess Marks.\nDifference Rule\n\nThe percentage difference between the two scenarios will always be equal to the sum of the shortfall marks and the excess marks.",
