        userTime: "00:46",
        avgTime: "00:33",
        question: "Two tangents TP and TQ are drawn to circle with centre O from an external point T, where P and Q are points on circumference of the circle. If \u2220PTQ = 50\xB0, then find the value of \u2220TOQ.",
        options: {
          a: "100\xB0",
          b: "75\xB0",
          c: "55\xB0",
          d: "65\xB0"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nThe line joining the external point to the centre bisects the angle between the two tangents.\n\n\u21D2 \u2220OTQ = \u2220PTQ \xF7 2 = 50\xB0 \xF7 2 = 25\xB0\n\nIn right-angled \u0394OQT, the radius OQ is perpendicular to the tangent TQ at point Q (\u2220OQT = 90\xB0).\n\n\u21D2 \u2220TOQ = 90\xB0 - \u2220OTQ = 90\xB0 - 25\xB0 = 65\xB0\n\u2234 The correct answer is 65\xB0.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\n\u21D2 Tangents TP and TQ are drawn from an external point T.\n\u21D2 \u2220PTQ = 50\xB0\n\nConcept Used:\n\n\u21D2 The radius is perpendicular to the tangent at the point of contact.\n\u21D2 The sum of the angles of a quadrilateral is 360\xB0.\n\n50\xB0\n?\nO\nT\nP\nQ\n\nCalculations:\nIn quadrilateral OPTQ :\n\n\u21D2 \u2220OPT = 90\xB0 and \u2220OQT = 90\xB0\n\u21D2 \u2220OPT + \u2220OQT + \u2220PTQ + \u2220POQ = 360\xB0\n\u21D2 90\xB0 + 90\xB0 + 50\xB0 + \u2220POQ = 360\xB0\n\u21D2 230\xB0 + \u2220POQ = 360\xB0\n\u21D2 \u2220POQ = 360\xB0 - 230\xB0 = 130\xB0\nSince the line OT joining the external point to the centre bisects \u2220POQ :\n\u21D2 \u2220TOQ = \u2220POQ \xF7 2\n\u21D2 \u2220TOQ = 130\xB0 \xF7 2 = 65\xB0\n\u2234 The correct answer is 65\xB0.\n\nAdditional Information\nProperties of Tangents from an External Point\n\nThe lengths of the two tangents drawn from an external point to a circle are exactly equal to each other (TP = TQ).\n\nAngle Subtended at the Centre\n\nThe two tangents subtend equal angles at the centre of the circle, meaning the line connecting the external point to the centre acts as an angle bisector (\u2220POT = \u2220QOT).\n\nCyclic Quadrilateral Formation\n\nThe quadrilateral formed by the two tangents and the two radii to the points of contact has opposite angles that sum to 180\xB0, specifically \u2220PTQ + \u2220POQ = 180\xB0.",
