        avgTime: "00:23",
        question: "A vessel can be filled by a tap A in 8 minutes, and by another tap B in 10 minutes. A third tap C can empty the vessel in 4 minutes. If all taps are opened simultaneously when the vessel is half full, then which of the following is true?",
        options: {
          a: "The vessel will be filled in 20 minutes.",
          b: "The vessel will be filled in 40 minutes.",
          c: "The vessel will be emptied in 40 minutes.",
          d: "The vessel will be emptied in 20 minutes."
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTotal Capacity = LCM(8, 10, 4) = 40 units.\nEfficiency of taps \u2192 A: +5, B: +4, C: -10.\nNet efficiency = 5 + 4 - 10 = -1 unit/min (Negative means vessel empties).\nSince the vessel is half full, volume to empty = 40 \xF7 2 = 20 units.\nTime to empty the vessel = 20 \xF7 1 = 20 minutes.\n\u2234 The correct answer is emptied in 20 minutes.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTime taken by tap A to fill the vessel = 8 min\nTime taken by tap B to fill the vessel = 10 min\nTime taken by tap C to empty the vessel = 4 min\n\nThe vessel is half full initially.\n\nTap	Time (min)	Total Capacity	Efficiency\nA	8	40	+5\nB	10	+4\nC (Empty)	4	-10\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Net efficiency of all taps together = 5 + 4 - 10 = -1 unit/min.\n\u21D2 A negative efficiency indicates that the vessel is being emptied.\n\u21D2 Quantity of water in the half-full vessel = 40 \xD7 (1/2) = 20 units.\n\u21D2 Time required to empty the vessel = Total water \xF7 Effective efficiency\n\u21D2 Time = 20 \xF7 1 = 20 minutes.\n\u2234 The correct answer is emptied in 20 minutes.\n\nAdditional Information\nPipes and Cisterns Basics\n\nIf a pipe fills a tank in x hours, its part filled in 1 hour is 1/x. If it empties a full tank in y hours, its part emptied in 1 hour is -1/y.\n\nNet Work Method\n\nNet part filled in 1 hour by both pipes = (1/x) - (1/y). If the result is positive, the tank is being filled. If negative, it is being emptied.\nTotal Capacity (LCM Method)\n\nTotal capacity is assumed to be the Least Common Multiple (LCM) of all given individual times. Efficiency = Total Capacity \xF7 Time taken.",
