        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nWhen the vertices of an equilateral triangle are cut to form a regular hexagon, the side length is exactly trisected.\n\nSide of the regular hexagon = Side of triangle \xF7 3 = a / 3.\nArea of a regular hexagon = 6 \xD7 Area of small equilateral triangle = 6 \xD7 (\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 (side)\xB2.\nArea = 6 \xD7 (\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 (a / 3)\xB2 = (3\u221A3 \xF7 2) \xD7 (a2 \xF7 9).\nArea = \u221A3a2 \xF7 6 = a2 / (2\u221A3).\n\u2234 The correct answer is a2 / (2\u221A3) square units.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nAn equilateral triangle with side length a.\n\nThe vertices are cut out to make a regular hexagon.\n\nFormula Used:\nArea of an equilateral triangle = (\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 a2\nArea of a regular hexagon = 6 \xD7 (\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 x2 (where x is the side length)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the side length of the regular hexagon be x.\n\u21D2 When cutting the vertices of an equilateral triangle to form a regular hexagon, the cut-out portions are smaller equilateral triangles of side length x.\n\u21D2 The original side 'a' consists of three linear segments: one side of a cut-off triangle, one side of the hexagon, and one side of another cut-off triangle.\n\u21D2 x + x + x = a\n\u21D2 3x = a\n\u21D2 x = a / 3\n\u21D2 The area of the regular hexagon is the combined area of 6 small equilateral triangles of side x.\n\u21D2 Area = 6 \xD7 [(\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 (a / 3)\xB2]\n\u21D2 Area = 6 \xD7 (\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 (a2 \xF7 9)\n\u21D2 Area = (6\u221A3 \xD7 a2) \xF7 36\n\u21D2 Area = (\u221A3 \xD7 a2) \xF7 6\n\u21D2 Multiplying numerator and denominator by \u221A3 to simplify the fraction:\n\u21D2 Area = (\u221A3 \xD7 \u221A3 \xD7 a2) \xF7 (6 \xD7 \u221A3) = 3a2 \xF7 6\u221A3\n\u21D2 Area = a2 / (2\u221A3)\n\u2234 The correct answer is a2 / (2\u221A3) square units.\n\nAdditional Information\nArea of an Equilateral Triangle\n\nThe area of an equilateral triangle with side 'a' is given by Area = (\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 a2, and its height is h = (\u221A3 \xF7 2) \xD7 a.\n\nRegular Hexagon Area\n\nA regular hexagon with side 'x' is structurally made of 6 identical equilateral triangles. Its total area is 6 \xD7 (\u221A3 \xF7 4) \xD7 x2 = (3\u221A3 \xF7 2) \xD7 x2.\n\nInradius & Circumradius of Equilateral Triangle\n\nFor an equilateral triangle of side 'a', the inradius is r = a \xF7 (2\u221A3) and the circumradius is R = a \xF7 \u221A3. Note that the circumradius is always exactly double the inradius (R = 2r).\n\nMax Area Hexagon in a Triangle\n\nThe largest regular hexagon that can be inscribed in any equilateral triangle is formed precisely by cutting off its corners, and its side length is always strictly one-third of the parent triangle's side.",
