        question: "From 11 to 50, how many numbers are there which are exactly divisible by 7 but not by 3?",
        options: {
          a: "Two",
          b: "Four",
          c: "Five",
          d: "Six"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nTotal multiples of 7 from 11 to 50 = (Largest multiple - Smallest multiple) \xF7 7 + 1 = (49 - 14) \xF7 7 + 1 = 6.\n\nTotal multiples of 21 (divisible by both 7 and 3) from 11 to 50 = (42 - 21) \xF7 21 + 1 = 2.\n\nTotal numbers divisible by 7 but not by 3 = 6 - 2 = 4.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 numbers.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nRange of numbers: From 11 to 50.\nCondition: Numbers must be divisible by 7 but not by 3.\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 First, list all the numbers between 11 and 50 that are exactly divisible by 7.\n\u21D2 These multiples are: 14, 21, 28, 35, 42, 49.\n\u21D2 Total count of these multiples = 6.\n\u21D2 Next, identify the numbers from this list that are also divisible by 3.\n\u21D2 A number divisible by both 7 and 3 must be divisible by their LCM, which is 21.\n\u21D2 The multiples of 21 in the given range are: 21 and 42.\n\u21D2 We must exclude these 2 numbers from our initial list.\n\u21D2 The remaining numbers are: 14, 28, 35, 49.\n\u21D2 Total count of the remaining numbers = 4.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 numbers.\n\nAdditional Information\nLCM of Co-prime Numbers\n\nIf two numbers are co-prime (like 3 and 7, having no common factors other than 1), their Least Common Multiple (LCM) is simply their product (3 \xD7 7 = 21). Any number divisible by both must be a multiple of this LCM.\n\nArithmetic Progression (AP) Counting\n\nTo find the number of terms in an AP, you can use the formula: Number of terms = (Last term - First term) \xF7 Common Difference + 1.\n\nDivisibility Rule of 3\n\nA number is exactly divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is a multiple of 3.",
