        userTime: "00:28",
        avgTime: "00:42",
        question: "In \u2206ABC, \u2220B = 90\xB0 and BD \u27C2 AC. If AC = 9 cm and AD = 3 cm, then BD is equal to:",
        options: {
          a: "2\u221A3 cm",
          b: "3\u221A3 cm",
          c: "2\u221A2 cm",
          d: "3\u221A2 cm"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nBy the Geometric Mean Theorem for a right-angled triangle:\n\nAltitude to hypotenuse = \u221A(Product of hypotenuse segments)\nCD = AC - AD = 9 - 3 = 6 cm\nBD = \u221A(3 \xD7 6) = \u221A18 = 3\u221A2 cm\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3\u221A2 cm.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nIn right-angled \u0394ABC, \u2220B = 90\xB0\n\nBD \u22A5 AC\n\nAC = 9 cm, AD = 3 cm\n\nFormula Used:\nIn a right triangle, when an altitude is drawn from the right angle to the hypotenuse, the square of the altitude equals the product of the lengths of the two segments on the hypotenuse: BD2 = AD \xD7 DC.\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 DC = AC - AD\n\u21D2 DC = 9 - 3 = 6 cm\n\u21D2 BD2 = AD \xD7 DC\n\u21D2 BD2 = 3 \xD7 6\n\u21D2 BD2 = 18\n\u21D2 BD = \u221A18 = 3\u221A2 cm\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3\u221A2 cm.\n\nAdditional Information\nGeometric Mean Theorem (Altitude Rule)\n\nIn a right-angled triangle, the altitude drawn to the hypotenuse divides it into two segments such that the altitude is the geometric mean of these two segments. (Altitude2 = Segment 1 \xD7 Segment 2)\n\nLeg Rule of a Right Triangle\n\nEach leg of a right-angled triangle is the geometric mean of the hypotenuse and the segment of the hypotenuse adjacent to that leg. (Leg2 = Hypotenuse \xD7 Adjacent Segment)\n\nPythagorean Theorem\n\nIn a right-angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two legs. (H2 = P2 + B2)",
