        userTime: "00:51",
        avgTime: "00:29",
        question: "Pabitra takes twice as long as Kader or three times as long as Rahim to complete a task. If they work together, they can complete the task in 2 days. How long will it take Kader to complete the task on his own?",
        options: {
          a: "6 days",
          b: "5 days",
          c: "12 days",
          d: "8 days"
        },
        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nRatio of time taken by Pabitra, Kader, and Rahim = 6 : 3 : 2\nEfficiency ratio = 1/6 : 1/3 : 1/2 = 1 : 2 : 3\nCombined efficiency = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 units/day\nTotal Work = Combined Efficiency \xD7 Time = 6 \xD7 2 = 12 units\nTime taken by Kader alone = Total Work \xF7 Kader's Efficiency = 12 \xF7 2 = 6 days\n\u2234 The correct answer is 6 days.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPabitra takes twice as long as Kader and three times as long as Rahim to complete the task.\n\nLet the time taken by Pabitra be 6x days.\n\u21D2 Time taken by Kader = 6x \xF7 2 = 3x days\n\u21D2 Time taken by Rahim = 6x \xF7 3 = 2x days\nMethod Used: LCM Method for Time & Work\n\nPerson	Days	Total Work	Efficiency\nPabitra	6x	6x	1\nKader	3x	2\nRahim	2x	3\n\n\u21D2 Total efficiency = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 units/day\n\u21D2 Total time taken working together = Total Work \xF7 Total Efficiency = 6x \xF7 6 = x days\n\u21D2 It is given that they complete the task together in 2 days.\n\u21D2 Therefore, x = 2\n\u21D2 Time taken by Kader alone = 3x = 3 \xD7 2 = 6 days\n\u2234 The correct answer is 6 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nEfficiency and Time Relationship\n\nEfficiency is inversely proportional to the time taken. If a person takes 'd' days to complete a work, their efficiency (one day's work) is 1/d.\n\nTotal Work Concept\n\nTotal Work is considered as the LCM of the individual times taken by each person. Total Work = Combined Efficiency \xD7 Total Time Taken.\n\nWork and Wages\n\nWages are always distributed in the ratio of the total work done by each person. If people work for the same number of days, their wages are distributed directly in the ratio of their efficiencies.",
