        userTime: "00:54",
        avgTime: "00:35",
        question: "If a sum triples in 4 years at simple interest, find the time that it would take to become 5 times itself at the same rate of interest.",
        options: {
          a: "6 years",
          b: "8 years",
          c: "5 years",
          d: "3 years"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\nShortcut Trick\nIn Simple Interest, Interest is directly proportional to Time when Principal and Rate are constant.\n\nFor a sum to triple (3 times), Interest = 3 - 1 = 2 units.\nFor a sum to become 5 times, Interest = 5 - 1 = 4 units.\nTime for 2 units interest = 4 years.\nTime for 4 units interest = (4 \xF7 2) \xD7 4 = 8 years.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8 years.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven: Time (T1) = 4 years, Amount1 = 3P, Amount2 = 5P (where P is Principal)\nFormula: Simple Interest (SI) = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) / 100\nPrincipal\n(P)\xB3 \xD7 P\n5 \xD7 P\nInterest = 2P\n(4 years)\nInterest = 2P\n(4 years)\nTotal Time = 4 + 4 = 8 years\n\u21D2 SI1 = Amount1 - P = 3P - P = 2P \u21D2 2P = (P \xD7 R \xD7 4) / 100 \u21D2 R = (2P \xD7 100) / (4P) = 50% \u21D2 SI2 = Amount2 - P = 5P - P = 4P \u21D2 4P = (P \xD7 50 \xD7 T2) / 100 \u21D2 T2 = (4P \xD7 100) / (50P) \u21D2 T2 = 400 / 50 = 8 years\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8 years.\n\nAdditional Information\nInterest Proportionality\n\nIn simple interest, the interest earned is directly proportional to time (T) and rate\n(R) since SI = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) / 100.\n\nAmount as n-times Principal\n\nIf a sum becomes n times itself, the interest earned is (n - 1) \xD7 P. For same rate and principal, T1 / (n1 - 1) = T2 / (n2 - 1).\n\nRule of SI vs CI\n\nFor the first year (or first compounding period), SI and CI are equal if the principal and rate are the same.",
