        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIf a sum becomes n times in T years at Compound Interest,\n\nIt will become nm times in m \xD7 T years.\n\nHere, the sum becomes 2 times in 3 years.\n\nWe need the sum to become 8 times, which is 23 times (so m = 3).\nRequired time = 3 \xD7 3 = 9 years.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 9 years.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPrincipal\n(P) becomes 2P in Time (T) = 3 years.\nLet Rate of Interest be R% per annum.\n\nFormula Used:\nAmount (A) = P(1 + R/100)T\n\nCalculation:\n\u21D2 2P = P(1 + R/100)\xB3\n\u21D2 2 = (1 + R/100)\xB3    ... (Equation 1)\n\nWe need to find the time (T') when the principal becomes 8P.\n\n\u21D2 8P = P(1 + R/100)T'\n\u21D2 8 = (1 + R/100)T'\nSince 8 can be written as 23, substitute the value of 2 from Equation 1:\n\u21D2 [(1 + R/100)\xB3]3 = (1 + R/100)T'\n\u21D2 (1 + R/100)9 = (1 + R/100)T'\n\nComparing the powers on both sides:\n\n\u21D2 T' = 9\n\u2234 The correct answer is 9 years.\n\nAdditional Information\nMultiplying Factor in Compound Interest\n\nIf a sum of money becomes x times in T years at CI, it becomes xn times in n \xD7 T years due to successive compounding.\n\nSimple Interest Linear Growth\n\nUnlike CI, in Simple Interest, if a sum becomes 2 times in T years (i.e., Interest = P), it will become 8 times (Interest = 7P) in 7 \xD7 T years since interest remains constant.\n\nHalf-Yearly Compounding\n\nWhen compounded half-yearly, the rate of interest becomes half (R/2) and the time period is doubled (2T). Formula: A = P(1 + R/200)\xB2T.\n\nCI and SI Difference\n\nThe difference between Compound Interest and Simple Interest for 2 years on a given principal is calculated as: D = P \xD7 (R/100)\xB2.",
