        userTime: "01:13",
        avgTime: "00:48",
        question: "A saves 18% of his income. If his income increases by 41% and still saves the same amount, find the percentage increase in his expenditure.",
        options: {
          a: "60%",
          b: "35%",
          c: "50%",
          d: "40%"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\nShortcut Trick\nLet initial Income = 100. Then Savings = 18 and Expenditure = 82.\n\nIncome increases by 41% (100 \u2192 141). The savings amount remains the same (18).\n\nSince Income = Expenditure + Savings, the entire increase in income must go to expenditure if savings is constant.\nIncrease in Expenditure = Income Increase = 41.\n% Increase in Expenditure = (Increase \xF7 Original Expenditure) \xD7 100 = (41 \xF7 82) \xD7 100 = 50%.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 50%.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nInitial Savings = 18% of Income\nIncome Increase = 41%\nFinal Savings = Initial Savings (Amount)\n\nFormula:\n\nIncome = Expenditure + Savings\nPercentage Increase = (Change \xF7 Original Value) \xD7 100\n\u21D2 Let original income = 100\n\u21D2 Original Savings = 18\n\u21D2 Original Expenditure = 100 - 18 = 82\n\u21D2 New Income = 100 + 41% of 100 = 141\n\u21D2 New Savings = 18 (amount remains constant)\n\u21D2 New Expenditure = 141 - 18 = 123\n\u21D2 Increase in Expenditure = 123 - 82 = 41\n\u21D2 Percentage Increase = (41 \xF7 82) \xD7 100\n\u21D2 Percentage Increase = 1/2 \xD7 100 = 50%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 50%.\n\nAdditional Information\nIncome-Expenditure Components\n\nIncome is the sum of Expenditure and Savings. A change in any one component must be balanced by the others to satisfy the equation: \u0394Income = \u0394Expenditure + \u0394Savings.\n\nPercentage Change Formula\n\nTo find the percentage increase or decrease, always use the initial value as the base: % Change = [(New Value - Old Value) \xF7 Old Value] \xD7 100.\n\nEffect of Constant Component\n\nIf savings remains constant (\u0394Savings = 0), then the absolute increase in income is exactly equal to the absolute increase in expenditure.",
