        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nMultiplying factor for 15% compound interest annually = 1 + 15/100 = 115/100 = 23/20.\nRatio of Amount to Principal = 279841 \xF7 160000.\n\nWe know the denominator 160000 is exactly 204, so we check if 279841 is 234.\n\nSince 232 = 529 and 529 \xD7 529 = 279841, the ratio is strictly (23/20)4.\n\nThe exponent represents the number of compounding years.\n\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 years.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPrincipal (P) = \u20B91, 60, 000\nAmount (A) = \u20B92, 79, 841\nRate of Interest (R) = 15% per annum compounded annually\nTime = n years\n\nFormula Used:\nA = P \xD7 (1 + R/100)n\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 279841 = 160000 \xD7 (1 + 15/100)n\n\u21D2 279841 \xF7 160000 = (115/100)n\n\u21D2 279841 \xF7 160000 = (23/20)n\n\u21D2 Observing the denominator: 160000 = 16 \xD7 10000 = 24 \xD7 104 = 204\n\u21D2 Similarly, analyzing the numerator: 232 = 529 and 529 \xD7 529 = 279841, therefore 279841 = 234\n\u21D2 (23/20)4 = (23/20)n\n\u21D2 By comparing the bases and equating the powers, we get n = 4.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 4 years.\n\nAdditional Information\nHalf - Yearly Compounding\n\nIf the interest is compounded half - yearly instead of annually, the applied rate is halved (R/2)% and the time period becomes doubled (2n) in the CI formula.\n\nEffective Rate of Compound Interest\n\nFor 2 years at an annual rate of R%, the effective compound interest percentage can be directly calculated using the successive percentage formula: (2R + R\xB2/100)%.\n\nDifference Between CI and SI\n\nFor exactly 2 years, the difference between CI and SI on the same principal is given by: CI - SI = P \xD7 (R/100)\xB2. For 3 years, the difference formula extends to: P \xD7 (R/100)\xB2 \xD7 (3 + R/100).",
