        userTime: "01:35",
        avgTime: "01:08",
        question: "The number of students in a school increases and decreases by 10% respectively in every alternate year. If there was increase in the year 2000 then in how much percentage what happened in the number of students in the year 2003 in comparison to year 2000.",
        options: {
          a: "8.9% decrease",
          b: "9.8% increase",
          c: "8.9% increase",
          d: "9.8% decrease"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nLet the initial number of students in the year 2000 be 100.\n\nApplying successive changes of + 10%, -10%, and + 10% respectively for 3 years using multiplying factors: Students in 2003 = 100 \xD7 (110 \xF7 100) \xD7 (90 \xF7 100) \xD7 (110 \xF7 100)\n\nStudents in 2003 = 100 \xD7 1.1 \xD7 0.9 \xD7 1.1 = 108.9\nOverall percentage change = 108.9 - 100 = +8.9%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8.9% increase.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nPercentage change in year 2000 - 2001 = +10%\nPercentage change in year 2001 - 2002 = -10%\nPercentage change in year 2002 - 2003 = +10%\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the number of students in the year 2000 be 100.\n\u21D2 Number of students in 2001 = 100 + (10% of 100) = 110\n\u21D2 Number of students in 2002 = 110 - (10% of 110) = 110 - 11 = 99\n\u21D2 Number of students in 2003 = 99 + (10% of 99) = 99 + 9.9 = 108.9\n\u21D2 Increase in students compared to year 2000 = 108.9 - 100 = 8.9\n\u21D2 Percentage increase = (8.9 \xF7 100) \xD7 100% = 8.9%\n\u2234 The correct answer is 8.9% increase.\n\nAdditional Information\nSuccessive Percentage Change\n\nFor two successive percentage changes of a% and b%, the effective net change is calculated using the formula: Net Change = a + b + (a \xD7 b) \xF7 100.\n\nEqual Increase and Decrease\n\nIf a value is first increased by x% and then decreased by x% (or vice versa), there is always a net percentage decrease which is equal to (x\xB2 \xF7 100)%.\n\nMultiplying Factor Method\n\nFor a given series of percentage changes, a new value is simply the original value multiplied by its consecutive multiplying factors. For an R% increase, the factor is (100 + R)/100, and for an R% decrease, it is (100 - R)/100.",
