        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nLet Total Work = LCM(10, 15) = 30 units.\nEfficiency of A = 30 \xF7 10 = 3 units/day, and B = 30 \xF7 15 = 2 units/day.\nWork done by B in the first 5 days = 5 \xD7 2 = 10 units.\nRemaining work (30 - 10 = 20 units) is done by A and B together (combined efficiency = 5).\nTotal time = 5 days + (20 \xF7 5) days = 5 + 4 = 9 days.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 9 days.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nTime taken by A = 10 days\nTime taken by B = 15 days\n\nB starts the work and A joins him after 5 days.\n\nFormula Used:\nTotal Work = Efficiency \xD7 Time\n\nCalculation:\nPerson Days Total Work Efficiency\n\nA 10 30 3\n\nB 15 2\n\n\u21D2 Work done by B in the initial 5 days = Efficiency of B \xD7 5 = 2 \xD7 5 = 10 units\n\u21D2 Remaining work = 30 - 10 = 20 units\n\u21D2 When A joins B, their combined efficiency = 3 + 2 = 5 units/day\n\u21D2 Time taken by A and B to finish the remaining work = 20 \xF7 5 = 4 days\n\u21D2 Total days required to complete the total work = Initial 5 days + 4 days = 9 days\n\u2234 The correct answer is 9 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nLCM Method for Total Work\n\nAssuming total work as the least common multiple (LCM) of the given times ensures individual efficiencies are whole numbers, avoiding complex fraction calculations.\n\nEfficiency and Time Relationship\n\nEfficiency is inversely proportional to the time taken to complete a specific task. If a person takes less time, their daily work efficiency is proportionally higher.\n\nCombined Working\n\nWhen individuals work together simultaneously, their total efficiency is the sum of their individual efficiencies (Combined Efficiency = EA + EB).",
