        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nIn Simple Interest, the interest earned is directly proportional to the time period.\n\nDirect Formula: (n1 - 1) \xF7 t1 = (n\xB2 - 1) \xF7 t\xB2\nGiven: n1 = 4, t1 = 5 years, t\xB2 = 15 years.\n\u21D2 (4 - 1) \xF7 5 = (n\xB2 - 1) \xF7 15\n\u21D2 3 \xF7 5 = (n\xB2 - 1) \xF7 15 \u21D2 n\xB2 - 1 = 9 \u21D2 n\xB2 = 10\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10 times.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nA sum becomes 4 times of itself in 5 years at simple interest.\n\nFormula Used:\nSimple Interest (SI) = (P \xD7 R \xD7 T) \xF7 100\nAmount (A) = P + SI\n\nP\n\n4P\n\n7P\n\n10P\n\n0 Yrs 5 Yrs 10 Yrs 15 Yrs + 3P (SI) + 3P (SI) + 3P (SI)\n\nTotal Amount at 15 Years = 10P (10 times)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 Let the principal sum be P.\n\u21D2 Amount after 5 years = 4P\n\u21D2 Simple Interest earned in 5 years = 4P - P = 3P\n\u21D2 Rate of Interest (R) = (SI \xD7 100) \xF7 (P \xD7 T)\n\u21D2 R = (3P \xD7 100) \xF7 (P \xD7 5) = 60%\n\u21D2 Now, we need to find the amount after T = 15 years at R = 60%:\n\u21D2 SI = (P \xD7 60 \xD7 15) \xF7 100\n\u21D2 SI = 900P \xF7 100 = 9P\n\u21D2 New Amount = Principal + SI = P + 9P = 10P\n\u21D2 Thus, the sum will become 10 times of itself.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 10 times.\n\nAdditional Information\nSimple Interest Constant Growth\n\nUnlike compound interest, simple interest grows by a constant amount every year. If a sum earns 3P interest in 5 years, it will earn exactly 3P interest in every subsequent 5 - year period.\n\nDirect Formula for Multipliers\n\nWhen a sum becomes n1 times in t1 years and n\xB2 times in t\xB2 years at simple interest, use the relation: (n1 - 1) \xF7 t1 = (n\xB2 - 1) \xF7 t\xB2.\n\nPrincipal and Amount Relation\n\nThe amount (A) is the sum of Principal (P) and Simple Interest (SI). Always subtract 1 from the number of times it becomes to get the interest multiplier: SI = (n - 1)P.",
