        question: "What is the single discount equivalent to three successive discounts 10%, 15% and 20%?",
        options: {
          a: "45%",
          b: "39.8%",
          c: "38.8%",
          d: "61.2%"
        },
        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nMultiplying Factors for 10%, 15%, and 20% discounts are 0.9, 0.85, and 0.8.\n\nCombined Multiplying Factor = 0.9 \xD7 0.85 \xD7 0.8 = 0.9 \xD7 0.68 = 0.612\nEquivalent Single Discount = 1 - 0.612 = 0.388\n\u2234 The correct option is C.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven: Successive discounts d1 = 10%, d\xB2 = 15%, d\xB3 = 20%\nFormula: Net Selling Price = MP \xD7 (1 - d1/100) \xD7 (1 - d\xB2/100) \xD7 (1 - d\xB3/100)\n\nMP (100%) - 10% -15% -20%\n\nSP (61.2%)\n\nNet Discount = 38.8%\n\u21D2 Let the initial Marked Price (MP) = 1\n\u21D2 Price after 10% discount = 1 \xD7 (1 - 0.10) = 0.9\n\u21D2 Price after 15% discount = 0.9 \xD7 (1 - 0.15) = 0.9 \xD7 0.85 = 0.765\n\u21D2 Price after 20% discount = 0.765 \xD7 (1 - 0.20) = 0.765 \xD7 0.8 = 0.612\n\u21D2 Final Selling Price (SP) = 0.612\n\u21D2 Equivalent Single Discount = MP - SP = 1 - 0.612 = 0.388\n\u2234 The correct option is C.\n\nAdditional Information\nSuccessive Discount Formula for Two Rates\n\nFor two successive discounts of x% and y%, the single equivalent discount is (x + y - (x \xD7 y)/100)%.\n\nSuccessive Percentage Change\n\nThe order in which successive discounts or percentage increases are applied does not change the final result.\n\nRelation between MP and SP\n\nSelling Price = Marked Price \xD7 (100 - D1)/100 \xD7 (100 - D\xB2)/100 \xD7 ... where Dn are successive discount percentages.",
