        userTime: "01:20",
        avgTime: "00:31",
        question: "The point of intersection of the graphs of the equations 12x + y - 8 = 0 and 2x + 3y + 10 = 0 lies on the graph of which of the following equations?",
        options: {
          a: "2x - y + 5 = 0",
          b: "3x + y - 1 = 0",
          c: "2x - y - 5 = 0",
          d: "3x + y + 1 = 0"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nSolve the two given equations quickly to find the point of intersection.\n\n12x + y = 8 and 2x + 3y = -10 \u21D2 multiply the first by 3 to get 36x + 3y = 24.\nSubtracting the second equation: 34x = 34 \u21D2 x = 1. Substituting x = 1 gives y = -4.\nNow check the options with the point (1, -4): Only Option 4 satisfies the point: 3(1) + (-4) + 1 = 3 - 4 + 1 = 0.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3x + y + 1 = 0.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nEquation 1: 12x + y - 8 = 0\nEquation 2: 2x + 3y + 10 = 0\n\nX / Y\n\nO\n\n12x + y - 8 = 0\n2x + 3y + 10 = 0\n3x + y + 1 = 0\n\nP(1, -4)\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 From Equation 1, express y in terms of x:\n\u21D2 y = 8 - 12x\n\u21D2 Substitute the value of y into Equation 2:\n\u21D2 2x + 3(8 - 12x) + 10 = 0\n\u21D2 2x + 24 - 36x + 10 = 0\n\u21D2 -34x + 34 = 0\n\u21D2 34x = 34\n\u21D2 x = 1\n\u21D2 Now, substitute x = 1 back into y = 8 - 12x:\n\u21D2 y = 8 - 12(1) = 8 - 12 = -4\n\u21D2 The point of intersection of the two graphs is P(1, -4).\n\u21D2 We now test the point (1, -4) against the given options.\n\u21D2 Option 1: 2(1) - (-4) + 5 = 2 + 4 + 5 = 11 = \u0338 0\n\u21D2 Option 2: 3(1) + (-4) - 1 = 3 - 4 - 1 = -2 = \u0338 0\n\u21D2 Option 3: 2(1) - (-4) - 5 = 2 + 4 - 5 = 1 = \u0338 0\n\u21D2 Option 4: 3(1) + (-4) + 1 = 3 - 4 + 1 = 0\n\u21D2 Since only Equation 4 is satisfied, the point lies on its graph.\n\u2234 The correct answer is 3x + y + 1 = 0.\n\nAdditional Information\nIntersection of Lines\n\nThe point where two straight lines intersect is the unique coordinate pair (x, y) that satisfies both linear equations simultaneously.\n\nConcurrent Lines\n\nThree or more lines are termed concurrent if they all intersect at exactly the same geometric point on a plane.\n\nChecking a Point on a Line\n\nTo determine if a specific point (a, b) lies on a graph, substitute x = a and y = b into the line's equation and verify if the Left Hand Side equals the Right Hand Side (LHS = RHS).",
