        solution: "Solution\n\nShortcut Trick\nThe multiples of any number form an Arithmetic Progression (A.P.).\n\nFor an A.P. with an odd number of terms, the average is exactly the middle term.\n\nThe middle term of the first 5 multiples is the 3rd multiple.\n\n\u21D2 Average = 3 \xD7 3 = 9\n\u2234 The correct answer is 9.\n\nAlternate Method\nGiven:\nThe sequence consists of the first five multiples of 3.\n\nFormula Used:\nAverage = Sum of all observations \xF7 Total number of observations\n\nCalculations:\n\u21D2 The first five multiples of 3 are: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15.\n\u21D2 Sum of these multiples = 3 + 6 + 9 + 12 + 15\n\u21D2 Sum = 45\n\u21D2 Total number of multiples = 5\n\u21D2 Average = 45 \xF7 5\n\u21D2 Average = 9\n\u2234 The correct answer is 9.\n\nAdditional Information\nAverage of an Arithmetic Progression (A.P.)\n\nFor any sequence in A.P., the average can also be calculated directly using the formula: (First Term + Last Term) \xF7 2. Here, it is (3 + 15) \xF7 2 = 9.\n\nAverage of first n multiples of a number k\n\nThe direct shortcut formula to find the average of the first n multiples of a number k is k \xD7 (n + 1) \xF7 2. Here, it is 3 \xD7 (5 + 1) \xF7 2 = 9.\n\nAverage of first n natural numbers\n\nThe average of the first n consecutive natural numbers is given by the formula (n + 1) \xF7 2.",
