        answer: "c",
        solution: 'Option c is correct: Whale is not a true fish - it is an aquatic mammal belonging to the phylum Chordata, class Mammalia (order Cetacea). Like all mammals, whales are warm-blooded, breathe through lungs (surfacing periodically for air), give birth to live young, and nurse them with milk - none of which are fish characteristics.\n\nWhy the other options are incorrect:\n(a). Jellyfish - Mollusca (Incorrect): Jellyfish actually belong to the phylum Coelenterata (Cnidaria), not Mollusca. They have a simple radially symmetrical body with a central body cavity (coelenteron) and stinging cells (cnidocytes/nematocysts) - a hallmark feature of cnidarians. Mollusca instead includes soft-bodied animals like snails, octopuses, and clams.\n\n(b). Crayfish - Insecta (Incorrect): Crayfish belong to the phylum Arthropoda, but within class Crustacea, not Insecta. Crustaceans (like crabs, prawns, and crayfish) typically have two pairs of antennae and gills for respiration, while insects have one pair of antennae, three pairs of legs, and (usually) wings - key distinguishing features.\n\n(d). Devil fish - Coelenterate (Incorrect): "Devil fish" commonly refers to the manta ray (or sometimes an octopus, depending on regional usage), which - if referring to the manta ray - belongs to phylum Chordata, class Chondrichthyes (cartilaginous fish), not Coelenterata.',
