        answer: "c",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is\n\nKey Points\nAssertion (A): Shivaji had to face opposition from the big Deshmukhs. \u2705 (True)\n\nDeshmukhs were local feudal lords or chiefs who controlled large territories and had significant military and economic power.\nMany Deshmukhs, especially those in western Maharashtra, were loyal to the Adil Shahi Sultanate of Bijapur and opposed Shivaji\u2019s rise, fearing a loss of their authority.\nShivaji\u2019s centralized administration and growing power threatened their semi-autonomous status.\nReason\n(R): These Deshmukhs were not in favour of an independent Maratha State and wanted to remain as feudal lords of Bijapur. \u2705 (True)\nMany Deshmukhs preferred to serve under the Bijapur Sultanate rather than join Shivaji\u2019s vision of an independent Maratha state.\nThey enjoyed privileges under Bijapur\u2019s rule and saw Shivaji\u2019s expansion as a challenge to their feudal control.\nSome of them, such as Jadhavs, Mohites, and Ghorpades, initially resisted Shivaji\u2019s authority.\nWhy\n(R) correctly explains (A)?\nSince the Deshmukhs did not support the idea of a sovereign Maratha state, they opposed Shivaji\u2019s consolidation of power.\nTheir allegiance to Bijapur and their desire to retain feudal autonomy directly led to their conflict with Shivaji.\nShivaji had to subdue many of these Deshmukhs to establish his independent kingdom.\n\nThus, the correct answer is (3) Both (A) and\n(R) are true, and\n(R) is the correct explanation of (A).",
