        userTime: "00:05",
        avgTime: "00:19",
        question: "Water reabsorption from the distal part of the renal tubule is under the control of which hormone?",
        options: {
          a: "Renin",
          b: "Vasopressin",
          c: "Oxytocin",
          d: "Angiotensin"
        },
        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nCONCEPT:\n\nWater Reabsorption in the Distal Renal Tubule\n\nWater reabsorption in the kidneys, specifically in the distal part of the renal tubule and collecting duct, is regulated by hormones to maintain fluid balance and blood pressure.\nThe hormone primarily responsible for controlling water reabsorption in this region is Vasopressin, also known as antidiuretic hormone (ADH).\nVasopressin is secreted by the posterior pituitary gland and acts on the kidneys to increase the permeability of the distal tubule and collecting ducts to water.\n\nEXPLANATION\n\nVasopressin binds to specific receptors on the cells of the distal tubule and collecting ducts.\nThis triggers the insertion of water channels called aquaporins into the cell membranes, allowing water to be reabsorbed back into the bloodstream.\nThis process helps conserve water in the body and concentrates the urine, especially during dehydration or low blood volume conditions.\n\nAdditional Information\nRenin (Option 1): Involved in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which regulates blood pressure and sodium balance.\nOxytocin (Option 3): A hormone involved in uterine contractions and milk ejection, not water reabsorption.\nAngiotensin (Option 4): A component of the renin-angiotensin system, which indirectly influences water and salt retention but does not directly control water reabsorption in the distal tubule.\nTherefore, the correct answer is Vasopressin.",
