        answer: "d",
        solution: "In a standard Judo contest, the Golden Score period has no time limit.\n\nWhile some age-specific or local variations might impose a time limit for safety or scheduling, the official International Judo Federation (IJF) rules for senior competitions do not set a maximum duration for overtime.\n\nIn the past, Judo used a system called Hantei (judge's decision by flags), but this was replaced by the infinite Golden Score to ensure a winner is determined by technical action.\n\nIf a match ends in a draw after the regulation four minutes, it immediately enters the Golden Score phase. The clock is reset, and the contest continues indefinitely until one judoka achieves a score (Waza-ari or Ippon) or until one judoka receives a third penalty (Shido), resulting in disqualification (Hansoku-make).\n\nFurther Insights:\nCarry-over: All scores and penalties (Shidos) accumulated during the regular four-minute match carry over into the Golden Score period.\nPhysical Endurance: Because there is no time limit, some high-level matches have been known to last over 10 to 15 minutes, making it an extreme test of physical and mental endurance.\nWinning Criteria: The very first point awarded ends the match instantly. However, a Shido (minor penalty) does not end the match unless it is the athlete's third and final penalty.\nTactical Shift: Athletes often change their strategy in Golden Score, becoming either more aggressive to find a quick score or more defensive to avoid a tie-breaking penalty.",
