        question: "The 'groundnut fair' known as Kadalekai Parishe in Bengaluru is associated historically with which temple/figure?",
        options: {
          a: "ISKCON Temple and offering of sugarcane",
          b: "Vidhana Soudha and agricultural subsidies launch",
          c: "Tipu Sultan\u2019s memorial and incense offerings",
          d: "Dodda Ganesha, Bull Temple or Nandi Basava"
        },
        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Dodda Ganesha, Bull Temple or Nandi Basava.\n\nKey Points\nKadalekai Parishe is a traditional groundnut fair held annually in Bengaluru, Karnataka, with deep religious and cultural significance.\nThe fair is historically associated with the Bull Temple (Dodda Basavana Gudi), which is dedicated to Nandi Basava, the sacred bull of Lord Shiva.\nDevotees offer freshly harvested groundnuts (kadalekai) to Nandi as part of a traditional ritual seeking prosperity, good harvest, and agricultural well-being.\nThe nearby Dodda Ganesha Temple is also closely linked to the festivities, and offerings are made to Lord Ganesha before visiting the Bull Temple.\nHence, the association of Kadalekai Parishe is correctly linked with Dodda Ganesha, Bull Temple, and Nandi Basava, making option 4 the correct answer.\n\nAdditional Information\nLocation: The fair is held in the Basavanagudi area of Bengaluru, which is one of the city\u2019s oldest residential and cultural localities.\nHistorical Origin: Kadalekai Parishe dates back over five centuries, originating during the reign of the Vijayanagara Empire, when farmers offered their produce to temples after harvest.\nReligious Significance: Nandi Basava is regarded as the guardian deity of agriculture and fertility, making the offering of groundnuts symbolically important for farming communities.\nTiming: The fair is usually conducted during the Kartika month (October-November), coinciding with the groundnut harvest season in southern India.\nIncorrect Options Explained:\nISKCON Temple is associated with Vaishnavite worship and not with Kadalekai Parishe.\nVidhana Soudha is a legislative building and has no religious or agricultural fair association.\nTipu Sultan\u2019s memorial is unrelated to groundnut offerings or this traditional festival.",
