        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is - Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\n\nKey Points\nPolicy of Paramountcy\nThe policy of paramountcy was a doctrine introduced by the British East India Company.\nIt allowed the British to assert their authority over Indian princely states.\nUnder this policy, the British could interfere in the administration of these states.\nThis policy was a significant tool for the British to maintain control over India's fragmented political landscape.\nMilitary and Foreign Affairs\nParamountcy meant that the British had the final authority in all military and foreign affairs of the princely states.\nThis ensured that the princely states could not form alliances or engage in military actions without British approval.\nIt reinforced British dominance and prevented any potential unified resistance against British rule.\n\nAdditional Information\nDoctrine of Lapse\nA related policy was the Doctrine of Lapse, which allowed the British to annex princely states if the ruler died without a male heir.\nThis doctrine was also used to expand British territories in India.\nSubsidiary Alliance\nThe Subsidiary Alliance was another strategy used by the British to control Indian states.\nUnder this system, Indian rulers were required to keep British forces in their territory and pay for their maintenance.\nThis further solidified British control over the Indian subcontinent.\nBritish Residency System\nBritish Residents were officials appointed to Indian princely states to oversee their administration.\nThey acted as intermediaries between the British government and the princely states.\nThis system ensured that the princely states remained under close British supervision.",
