        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Only two.\n\nKey Points\nStatement 1: Oxbow lakes are formed due to deposition of sediments on the concave side of meanders. \u2718 Incorrect\nIn a meandering river, erosion occurs on the concave (outer) bank and deposition occurs on the convex (inner) bank. Oxbow lakes are formed when a meander loop gets cut off from the main river channel.\nStatement 2: Natural levees are raised embankments formed due to sediment deposition along riverbanks. \u2714 Correct\nDuring floods, rivers deposit heavier sediments near their banks, gradually building up natural embankments called natural levees.\nStatement 3: Floodplains are replenished with nutrients during floods, making them highly fertile. \u2714 Correct\nFloodwaters spread fine alluvial sediments rich in nutrients over adjacent lands, increasing soil fertility.\nHence, only two statements are correct.\n\nAdditional Information\nOxbow Lake:\nFormed when a meander loop is cut off from the main river channel.\nCommon in mature and old-stage rivers.\nNatural Levees:\nRaised ridges formed naturally by flood deposition along riverbanks.\nHelp confine river flow during normal conditions.\nFloodplains:\nFlat areas adjacent to rivers formed by repeated deposition of alluvium.\nAmong the most fertile agricultural regions in the world.",
