        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is 60 days.\n\nKey Points\nUnder the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Samhita (BNSS), the provision for default bail is applicable in cases where the investigation is not completed within the prescribed timeline.\nFor minor offences, the timeline for completing investigations is 60 days. If the investigation is not completed within this period, the accused becomes eligible for default bail.\nThis provision ensures that individuals accused of minor offences are not subjected to prolonged incarceration without trial. Hence, the correct timeline is 60 days.\nThe BNSS aims to streamline criminal justice and protect the rights of individuals by limiting the duration of pretrial detention for minor offences. This provision aligns with the principles of fairness and justice in the legal system.\nIt is important to note that the timeline varies for serious offences, which typically have a longer investigation period before the provision for default bail can be invoked. Hence, the correct answer is 60 days.\n\nAdditional Information\nBharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Samhita (BNSS):\nThe BNSS is proposed as a replacement for the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC) in India, aiming to modernize the criminal justice system and make processes more efficient.\nIt introduces several reforms, including the timeline for investigations to ensure speedy justice and reduce the burden on the judicial system.\nDefault Bail:\nDefault bail is granted when the prosecution fails to complete the investigation within the statutory period prescribed under the law. It is a safeguard against unjustified prolonged detention.\nFor minor offences, the period is 60 days, while for serious offences, it may extend to 90 days or more, depending on the nature of the crime.\nExample: If the police fail to file a charge sheet within 60 days for a minor offence, the accused can apply for default bail under the BNSS provisions.\nKey Features of BNSS :\nThe BNSS emphasizes on reducing delays in the investigation process and protecting the rights of accused individuals.\nIt promotes transparency and accountability in the criminal justice system, ensuring better compliance with legal timelines.\nThe BNSS also provides for the digitization of records and processes to expedite judicial proceedings.\n\nImportant Points\n\nKey Legal Provisions:\nThe provision for default bail under the BNSS is rooted in the principle of safeguarding individual liberty. It ensures that no one is detained indefinitely without proper legal justification.\nThe timeline for investigations under the BNSS is strictly enforced to encourage efficiency and accountability in the police force.\nComparison with Existing Laws:\nUnder the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC), the timeline for default bail is similar, with 60 days for minor offences and 90 days for serious offences.\nThe BNSS aims to enhance these provisions by introducing reforms like digitization and stricter enforcement mechanisms.\nThese reforms are expected to reduce case backlogs and improve the overall efficiency of the criminal justice system.",
