        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Criminal Substantive Law.\n\nKey Points\nThe Bharatiya Nyaya Samhita (BNS), 2023, is aimed at consolidating and amending laws related to Criminal Substantive Law in India.\nBNS is designed as a replacement for the Indian Penal Code (IPC), 1860, which was drafted during British colonial rule in India.\nThe primary objective of the BNS is to modernize and reform criminal laws to align with the changing socio-economic and technological landscape of the country.\nIt seeks to simplify criminal laws, ensure speedy justice, and address contemporary challenges such as cybercrime and organized crime.\nThe Bharatiya Nyaya Samhita Bill, 2023, was introduced in Parliament in August 2023 as part of a broader initiative to overhaul colonial-era legal frameworks, including the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC) and the Indian Evidence Act.\n\nAdditional Information\nCivil Procedure Law\nCivil Procedure Law governs the procedures and practices in civil courts, such as filing lawsuits, conducting trials, and enforcing judgments.\nThe primary legislation governing this branch in India is the Civil Procedure Code (CPC), 1908.\nCivil laws deal with private disputes between individuals or organizations, such as property disputes, contracts, or family matters.\nEvidence Law\nThe law of evidence deals with the rules and principles that determine what evidence is admissible in courts.\nThe Indian Evidence Act, 1872, is the governing framework for evidence-related matters in Indian courts.\nIt specifies the type and manner of evidence that can be presented in civil or criminal cases, including oral, documentary, and circumstantial evidence.\nContract Law\nContract law governs agreements between parties that are enforceable by law.\nThe primary legislation in India is the Indian Contract Act, 1872, which outlines the essentials of a valid contract, including offer, acceptance, consideration, and capacity to contract.\nIt also covers specific contracts such as indemnity, guarantee, bailment, agency, and partnership agreements.",
