        answer: "b",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Option 2 i.e. 2 and 3\n\nKey Points\nFact: Criminal law (like offenses, punishments, procedure) is mainly a Concurrent subject under the Concurrent List (List III).\nWhy not Union List? The Union List is for subjects where only the Central Government can make laws (like defense, foreign affairs, currency).\nSo this\nstatement is wrong.\nAgriculture is a State subject according to the State List in the Seventh Schedule of the Constitution. It covers topics like agriculture, land, irrigation, and other related matters that fall under the jurisdiction of state governments.\nIntra-state trade is listed under the State List. This means trade and commerce within a state are regulated by the respective state government, as specified in the Constitution. Note: This is distinct from Inter-state trade (trade between states), which falls under the Union List.\nThe Seventh Schedule of the Indian Constitution divides subjects into three lists: Union List, State List, and Concurrent List, which define the distribution of powers between the Union and State governments.\nStatements 2 and 3 are correct as they align with the constitutional provisions under Article 246 and the Seventh Schedule.\n\nAdditional Information\nUnion List:\nIt contains subjects of national importance such as defense, foreign affairs, banking, currency, atomic energy, and railways. It also includes Inter-state trade and commerce.\nOnly the central government can legislate on matters listed in the Union List.\nState List:\nIt includes subjects of state and local importance such as police, public health, sanitation, agriculture, irrigation, and intra-state trade.\nState governments have exclusive authority to legislate on subjects in this list.\nConcurrent List:\nThis list includes subjects on which both the Union and State governments can legislate, such as education, marriage, contracts, and bankruptcy.\nIn case of conflict between state and union laws, the Union law prevails.\nSeventh Schedule:\nEstablished by the Constitution of India, it defines the allocation of powers and responsibilities between the Union and State governments.\nIt ensures federal governance by categorizing subjects into three lists: Union, State, and Concurrent.",
