        solution: "Solution\nThe word Cyclone is derived from the Greek word Cyclos meaning the coils of a snake.\nIt was coined by Henry Paddington because the tropical storms in the Bay of Bengal and the Arabian Sea appear like coiled serpents of the sea\nCyclones are rapid inward air circulation around a low-pressure area.\nIt is usually accompanied by violent storms and bad weather.\nTropical cyclones are capable of producing highly strong winds and torrential rain, high waves and a destructive storm surge.\nA tropical cyclone is a series of storms characterized by a low-pressure core and multiple thunderstorms causing powerful winds and heavy rain.\nHowever, heavy rains can cause major inland flooding.\n\nFactors such as wind speed, wind direction, humidity and temperature contribute to the development of a cyclone. The most influential factors are:\n\nHumidity and Temperature:\n\nA source of warm, moist air derived from tropical oceans with sea surface temperatures normally in the region of, or in excess, of 27 \xB0C.\n\nWinds:\n\nWinds near the ocean surface blow from different directions converging and causing air to rise and storm clouds to form.\nWinds that do not vary greatly with height - known as low wind shear.\nThis allows the storm clouds to rise vertically to high levels.\n\nThus, rainfall is NOT responsible for the development of a cyclone.",
