        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Option I: Only\nStatement 1 is correct.\nStatement 1 is correct as the Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra were recently inscribed into UNESCO's Memory of the World Register.\nStatement 2 is incorrect because India has 14 entries in the Memory of the World Register, not 18.\n\nIn News\nThe Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra were added to UNESCO\u2019s Memory of the World Register, contributing to India\u2019s cultural heritage.\n\nKey Points\nUNESCO Memory of the World Register: A prestigious recognition for global cultural heritage, preserving significant documentary heritage.\nIndia\u2019s Contribution: India now has 14 entries, including the Bhagavad Gita and Natyashastra, as well as other cultural texts and manuscripts.\nPurpose: The register ensures the preservation and accessibility of key historical and cultural materials globally.\nBhagavad Gita:\nCore spiritual and philosophical text of Hinduism.\nPart of the Mahabharata.\nTranslated into nearly 80 languages.\nRevered for its teachings on duty, righteousness, and spirituality.\nNatyashastra:\nAncient treatise by Bharat Muni, foundational to Indian performing arts.\nCovers dramaturgy, acting, stage design, music, dance, and the theory of aesthetics (rasa).\nShaped classical Indian dance and drama.\nOther Notable Indian Entries on the UNESCO Memory of the World Register:\n30 Manuscripts of the Rig Veda (listed in 2007)\nSaiva Manuscripts from Pondicherry (listed in 2005)\nThe Gilgit Manuscript (listed in 2017)\nMaitreyayvarakarana (listed in 2023)\nRamcharitmanas and Panchatantra texts\nManuscripts of philosopher Abhinavagupta\nSahrdaya\u0304loka-Locana texts\n\nAdditional Information\nMemory of the World Register: Established in 1992, it aims to safeguard documentary heritage of global significance.\nUNESCO's Ongoing Efforts: Regular updates to the register help preserve evolving cultural heritage, with new inscriptions being added regularly.\nUNESCO's Inscription Criteria: Documentary heritage is inscribed based on its world significance, universal value, and cultural importance\nMemory of the World Register: Represents a critical resource for preserving humanity's shared cultural heritage, including manuscripts, oral traditions, and audio-visual materials.",
