        answer: "a",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Option 1.\n\nKey Points\nNational Emergency:\nUnder article 352, the President can declare a national emergency when the security of India is threatened by war or external aggression or armed rebellion.\nThere is no maximum period prescribed for its operation.\nUnder this, the relationship of the centre with all the states undergoes a modification.\nIt affects fundamental rights of citizens. Hence,\nstatement 1 is not correct.\nLok sabha can pass a resolution for its revocation.\nPresident's Rule:\nIt is mentioned under Article 356 of the Constitution.\nThe maximum period prescribed for its operation is three years.\nUnder this, the relationship of only the state under emergency with the centre undergoes a modification.\nIt does not affect the fundamental rights of citizens.\nIt can be revoked by the President only on his own.\u200B\u200B\n\nAdditional Information\nThe Emergency provisions are contained in Part XVIII of the constitution, from articles 352-360.\nThe Constitution stipulates three types of emergencies:\nNational emergency (Article 352): An emergency due to war, external aggression or armed rebellion.\nConstitutional emergency (Article 356): It is due to the failure of the constitutional machinery in the states. It is also known as President's rule.\nFinancial emergency (Article 360): It is due to the threat to the financial stability or credit of India\nFinancial Emergency:\nIt is mentioned under Article 360 of the Constitution.\nArticle 360 empowers the President to proclaim a Financial emergency if he is satisfied that a situation has arisen due to which the financial stability or credit of India or any part of its territory is threatened.\nThe proclamation must be approved by both the house of Parliament within two months from the date of issue.\nThe Financial emergency continues indefinitely till it is revoked.\nThere is no effect on the fundamental rights of the citizens.",
