        solution: "When an object is at a height, it has maximum potential energy, not kinetic energy.\n\nAs it falls, potential energy (PE) decreases, and kinetic energy (KE) increases.\n\nEnergy transforms from potential \u2192 kinetic, not the other way around.\n\nThe energy distribution in a pendulum is the opposite of what is stated:\n\nAt the highest points \u2192 speed is zero \u2192 100% potential energy, 0% kinetic energy.\n\nAt the lowest point (center) \u2192 speed is maximum \u2192 maximum kinetic energy, minimum potential energy.\n\nFurther Insights\nEnergy cannot be created or destroyed; it only changes form (Law of Conservation of Energy).\n\nA freely falling body converts gravitational potential energy entirely into kinetic energy (neglecting air resistance).\n\nIn real pendulums, friction and air resistance slowly reduce total mechanical energy.\n\nAt the midpoint of a pendulum, velocity is maximum; acceleration is zero.\n\nPotential energy depends on height; kinetic energy depends on speed.",
