        solution: 'In Hindustani classical music, "Andolan" refers to the slow and gentle oscillation of a note.\n\nExplanation of Options:\n\n(a) is Incorrect. Fast oscillations are typically referred to as Taan, Murki, or certain types of Gamak.\n\n(b) is Correct. Andolan is a specific type of ornamentation (Gamak) where a note (swara) is gently swung back and forth within a very small range of its pitch. The singer or instrumentalist touches the microtones (shrutis) adjacent to the main note, but never moves far enough to hit a different note.\n\n(c) is Incorrect. A sudden jump between octaves or notes is generally called an Interval or a Meend (if glided).\n\n(d) is Incorrect. Rhythmic variations of tempo are related to Layakari.\n\nFurther Insights:\nRaga Application: Certain ragas are defined by their specific use of Andolan. For example, in Raga Darbari Kanhada, the note Ga (Gandhar) must be performed with a heavy, slow Andolan to achieve its characteristic somber mood.\nEmotional Depth: Andolan is used to provide depth, gravity, and emotional resonance (Bhava) to a performance.',
