        answer: "b",
        solution: "Statement II is correct.\nStatement I is incorrect because the Government of India Act, 1919, introduced 'Diarchy' at the Provincial level, while the Central government remained unitary.\n\nExplanation of Statements:\n\nI. is Incorrect. The Government of India Act, 1919 (also known as the Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms), introduced Diarchy (dual government) specifically in the Provinces. Under this system, provincial subjects were divided into two categories: 'Reserved' (administered by the Governor) and 'Transferred' (administered by the Governor with the aid of Ministers). Diarchy was only introduced at the Central level by the subsequent Government of India Act of 1935.\n\nII. is Correct. The Act of 1919 provided for the establishment of a Public Service Commission for recruiting civil servants in India. This was a response to the growing demand for the Indianisation of the civil services. Consequently, the Central Public Service Commission was set up in 1926 following the recommendations of the Lee Commission (1924).\n\nFurther Insights:\nBicameralism: The 1919 Act introduced for the first time a bicameral legislature at the Center, consisting of an Upper House (Council of State) and a Lower House (Legislative Assembly).\nDirect Elections: It introduced direct elections in the country for the first time, though the franchise was limited based on property, tax, or education.\nCommunal Representation: The Act extended the principle of communal representation by providing separate electorates for Sikhs, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians, and Europeans (expanding upon the 1909 Act which provided it for Muslims).\nHigh Commissioner: It created a new office of the High Commissioner for India in London and transferred to him some of the functions hitherto performed by the Secretary of State for India.",
