        answer: "c",
        solution: 'A stalagmite is the feature formed on the floor of a cave by mineral-rich water dripping from the ceiling.\n\nExplanation of Options:\n\n(a) is Incorrect. Stalactites are the icicle-like formations that hang "tite" from the ceiling of a cave. They are formed as water evaporates before it falls, leaving calcium carbonate behind.\n\n(b) is Incorrect. A Sinkhole is a surface feature. It is a depression or hole in the ground caused by the collapse of the surface layer, often due to the dissolution of limestone bedrock underneath.\n\n(c) is Correct. As water drops fall from the ceiling to the floor, they deposit small amounts of calcium carbonate. Over thousands of years, these deposits build upward to form a mound or pillar known as a Stalagmite.\n\n(d) is Incorrect. Lapies are a surface feature of Karst topography, characterized by grooved, fluted, or jagged ridges formed by the chemical weathering of limestone by rainwater.\n\nFurther Insights:\nWhen a stalactite (growing down) and a stalagmite (growing up) eventually meet, they form a continuous structure known as a Pillar or Column.\n\nThis type of landscape is known as Karst Topography, named after the Karst region in Slovenia where these limestone features are prominent.',
