        answer: "d",
        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Option D.\n\nKey Points\nThe statement that Directive Principles of State Policy (DPSPs) are subordinate to Fundamental Rights in all situations is not correct. While DPSPs are generally non-justiciable, the Constitution has evolved through amendments and judicial interpretation to alter this relationship. Hence, Statement I is incorrect.\nCertain DPSPs have been accorded primacy over Fundamental Rights through constitutional amendments, most notably Article 31C, which gives precedence to laws enacted to implement Article 39(b) and 39(c) over Articles 14 and 19. Hence, Statement II is correct.\nThus, Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct \u2192 Option D.\n\nAdditional Information\nArticle 31C was introduced by the 25th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1971 and later expanded by the 42nd Amendment, though the expansion was curtailed by the Supreme Court.\nIn Kesavananda Bharati case (1973), the Supreme Court upheld Article 31C only to the extent of Articles 39(b) and (c).\nThe Court has consistently held that Fundamental Rights and DPSPs are complementary, not antagonistic (e.g., Minerva Mills case, 1980).\nThe current constitutional position seeks a harmonious balance between individual liberties and socio-economic justice.",
