        solution: "Solution\n\nThe correct answer is Option 2.\n\nKey Points\nStatement A: Black soil covers most of the Deccan Plateau, specifically in states like Maharashtra, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Karnataka, and Andhra Pradesh. It is highly fertile and suitable for the cultivation of crops like cotton. This statement is correct.\nStatement B: Black soil is indeed rich in lime, iron, magnesium, and alumina. However, it does not lack magnesium; rather, it lacks phosphorus, nitrogen, and organic matter. This makes the statement factually incorrect.\nSince Statement B is incorrect, the option stating that Statement A is accurate.\nBlack soil is also known as Regur soil or cotton soil, and it has a fine texture and retains moisture well, which is ideal for dry farming.\nHence, Option 2 is the correct answer, because statement (b) is incorrect.\n\nAdditional Information\nBlack Soil Characteristics:\nBlack soil is formed from lava basalt rocks, making it one of the most fertile soils in India.\nIt is highly suitable for crops like cotton, sugarcane, tobacco, oilseeds, and pulses.\nIts fine-grained structure allows it to retain moisture, but it becomes extremely hard when dry.\nIt is rich in lime, iron, magnesium, and alumina, but lacks nitrogen, phosphorus, and organic matter.\nDistribution of Black Soil in India:\nBlack soil covers around 15% of India\u2019s total land area.\nIt is predominantly found in the Deccan Plateau regions such as Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, Karnataka, and Andhra Pradesh.\nUses of Black Soil:\nBlack soil is primarily used for cultivating cotton, which is why it is also known as cotton soil.\nDue to its moisture-retaining ability, it is ideal for crops requiring less irrigation.\nImportant Points:\nBlack soil is classified as one of the seven soil types in India, along with Alluvial, Red, Laterite, Desert, Mountain, and Peaty soils.\nThe soil is highly fertile but requires supplementation of nitrogen and phosphorus fertilizers to support crop growth efficiently.\nIt is an essential resource for agricultural productivity in India.",
